            {
              "id": "7aa34c56-a7d6-38c5-9de5-697dde8c9f2e",
              "name": "XSS Vulnerability in User-Controlled HTML Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is logging an error message that includes user-controlled data. If the `utils.getErrorMessage(err)` function does not properly sanitize the `err` input, this could lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Use frameworks that automatically escape XSS by design, such as Ruby on Rails, React JS, etc.\n- Escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) will resolve Reflected and Stored XSS vulnerabilities.\n- Apply context-sensitive encoding when modifying the browser document on the client side acts against DOM XSS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code should be updated to ensure that the error message is properly escaped. This can be done using a library like lodash's `escape` function:\n\n```javascript\nconst _ = require('lodash');\n\nlogger.error(`Could not create card: ${_.escape(utils.getErrorMessage(err))}`);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- A logging library that provides the `error` function (e.g., `winston` or `log4js`).\n- The `lodash` library for the `escape` function.\n- A utility library or module that provides the `getErrorMessage` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cdf18332-6293-37d6-99e1-95d25397c2aa",
              "name": "XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is logging an error message that includes user-controlled data (`userId` and `utils.getErrorMessage(err)`). If this logged information is later displayed in a web page without proper escaping, it could lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Use frameworks that automatically escape XSS by design, such as Ruby on Rails, React JS, etc.\n- Escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL).\n- Apply context-sensitive encoding when modifying the browser document on the client side.\n- Validate, filter, or sanitize user-generated inputs.\n- Use Content Security Policy (CSP) as a defense-in-depth mitigating control against XSS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, if the logged information is not displayed in a web page, there is no direct XSS vulnerability. However, if it is displayed, you should ensure that `userId` and `utils.getErrorMessage(err)` are properly escaped. Here is an example of how you might do this using the `escape-html` library:\n\n```javascript\nconst escapeHtml = require('escape-html');\n\nlogger.error(`Could not perform soft delete for the user ${escapeHtml(userId)}: ${escapeHtml(utils.getErrorMessage(err))}`);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not specify any library dependencies. However, the recommended fix requires the `escape-html` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP XSS](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
