            {
              "id": "c388c179-b1fc-3c69-95eb-bc1187f84d02",
              "name": "Enabled Directory Listing/Indexing Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Enabled Directory Listing/Indexing Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that allows unauthorized users to list all files in a directory due to the server being improperly configured. This can lead to information disclosure as attackers can discover files not intended for their view. In the provided code snippet, the vulnerability lies in the `serveIndex` middleware which is used to serve an index of the 'ftp' directory.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should disable directory listing in your application. If directory listing is necessary, ensure that it is properly protected with authentication and authorization controls. Also, sensitive files should not be stored in directories that allow listing.\n\n## Source Code Fix\n\nTo fix the vulnerability in the provided code snippet, you can remove the `serveIndex` middleware:\n\n```javascript\napp.use('/ftp', serveIndexMiddleware)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js\n- serve-index\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-548: Information Exposure Through Directory Listing](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/548.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-548",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/548.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-548"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "bfbed3ec-722c-3da9-adf5-4154b2d4ce21",
              "name": "Enabled Directory Listing/Indexing Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Enabled Directory Listing/Indexing Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that allows unauthorized users to view the contents of directories that are not explicitly forbidden. This can lead to information disclosure as the attacker can view sensitive files or data. In the provided code, the `serveIndex` middleware is used to serve directory listings, which can lead to this vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should disable directory listing unless it is absolutely necessary. If directory listing is required, ensure that it is only enabled for directories that do not contain sensitive information. Also, ensure that access to these directories is properly authenticated and authorized.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix the vulnerability in the provided code, you can remove the `serveIndex` middleware for the `/.well-known` route:\n\n```javascript\napp.use('/.well-known', serveIndexMiddleware)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- serve-index: Middleware for serving directory listings.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-548: Information Exposure Through Directory Listing](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/548.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-548",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/548.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-548"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ca565583-1fb2-3cf3-ae61-5bb7e73e7ac1",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability can occur when user-controlled data is used in an HTML string without proper sanitization or encoding. In the provided JavaScript code, the `basket.UserId` and `utils.getErrorMessage(err)` are user-controlled data that are directly inserted into an HTML string. If an attacker can control these values, they can inject malicious scripts that can be executed in the browser of any user viewing the generated HTML.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always encode user-controlled data before inserting it into an HTML string. This can be done using built-in JavaScript functions or third-party libraries that are designed for this purpose. Additionally, you should use context-specific encoding when inserting data into different parts of an HTML document.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nconst sanitizeHtml = require('sanitize-html');\n\nlet userId = sanitizeHtml(basket.UserId);\nlet errorMessage = sanitizeHtml(utils.getErrorMessage(err));\n\nlogger.error(`Could not insert Basket for UserId ${userId}: ${errorMessage}`);\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we use the `sanitize-html` library to sanitize the user-controlled data before inserting it into the HTML string. This will remove any potentially harmful scripts from the data.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- sanitize-html\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6c21e221-4e0a-3903-a59d-e06db991296b",
              "name": "Enabled Directory Listing/Indexing Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Enabled Directory Listing/Indexing Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that allows unauthorized users to view a list of all files in a directory due to the server being configured to provide a file index. This can lead to information disclosure as sensitive files or data might be exposed to attackers.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `serveIndex` middleware is used to serve directory listings. The directory 'logs' is exposed with detailed view and icons, which could potentially disclose sensitive information in the log files.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should disable directory listing for your application. If directory listing is necessary for some reason, ensure that it is only enabled for directories that do not contain sensitive information.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou can disable directory listing by not using the `serveIndex` middleware for sensitive directories. If you need to serve static files from a directory, use the `express.static` middleware instead. Here is how you can modify the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nvar express = require('express');\nvar app = express();\n\napp.use('/support/logs', express.static('logs'));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- express\n- serve-index\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Insecure Direct Object References](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A5_2017-Broken_Access_Control)\n- [CWE-548: Information Exposure Through Directory Listing](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/548.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-548",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/548.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-548"
                ]
              }
