            {
              "id": "bc05e1dc-6272-31df-a704-7bfe2274d886",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected, but this rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior and potential security vulnerabilities. In Node.js, unhandled Promise rejections will soon be deprecated, meaning that Node.js will terminate the process whenever it encounters an unhandled Promise rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always handle Promise rejections. This can be done by using a `.catch()` block after your `.then()` block, or by using a try/catch block with async/await. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ntry {\n    const data = await readFile(filePath, 'utf8');\n    return safeLoad(data);\n} catch (error) {\n    console.error('Error:', error);\n    // Handle error appropriately\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, if `readFile` or `safeLoad` throws an error, it will be caught and handled in the catch block.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `fs` or `fs/promises` from Node.js for the `readFile` function.\n- `js-yaml` for the `safeLoad` function.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP Node.js Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a62a6e21-3e25-39d3-bd50-5cb04da5759a",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This vulnerability can lead to exposure of sensitive data to unauthorized parties, as the data can be easily intercepted and read by anyone who has access to the network.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the TestBed is configuring an HTTP client for an AddressService. If this HTTP client is used to communicate with an unencrypted HTTP server, it could expose sensitive data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for unauthorized parties to intercept and read the data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code fix would depend on the implementation of the `provideHttpClient` and `provideHttpClientTesting` functions. However, generally, you should ensure that these functions are set up to use HTTPS. Here's an example of how you might do this using Angular's `HttpClientModule`:\n\n```javascript\nimport { HttpClientModule } from '@angular/common/http';\n\nTestBed.configureTestingModule({\n  imports: [HttpClientModule],\n  providers: [AddressService]\n});\n```\n\nIn this example, the `HttpClientModule` is imported and added to the `imports` array in the `TestBed.configureTestingModule` method. This module uses HTTPS by default.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet appears to be using Angular's testing utilities, so the following dependencies are likely required:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
