            {
              "id": "94a67e0c-2c50-3e5c-8d25-df3121a05705",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of unencrypted HTTP servers instead of HTTPS in JavaScript programming. This makes the application susceptible to Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks. In a MitM attack, the attacker intercepts the communication between two parties, alters the communication, and gains unauthorized access to sensitive information.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the TestBed is being configured with certain providers, but it doesn't specify whether the communication with these providers is happening over HTTP or HTTPS. If it's over HTTP, it's vulnerable to MitM attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS instead of HTTP for any kind of communication that involves sensitive information. HTTPS ensures that the communication between the client and the server is encrypted and secure.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code fix would depend on the actual implementation of the `provideHttpClient` and `provideHttpClientTesting` functions. However, in general, you should ensure that these functions are set up to use HTTPS. Here's a general example of how you might set up an HTTPS server in Node.js:\n\n```javascript\nconst https = require('https');\nconst fs = require('fs');\n\nconst options = {\n  key: fs.readFileSync('test/fixtures/keys/agent2-key.pem'),\n  cert: fs.readFileSync('test/fixtures/keys/agent2-cert.pem')\n};\n\nhttps.createServer(options, (req, res) => {\n  res.writeHead(200);\n  res.end('hello world\\n');\n}).listen(8000);\n```\n\nIn this example, `https.createServer` is used instead of `http.createServer`, and the `options` object includes the necessary SSL certificate and key.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using Angular's TestBed, so the following dependencies are required:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic/testing`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "dbccc529-965c-34d5-9a48-92df6a30324d",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over an unencrypted HTTP connection. This can lead to the interception of the data by malicious actors, who can then use it for nefarious purposes. This vulnerability is particularly dangerous when dealing with sensitive data such as login credentials, personal information, or financial data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) for all connections, especially when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data sent between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for attackers to intercept and read the data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe TestBed configuration does not directly deal with HTTP or HTTPS connections, it is used for setting up a testing environment. The actual HTTP requests are likely made in the `AdministrationService` or the interceptors provided by `withInterceptorsFromDi()`. You should ensure that these services always use HTTPS for their requests.\n\nHere is an example of how to enforce HTTPS in Angular's HttpClient:\n\n```javascript\nimport { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';\n\nconstructor(private http: HttpClient) { }\n\ngetSecureData() {\n  return this.http.get('https://secure.example.com/data');\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using Angular's testing utilities, so the following dependencies are likely required:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `@angular/common/http/testing`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "97d566ee-98ce-31e5-bd46-62d79472a0be",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. The vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String specifically refers to the scenario where user-controlled data is used to construct an HTML string which is then dynamically inserted into the webpage. If the user-controlled data is not properly sanitized, an attacker can inject malicious scripts that will be executed in the context of the victim's browser.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the potential XSS vulnerability arises from the use of user-controlled data (`id`) in the construction of a URL string for an HTTP GET request. If the `id` is not properly sanitized, an attacker could potentially manipulate the URL to execute malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. **Validate Input:** Ensure that the user-controlled data is valid. For example, if `id` is expected to be a number, ensure that it is indeed a number.\n\n2. **Encode Output:** Ensure that user-controlled data is properly encoded before it is used in HTML strings. This can prevent potentially harmful characters from being interpreted as code.\n\n3. **Use Appropriate Response Headers:** To prevent XSS attacks, use the `Content-Security-Policy` HTTP response header to restrict the sources from which scripts can be loaded.\n\n4. **Use Libraries That Automatically Escape XSS:** Use libraries or templates that automatically escape XSS by design, such as ReactJS or Angular.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming `id` should be a number, a simple fix could be to parse the `id` to an integer before using it:\n\n```javascript\nid = parseInt(id);\nreturn this.http.get(`${this.hostServer}/rest/basket/${id}`).pipe(map((response: any) => response.data), catchError((error) => { throw error }))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using the `rxjs` library for handling asynchronous data streams. Therefore, the `rxjs` library is required for the code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5c6cae9f-d245-36de-8b6e-17dde815e196",
              "name": "Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. The \"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\" vulnerability in JavaScript refers to the situation where user-controlled data is directly used in an HTML string. This can allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts and execute them in the user's browser.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `id` parameter is directly concatenated into the URL string without any sanitization or encoding. If the `id` parameter is controlled by the user and not properly sanitized, it can lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize user inputs: Ensure that user-controlled data is properly validated and sanitized before using it in your application. This can help prevent malicious scripts from being injected into your application.\n\n2. Use context-specific output encoding: When dynamic data is inserted into HTML, JavaScript, CSS, XML, or any other context, it should be output encoded using context-specific rules.\n\n3. Use Content Security Policy (CSP): CSP is a security layer that helps detect and mitigate certain types of attacks, including XSS and data injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA simple fix for the provided code snippet would be to sanitize the `id` parameter before using it in the URL string. Here's an example using the `encodeURIComponent` function:\n\n```javascript\nreturn this.http.get(`${this.host}/${encodeURIComponent(id)}`).pipe(map((response: any) => response.data), catchError((error) => { throw error }))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using the `rxjs` library for handling asynchronous data streams. It also appears to be using Angular's `HttpClient` for making HTTP requests.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
