            {
              "id": "1944a211-bcd1-3f17-8dd3-d6ffc0b9f2a9",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. The \"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\" vulnerability in JavaScript refers to a situation where user-controlled data is directly used in an HTML string. This can allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts and execute them in the user's browser.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `id` and `params` variables are directly included in the HTTP request without any sanitization or encoding. If these variables contain user-controlled data, this could lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Validate and sanitize user input: Ensure that user input matches expected patterns and remove or escape any potentially harmful characters.\n\n2. Use context-specific output encoding: When inserting user-controlled data into HTML, use context-specific encoding to prevent the browser from interpreting it as code.\n\n3. Use Content Security Policy (CSP): CSP can help to mitigate the impact of any XSS vulnerabilities by restricting the sources from which scripts can be loaded.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code should be updated to sanitize the `id` and `params` variables before they are used in the HTTP request. This could be done using a library like DOMPurify:\n\n```javascript\nimport DOMPurify from 'dompurify';\n\nlet sanitizedId = DOMPurify.sanitize(id);\nlet sanitizedParams = DOMPurify.sanitize(params);\n\nreturn this.http.put(`${this.host}/${sanitizedId}`, sanitizedParams).pipe(map((response: any) => response.data), catchError((error) => { throw error }))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- An HTTP client library (such as Axios or the Fetch API)\n- An Observable library (such as RxJS)\n- A sanitization library (such as DOMPurify)\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
