            {
              "id": "3115d6d7-4041-36a5-ba3b-12b2ae86cb63",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. The potential XSS vulnerability from user-controlled data in HTML string refers to the situation where an attacker can inject malicious scripts into the HTML string that is being dynamically generated using user-controlled data. This can lead to various security issues like session hijacking, identity theft, defacement of websites, and other malicious activities.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the `id` parameter in the HTTP DELETE request URL is a potential source of this vulnerability. If the `id` is user-controlled and not properly sanitized, it can lead to an XSS attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate, sanitize, and escape user-controlled data before using it to generate dynamic HTML content. \n\n- **Validate**: Check if the user-controlled data meets certain criteria (e.g., type, length, format, range) before processing it.\n- **Sanitize**: Remove or replace any characters in the user-controlled data that have special meanings in HTML.\n- **Escape**: Convert special characters in the user-controlled data to their corresponding HTML entities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou can use the `encodeURIComponent` function in JavaScript to encode the `id` parameter before using it in the HTTP DELETE request URL:\n\n```javascript\nreturn this.http.delete(`${this.host}/${encodeURIComponent(id)}`).pipe(map((response: any) => response.data), catchError((error) => { throw error }))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example seems to be using the following libraries:\n\n- `rxjs`: For the `pipe`, `map`, and `catchError` functions.\n- An HTTP client library (e.g., `axios`, `fetch`, `http` in Node.js, or `HttpClient` in Angular) for the `http.delete` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/)\n- [OWASP: XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
