            {
              "id": "41cbf0f3-97eb-3c1f-abb7-6103f9139442",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability from user-controlled data in HTML string exists when an application includes untrusted data in a new HTML document without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing HTML document with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the user-controlled data `couponData` and `orderDetails` are directly included in the HTTP POST request without any sanitization or encoding. If these data contain malicious scripts, it could lead to XSS attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n- Always validate user input: User input should always be validated before it is processed to ensure it conforms to the expected format.\n- Escape user input: User input should be properly escaped before it is displayed in a web page.\n- Use secure APIs: Use APIs, such as element.textContent, which automatically escape HTML tag characters.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou should sanitize `couponData` and `orderDetails` before including them in the HTTP POST request. Here is an example using the `DOMPurify.sanitize()` method from the DOMPurify library:\n\n```javascript\nimport DOMPurify from 'dompurify';\n\nlet sanitizedCouponData = DOMPurify.sanitize(couponData);\nlet sanitizedOrderDetails = DOMPurify.sanitize(orderDetails);\n\nreturn this.http.post(`${this.hostServer}/rest/basket/${id}/checkout`, { sanitizedCouponData, sanitizedOrderDetails }).pipe(map((response: any) => response.orderConfirmation), catchError((error) => { throw error }))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Angular's HttpClient module for making HTTP requests.\n- RxJS for handling asynchronous operations and event-based programs.\n- DOMPurify for sanitizing HTML and preventing XSS attacks.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0588ca14-d9b9-3b53-8507-19d8bbe6b3aa",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the potential XSS vulnerability arises from the user-controlled data (`id` and `coupon`) being directly embedded into the HTML string without any sanitization or encoding. This could allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts by manipulating the `id` or `coupon` values.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. **Escape User Input**: Always escape user-controlled data before including it in your HTML. This will prevent any potentially harmful code from being executed.\n\n2. **Use Safe APIs**: Use safe APIs that automatically escape user-controlled data, such as `innerText` or `textContent`.\n\n3. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Implement a Content Security Policy (CSP) to mitigate the impact of any potential XSS vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code fix involves sanitizing the user-controlled data before using it in the HTML string. You can use a library like DOMPurify to sanitize the data.\n\n```javascript\nimport DOMPurify from 'dompurify';\n\nlet sanitizedId = DOMPurify.sanitize(id);\nlet sanitizedCoupon = DOMPurify.sanitize(coupon);\n\nreturn this.http.put(`${this.hostServer}/rest/basket/${sanitizedId}/coupon/${sanitizedCoupon}`, {}).pipe(map((response: any) => response.discount), catchError((error) => { throw error }))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `rxjs` for the `pipe`, `map`, and `catchError` functions.\n- `http` for making HTTP requests.\n- `DOMPurify` for sanitizing user-controlled data.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "231af04a-04c2-3001-92bb-2d5d03810ae8",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of unencrypted HTTP servers instead of HTTPS in JavaScript programming. This makes the application susceptible to Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks. In a MitM attack, the attacker intercepts the communication between two parties, alters the communication, and gains unauthorized access to sensitive information.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the TestBed is being configured with a CaptchaService and HttpClient. If the HttpClient is configured to communicate with an unencrypted HTTP server, it exposes the application to potential MitM attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS instead of HTTP for communication. HTTPS ensures that the communication between the client and the server is encrypted and secure. This makes it difficult for an attacker to intercept and alter the communication.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe specific fix would depend on how the HttpClient is being used in the application. However, a general recommendation would be to ensure that all requests made by the HttpClient are to HTTPS servers. This can be done by configuring the HttpClient to reject any non-HTTPS requests.\n\n```javascript\nimport { HttpClient, HttpClientModule } from '@angular/common/http';\n\nTestBed.configureTestingModule({\n  imports: [HttpClientModule],\n  providers: [CaptchaService, provideHttpClient(withInterceptorsFromDi()), provideHttpClientTesting()]\n})\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `@angular/core` for TestBed\n- `@angular/common/http` for HttpClient\n- `CaptchaService` (Assuming it's a custom service in your application)\n- `provideHttpClient` and `provideHttpClientTesting` (Assuming these are custom providers in your application)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8a45af55-fd16-3f6d-b8a3-b44661967515",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over an unencrypted HTTP connection. This can lead to the interception of the data by malicious actors, leading to data breaches and other security issues. This vulnerability is particularly relevant when dealing with APIs and other server-side interactions.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the TestBed is configuring a module with an HTTP client. If this client is used to send requests to an unencrypted HTTP server, it could expose the application to this vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for attackers to intercept and read the data.\n\nIn addition, consider using HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), which is a web security policy mechanism that helps to protect websites against protocol downgrade attacks and cookie hijacking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code fix would depend on the specific implementation of the `provideHttpClient` and `provideHttpClientTesting` functions. However, in general, you should ensure that any URLs used by these functions start with `https://` rather than `http://`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example appears to be using Angular's TestBed, so the following dependencies are likely required:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic/testing`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [OWASP Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
