            {
              "id": "c7802608-36cc-37fa-b713-f53e917bbf71",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This can lead to data being intercepted, read, and modified by attackers. This vulnerability is particularly dangerous when dealing with sensitive data such as login credentials, personal information, or financial data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for attackers to intercept and read the data.\n\nIn addition, consider using HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), which forces browsers to only use HTTPS, even if the user or a link specifies HTTP. This can help protect against downgrade attacks, where an attacker tries to force a connection to use HTTP instead of HTTPS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not directly show the use of HTTP or HTTPS, but it does show the configuration of a TestBed with a HttpClient. To ensure that this HttpClient uses HTTPS, you should configure your application to only use HTTPS URLs. This can be done in the environment configuration, or directly in the URLs used in your HTTP requests.\n\nFor example, if you have a service that makes HTTP requests:\n\n```javascript\nimport { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';\n\nexport class MyService {\n  constructor(private http: HttpClient) {}\n\n  getData() {\n    return this.http.get('http://example.com/data');\n  }\n}\n```\n\nYou should change the URL to use HTTPS:\n\n```javascript\nimport { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';\n\nexport class MyService {\n  constructor(private http: HttpClient) {}\n\n  getData() {\n    return this.http.get('https://example.com/data');\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/core` for `TestBed`\n- `@angular/common/http` for `HttpClient`\n- The `CodeFixesService` and `provideHttpClient`, `withInterceptorsFromDi`, and `provideHttpClientTesting` functions are not standard Angular functions and would need to be provided or imported from elsewhere in your application or from other libraries.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
