            {
              "id": "9903bedd-5488-30f7-88cf-7b531b881f69",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. The \"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\" vulnerability in JavaScript refers to the situation where user-controlled data is directly used in an HTML string. This can allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts and execute them in the user's browser.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `key` variable is directly appended to the `host` variable to form a URL for an HTTP GET request. If the `key` variable is user-controlled and not properly sanitized, it can lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always sanitize user-controlled data before using it in HTML strings or URLs. This can be done by using a library that provides functions for encoding or escaping special characters in strings.\n\nIn addition, you should use parameterized queries or prepared statements whenever possible to prevent SQL injection attacks. This is not directly related to XSS attacks, but it is a good practice for securing your code.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided code snippet using the `encodeURIComponent` function to sanitize the `key` variable:\n\n```javascript\nreturn this.http.get(this.host + `/${encodeURIComponent(key)}`).pipe(map((response: Fixes) => response), catchError((error: any) => { throw error }))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet appears to be using the RxJS library for handling asynchronous data streams. The `http` object might be an instance of the `HttpClient` class from the Angular framework.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "13574ad3-78fa-3d1a-a330-7adff605001c",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of unencrypted HTTP servers instead of HTTPS in JavaScript programming. This makes the application susceptible to Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks. In a MitM attack, the attacker intercepts the communication between two parties, alters the communication, and gains unauthorized access to sensitive information.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the TestBed is being configured with a set of providers, including `CodeSnippetService`, `provideHttpClient(withInterceptorsFromDi())`, and `provideHttpClientTesting()`. If these services are communicating over an unencrypted HTTP connection, they are vulnerable to MitM attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS instead of HTTP for all communications. HTTPS ensures that the communication between the client and the server is encrypted and secure. This makes it difficult for an attacker to intercept and alter the communication.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code fix would depend on the implementation of the `CodeSnippetService`, `provideHttpClient(withInterceptorsFromDi())`, and `provideHttpClientTesting()` services. Ensure that these services are using HTTPS for all communications.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet provided seems to be using Angular's TestBed for configuring a module. The dependencies required would be:\n\n- `@angular/core` for `TestBed`\n- `CodeSnippetService` (custom service)\n- `provideHttpClient(withInterceptorsFromDi())` (custom provider)\n- `provideHttpClientTesting()` (custom provider)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "20b71b13-f43b-3918-812a-25da13057a47",
              "name": "Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is vulnerable to XSS because it's using user-controlled data (`key`) in a HTTP GET request without proper sanitization or encoding. An attacker could potentially manipulate the `key` to inject malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. **Validate Input**: Validate user input by only accepting known good. Use a whitelist of allowed inputs.\n\n2. **Encode Output**: Encode user-controlled data before including it in HTML output.\n\n3. **Use Appropriate Response Headers**: To prevent XSS in HTTP responses that aren't intended to contain any HTML or JavaScript, you can use the `Content-Type` and `X-Content-Type-Options` headers to ensure that browsers interpret the responses the way you intend.\n\n4. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Implementing CSP makes it possible for server administrators to reduce or eliminate the vectors by which XSS can occur.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code does not show where the `key` variable is coming from, but assuming it's user-controlled data, you should sanitize it before using it in the HTTP GET request. Here's an example using the `encodeURI` function:\n\n```javascript\nlet sanitizedKey = encodeURI(key);\nreturn this.http.get<CodeSnippet>(`${this.host}/${sanitizedKey}`).pipe(map((response: CodeSnippet) => response), catchError((err) => { throw err }))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet seems to be using Angular's HttpClient module, and RxJS for handling asynchronous operations. The required dependencies are:\n\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `rxjs`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c49dbde5-1ac2-3847-b83d-bae27f866ba1",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This can lead to the interception of data by malicious actors, who can then use this data for nefarious purposes. This vulnerability is particularly dangerous when dealing with sensitive data such as login credentials, personal information, or financial data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) for all connections, especially when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data sent between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for attackers to intercept and understand the data.\n\nIn addition, it is also recommended to use HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), which forces browsers to only use HTTPS connections. This can help prevent downgrade attacks, where an attacker might try to force a connection to use HTTP instead of HTTPS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe specific fix would depend on the exact code and framework being used. However, in general, you should ensure that all URLs used for network connections start with \"https://\" instead of \"http://\". \n\nFor example, if you are using the `http` module in Node.js, you should switch to using the `https` module instead. If you are using a library like axios, you should ensure that the base URL starts with \"https://\".\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example provided seems to be using Angular's TestBed and HttpClient modules, along with a custom ComplaintService and some form of HTTP interceptor. The exact dependencies would depend on the specifics of these custom services and interceptors.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP: HTTP Strict Transport Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/HTTP_Strict_Transport_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
