              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This can lead to data being intercepted, read, and modified by attackers. In the context of the provided code, if the `DataSubjectService` or `provideHttpClient` functions are communicating with a server over HTTP, they could be exposing sensitive data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much harder for attackers to intercept or modify the data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout the full context of the code, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, generally, you should ensure that any URLs used in `DataSubjectService` or `provideHttpClient` start with 'https://' rather than 'http://'. If these functions are using a base URL that's defined elsewhere in your code, make sure that base URL uses HTTPS.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to be using Angular's testing utilities, so the following dependencies are likely required:\n\n- `@angular/core`: for `TestBed`\n- `@angular/common/http`: if `provideHttpClient` is using Angular's `HttpClient`\n- `@angular/common/http/testing`: if `provideHttpClientTesting` is using Angular's `HttpClientTestingModule`\n\nThe `DataSubjectService` and `withInterceptorsFromDi` are likely custom functions or services, so any dependencies they have would depend on their implementation.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
