                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over an unencrypted HTTP connection. This can lead to the interception of the data by malicious actors, leading to data breaches and other security issues. This vulnerability is particularly relevant when dealing with APIs and other server-side connections that handle sensitive data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data sent between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for malicious actors to intercept and understand the data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, it's not directly visible if HTTP or HTTPS is used, as it depends on the implementation of `CountryMappingService` and `provideHttpClient` functions. However, if these functions are using HTTP, they should be modified to use HTTPS. \n\nFor example, if `CountryMappingService` is using a base URL for its requests, this URL should start with \"https://\" instead of \"http://\". Similarly, if `provideHttpClient` is creating an instance of HttpClient with a base URL, this URL should also start with \"https://\".\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using Angular's testing utilities, so the following dependencies are required:\n\n- `@angular/core`: for `TestBed`\n- `@angular/common/http`: for `HttpClient`\n- `@angular/common/http/testing`: for `HttpClientTestingModule`\n\nThe actual services (`CountryMappingService`, `provideHttpClient`, `withInterceptorsFromDi`, `provideHttpClientTesting`) are not standard Angular services, so their dependencies would depend on their implementation.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
