            {
              "id": "915482ea-700d-346b-b617-f2a1b1886751",
              "name": "Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. The potential XSS vulnerability from user-controlled data in an HTML string is a common security issue in JavaScript. This vulnerability can allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. These scripts can steal sensitive information like session cookies, perform actions on behalf of the user, or even rewrite the HTML content of the page.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `id` parameter is directly used to construct a URL for an HTTP GET request. If the `id` parameter is controlled by the user and not properly sanitized, it can lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. **Validate Input**: Ensure that all user-supplied input is validated before it is processed. Use a whitelist of allowed inputs rather than a blacklist of disallowed inputs.\n\n2. **Encode Output**: Ensure that user-supplied input is properly encoded before it is included in HTML output. This can prevent potentially harmful characters from being interpreted as code.\n\n3. **Use Appropriate Response Headers**: Use HTTP headers like Content-Security-Policy (CSP) to restrict the sources of scripts and other potentially harmful resources.\n\n4. **Use Updated Libraries**: Ensure that all libraries and frameworks used by your application are up-to-date and free of known vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe `id` parameter should be validated and sanitized before it is used to construct the URL. Here is a simple fix using a regular expression to ensure that `id` only contains alphanumeric characters:\n\n```javascript\nif (/^\\w+$/.test(id)) {\n  return this.http.get(`${this.host}/${id}`).pipe(map((response: DeliverySingleMethodResponse) => response.data), catchError((err) => { throw err }))\n} else {\n  throw new Error('Invalid ID');\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet appears to use the following libraries:\n\n- `rxjs` for the `pipe`, `map`, and `catchError` functions.\n- An HTTP client library, possibly `@angular/common/http` if this is an Angular application.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1ac6e32d-732b-3409-bf58-9d8b9f28290c",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over an unencrypted HTTP connection. This can lead to the interception of data by malicious actors, leading to data breaches and other security issues. In the provided code, the TestBed is configuring a module with an HTTP client that may not be using HTTPS for secure communication.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much harder for attackers to intercept and read the data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nEnsure that all HTTP requests are made over HTTPS. If the server supports it, enable HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) to ensure that all future requests are automatically redirected to HTTPS.\n\n```javascript\nTestBed.configureTestingModule({\n  imports: [HttpClientModule],\n  providers: [FeedbackService, provideHttpClient(withInterceptorsFromDi()), provideHttpClientTesting()]\n})\n```\n\nIn the above code, `HttpClientModule` is imported from `@angular/common/http` which uses HTTPS by default.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/core` for `TestBed`\n- `@angular/common/http` for `HttpClientModule`\n- The custom `FeedbackService`, `provideHttpClient`, `withInterceptorsFromDi`, and `provideHttpClientTesting` functions or services\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "24f51d44-6004-3614-9ceb-23f643c73c11",
              "name": "Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. An attacker can inject malicious scripts into the HTML content, which will be executed by the victim's browser. In the provided code, the `id` parameter is directly embedded into the HTML string, which could potentially lead to an XSS vulnerability if the `id` is user-controlled and not properly sanitized.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate, sanitize, and escape user input. Never insert untrusted data directly into HTML string. Use appropriate response headers to prevent any kind of script execution.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nimport { HttpParams } from '@angular/common/http';\n\nlet params = new HttpParams().set('id', id);\nreturn this.http.delete(`${this.host}`, { params: params }).pipe(map((response: any) => response.data), catchError((err) => { throw err }))\n```\n\nIn the above code, we are using Angular's `HttpParams` to safely encode the `id` parameter.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- Angular's HttpClient module\n- RxJS library\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
