            {
              "id": "9db1b1f0-d2ea-38dd-93f2-d587f41fd1d4",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over an unencrypted HTTP connection. This can lead to the interception of data by malicious actors, leading to data breaches and other security issues. In the provided code, the TestBed is configuring a module with an HTTP client that may not be using HTTPS for secure communication.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for attackers to intercept and understand the data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nEnsure that all HTTP requests are made over HTTPS. If the server supports HTTPS, simply replace 'http://' with 'https://' in the URL. If the server does not support HTTPS, you may need to configure it to do so.\n\n```javascript\nTestBed.configureTestingModule({\n  imports: [],\n  providers: [HintService, provideHttpClient(withInterceptorsFromDi('https://')), provideHttpClientTesting()]\n})\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/core` for `TestBed`\n- `@angular/common/http` for `HttpClient`\n- `@angular/common/http/testing` for `HttpClientTestingModule`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
