              "id": "473e52a1-619e-3ad2-ba5f-2fb77b6c8ff8",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\" refers to the risk of sensitive data being intercepted when it is transmitted over an unencrypted HTTP connection. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when HTTP requests are made without using the secure HTTPS protocol. This can allow an attacker to intercept, view, and potentially modify the data being transmitted, leading to a Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always use HTTPS for all connections, especially when transmitting sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for an attacker to intercept and understand the data. \n\nIn addition, it is also recommended to use HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), which forces browsers to only use HTTPS, even if the application tries to use HTTP. This can help prevent downgrade attacks, where an attacker tries to force the connection to use HTTP instead of HTTPS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not directly show the use of HTTP or HTTPS, but it is assumed that the `ImageCaptchaService` or `provideHttpClient` might be using HTTP. To fix this, ensure that these services are using HTTPS. If these services are using a URL to make requests, ensure that the URL starts with `https://` instead of `http://`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using Angular's testing module, so the following dependencies are required:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `@angular/common/http/testing`\n\nThe `ImageCaptchaService` and `provideHttpClient` are custom services, so their dependencies would depend on their implementation.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d7dcc145-1ccc-3516-9a0a-cc05075bb916",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over an unencrypted HTTP connection. This can lead to data being intercepted and read by malicious third parties. In the context of the provided code, this vulnerability could be exploited if the `LanguagesService` or `provideHttpClient` functions are communicating with a server over HTTP.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data sent between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for a third party to intercept and read the data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe specific fix would depend on the implementation of `LanguagesService` and `provideHttpClient`. However, generally, you should ensure that these services are configured to use HTTPS. For example, if `provideHttpClient` is creating an instance of Angular's `HttpClient`, you could ensure that the base URL is an HTTPS URL:\n\n```javascript\nprovideHttpClient() {\n  return new HttpClient({\n    baseURL: 'https://example.com'\n  });\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to be using Angular's testing utilities, so the following dependencies are likely required:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `@angular/common/http/testing`\n- `rxjs`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
