            {
              "id": "ce736ef0-88f4-3a8d-bd9c-2626b5be4efa",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the promise returned by `backupFile.text().then()` is not properly handled in case of rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that promises have a `.catch()` method to handle any errors that may occur during the execution of the promise. This will prevent the application from crashing and provide a way to handle the error gracefully.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, the promise returned by `backupFile.text().then()` should have a `.catch()` method to handle any errors. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```javascript\nreturn from(backupFile.text().then((backupData) => {\n  // ... existing code ...\n}).catch((err: Error) => {\n  console.error('An error occurred:', err);\n  // Handle the error appropriately here\n}));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to be using the following libraries:\n\n- RxJS (for the `from`, `of`, and `forkJoin` functions)\n- Angular Material (for the `snackBar` service)\n- An unspecified service for `challengeService`\n- An unspecified service for `snackBarHelperService`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0bd9bc56-9d4c-38dc-8a81-b3bff240a4f2",
              "name": "Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the `backupFile.name` is directly concatenated into the string that is passed to `this.snackBar.open()`. If `backupFile.name` is user-controlled and not properly sanitized, it could lead to a potential XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Use frameworks that automatically escape XSS by design, such as Ruby on Rails, React JS, etc.\n- Escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL).\n- Apply context-sensitive encoding when modifying the browser document on the client side.\n- Validate, filter or sanitize user-generated inputs.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn this specific case, you should sanitize `backupFile.name` before using it. Here is an example of how you can do it:\n\n```javascript\nconst sanitizedBackupFileName = encodeURI(backupFile.name);\nconst snackBarRef = this.snackBar.open('Backup has been restored from ' + sanitizedBackupFileName, 'Apply changes now', {\n  duration: 10000,\n  panelClass: ['mat-body']\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using Angular Material's Snackbar component. Therefore, the library dependencies required by the code example to execute properly are:\n\n- Angular\n- Angular Material\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c8afed33-c23d-36cc-ab48-b06e722df09d",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability can occur when user-controlled data is directly embedded into an HTML string without proper sanitization or encoding. In the provided JavaScript code, the error message (`err.message`) is directly embedded into an HTML string. If an attacker can control the content of `err.message`, they could inject malicious scripts that would be executed in the context of the victim's browser session.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always sanitize and/or encode user-controlled data before embedding it into HTML. This can be done using various methods, such as HTML encoding, JavaScript encoding, or using safe frameworks that automatically encode user data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, you can mitigate the potential XSS vulnerability by ensuring that `err.message` is properly encoded before it is embedded into the HTML string. Here is a simple fix using the `encodeURI` function:\n\n```javascript\nthis.snackBarHelperService.open(`Backup restore operation failed: ${encodeURI(err.message)}`, 'errorBar')\n```\n\nPlease note that this is a simple fix and may not cover all possible attack vectors. For a more comprehensive solution, consider using a library specifically designed for sanitizing user input, such as DOMPurify.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using a custom service `snackBarHelperService`. Without more context, it's hard to determine the exact library dependencies. However, it's likely that this code is part of an Angular application, which would require the `@angular/core` library.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/)\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "712a2a33-a3f1-3572-895d-a4815f40b27a",
              "name": "HTTP Server Usage Vulnerability Due to Lack of TLS Encryption",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"HTTP Server Usage Vulnerability Due to Lack of TLS Encryption\" refers to the situation where an application communicates with an HTTP server without using Transport Layer Security (TLS) encryption. This can expose sensitive data to potential eavesdropping or man-in-the-middle attacks. In the context of JavaScript, this can occur when using HTTP client libraries to make requests to servers without enabling or properly configuring TLS.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS instead of HTTP for any communication that involves sensitive data. This ensures that the data is encrypted during transit and cannot be easily intercepted or tampered with. \n\nIn addition, consider using HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) to ensure that the browser only communicates with the server using a secure HTTPS connection. This can help prevent downgrade attacks where an attacker tries to force the communication to use an insecure HTTP connection.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code snippet does not show the actual HTTP request, but assuming that the `OrderHistoryService` or the `provideHttpClient` function is making HTTP requests, you should ensure that these requests are made over HTTPS. \n\nIf you are using the `http` module in Node.js, you should switch to the `https` module. If you are using a library like `axios` or `fetch`, you should ensure that the URLs you are requesting start with `https://`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet appears to be using Angular's testing utilities, so the following dependencies are likely required:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `rxjs`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "603f464d-0806-33da-a1c5-7782ec95aa0f",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over an unencrypted HTTP connection. This can lead to the interception of the data by malicious actors, who can then use it for nefarious purposes. This vulnerability is particularly dangerous when dealing with sensitive data such as payment information, personal details, or login credentials.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) for all connections, especially when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data sent between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for attackers to intercept and understand the data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, it is not clear whether the HTTP connection is secure or not. However, if it is not, you should switch to using HTTPS. This can usually be done by simply changing the URL from `http://` to `https://`. If you are using a library or framework that handles HTTP connections, you should check its documentation for how to enable HTTPS.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using Angular's TestBed for testing, and a PaymentService and HttpClient for making HTTP requests. Therefore, the following libraries are likely required:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "bc698caa-b7e5-3730-8882-b2f5217be218",
              "name": "XSS Vulnerability in User-Controlled HTML Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the vulnerability lies in the fact that user-controlled data is being used to construct a URL without proper sanitization or encoding. This could potentially allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts or modify the URL to point to a different host.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Use frameworks that automatically escape XSS by design, such as Ruby on Rails, React JS, etc.\n- Escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL).\n- Apply context-sensitive encoding when modifying the browser document on the client side.\n- Validate, sanitize, and escape all user inputs.\n- Use Content Security Policy (CSP) as a defense-in-depth measure.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code should be updated to sanitize and encode the `id` and `params` variables before using them to construct the URL:\n\n```javascript\nlet sanitizedId = encodeURIComponent(id);\nlet sanitizedParams = encodeURIComponent(params);\nreturn this.http.put(`${this.host}/${sanitizedId}/delivery-status`, sanitizedParams).pipe(map((response: any) => response.data), catchError((err) => { throw err }))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example appears to be using the following libraries:\n\n- Angular's HttpClient module for making HTTP requests.\n- RxJS for handling asynchronous data streams.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "370ca1e7-c1a2-344d-b8ed-94d7ac1f6b86",
              "name": "Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. An attacker can inject malicious scripts into the HTML content, which will be executed by the victim's browser. In the provided code, the `id` parameter is directly used to construct a URL for an HTTP GET request. If the `id` parameter is controlled by the user and not properly sanitized, it can lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always validate, sanitize, and escape user input.\n2. Use HTTP response headers to restrict the capabilities of the browser to execute scripts.\n3. Use Content Security Policy (CSP) to restrict the sources from which scripts can be loaded.\n4. Use modern JavaScript frameworks that automatically escape user input.\n\n## Source Code Fix\n\nHere is a simple fix using the `encodeURIComponent` function to sanitize the `id` parameter:\n\n```javascript\nreturn this.http.get(`${this.host}/${encodeURIComponent(id)}`).pipe(map((response: any) => response.data), catchError((err: Error) => { throw err }))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using the following libraries:\n\n- `rxjs` for the `pipe`, `map`, and `catchError` functions.\n- An HTTP client library, possibly `@angular/common/http` if this is an Angular application.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8bf63237-0192-3af1-91d7-e471e393cb25",
              "name": "Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. An attacker can inject malicious scripts into the HTML content, which will be executed by the victim's browser. In the provided code, the `id` parameter is directly embedded into the HTML string, which could potentially lead to an XSS vulnerability if the `id` is user-controlled and not properly sanitized.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate, sanitize, and escape user input. Never insert untrusted data directly into HTML string. Use appropriate response headers to prevent any kind of script execution.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nimport { HttpParams } from '@angular/common/http';\n\nlet params = new HttpParams().set('id', id);\nreturn this.http.delete(`${this.host}`, { params: params }).pipe(map((response: any) => response.data), catchError((err) => { throw err }))\n```\n\nIn the above code, we are using Angular's `HttpParams` to safely encode the `id` parameter.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- Angular's HttpClient module\n- RxJS library\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
