            {
              "id": "6fcf21b2-2352-38db-bcc0-1bb94a3026ec",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of unencrypted HTTP servers instead of HTTPS in JavaScript programming. This makes the application susceptible to Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks. In a MitM attack, the attacker intercepts the communication between two parties, alters the communication, and gains unauthorized access to sensitive information.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the TestBed is being configured with a PhotoWallService and two HttpClient providers. If these services are communicating over an unencrypted HTTP connection, it could expose the application to MitM attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS instead of HTTP for all communications. HTTPS ensures that the communication between the client and the server is encrypted and secure. This makes it difficult for an attacker to intercept and alter the communication.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe specific fix would depend on the implementation of the `provideHttpClient`, `withInterceptorsFromDi`, and `provideHttpClientTesting` functions. However, a general recommendation would be to ensure that all HTTP requests are made over HTTPS. This can be done by setting the protocol to HTTPS in the base URL of the HttpClient.\n\n```javascript\nconst http = new HttpClient({\n  baseURL: 'https://your-secure-server.com',\n  // other options...\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using Angular's TestBed and HttpClient. Therefore, the following dependencies are required:\n\n- `@angular/core`: for the TestBed\n- `@angular/common/http`: for the HttpClient\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d16401ad-422e-3beb-9e08-54751cb5ae0b",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the potential XSS vulnerability arises from the use of user-controlled data (`id`) in the construction of an HTML string that forms part of a URL. If an attacker can control the `id` value, they could potentially inject malicious scripts that would be executed when the URL is processed.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. **Validate Input**: Ensure that the `id` value conforms to expected formats and values. This could involve checking that it is a number, or that it matches a certain regular expression.\n\n2. **Encode Output**: Use appropriate encoding functions to ensure that any potentially harmful characters in the `id` value are rendered harmless.\n\n3. **Use Appropriate Response Headers**: Use HTTP headers like Content-Security-Policy (CSP) to restrict the types of content that your application is allowed to execute.\n\n4. **Update and Patch**: Keep all systems, software, and components that are under your control up-to-date and patched.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe following code snippet demonstrates how you might validate and encode the `id` value to mitigate the potential XSS vulnerability:\n\n```javascript\nimport { isNumeric } from 'validator';\n\n// ...\n\nif (!isNumeric(id)) {\n  throw new Error('Invalid ID');\n}\n\nreturn this.http.get(`${this.host}/${encodeURIComponent(id)}/reviews`).pipe(\n  map((response: any) => response.data), catchError((err: Error) => {\n    throw err\n  })\n)\n```\n\nIn this code, the `isNumeric` function from the `validator` library is used to check that the `id` value is a number. The `encodeURIComponent` function is then used to encode the `id` value, ensuring that any potentially harmful characters are rendered harmless.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `rxjs` for the `pipe`, `map`, and `catchError` functions.\n- `validator` for the `isNumeric` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
