            {
              "id": "d3cbef3f-a9e1-3bae-ad93-4487eb3cfdb6",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over an unencrypted HTTP connection. This can lead to data being intercepted and read by unauthorized parties, leading to data breaches and other security issues. This vulnerability is particularly relevant when dealing with APIs and other server-side interactions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for unauthorized parties to intercept and read the data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe TestBed.configureTestingModule code provided does not directly deal with HTTP or HTTPS connections, so it's not possible to provide a specific fix for this code. However, when making HTTP requests in Angular (which the provided code seems to be using), you should always use the HttpClient module with HTTPS URLs. Here's an example:\n\n```javascript\nimport { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';\n\nconstructor(private http: HttpClient) { }\n\ngetData() {\n  return this.http.get('https://secure-api.example.com/data');\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using Angular's testing utilities, so the following dependencies are likely required:\n\n- `@angular/core`: for the `TestBed` class\n- `@angular/common/http`: for the `HttpClient` class\n- `@angular/common/http/testing`: for the `HttpClientTestingModule` class\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7f12f35a-0960-326e-b6e6-c92cb9aecd6a",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the application is vulnerable to XSS because it includes user-controlled data (`criteria`) in a request URL without proper escaping. An attacker could potentially manipulate the `criteria` parameter to inject malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. **Escape User Input**: Always escape user-controlled data before including it in your HTML. This will prevent any potentially malicious code from being executed.\n\n2. **Use Safe APIs**: Use APIs, such as `encodeURIComponent()`, that automatically escape user input.\n\n3. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Implementing CSP would reduce the risk of XSS attacks by declaring which dynamic resources are allowed to load.\n\n## Source Code Fix\n\nHere's how you could fix the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nreturn this.http.get(`${this.hostServer}/rest/products/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(criteria)}`).pipe(map((response: any) => response.data), catchError((err) => { throw err }))\n```\n\nIn this fix, `encodeURIComponent()` is used to escape the user-controlled `criteria` data.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using the following libraries:\n\n- `rxjs`: For the `pipe`, `map`, and `catchError` functions.\n- `@angular/common/http`: For the `http.get` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "29da129f-1686-3f7a-8336-1c8212965edb",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. The vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String specifically refers to the scenario where an attacker can control the data that is directly used to generate HTML content. In the provided JavaScript code, the user-controlled data is included in the URL string without proper sanitization or encoding, which could potentially lead to an XSS attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate, sanitize, and escape user input.\n2. Use HTTP response headers to enable certain browser security features.\n3. Use secure libraries and frameworks that automatically escape XSS by design.\n4. Use Content Security Policy (CSP) as a defense-in-depth measure.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, the `id` parameter is directly included in the URL string without any sanitization or encoding. This could potentially lead to an XSS attack if an attacker can control the `id` parameter. To fix this, you should encode the `id` parameter using `encodeURIComponent` function:\n\n```javascript\nreturn this.http.get(`${this.host}/${encodeURIComponent(id)}?d=${encodeURIComponent(new Date().toDateString())}`).pipe(map((response: any) =>\n      response.data), catchError((err) => { throw err }))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using the following libraries:\n\n1. RxJS for the `pipe`, `map`, and `catchError` functions.\n2. Angular's HttpClient for the `http.get` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a76b2938-59c9-3d26-aeff-9d5b5fa983e8",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. The \"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\" vulnerability in JavaScript refers to a situation where user-controlled data is directly used in an HTML string. This can allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts and execute them in the user's browser.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `id` and `params` are directly used in the HTTP PUT request without any sanitization or encoding. If these values are controlled by the user, it can lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always validate, sanitize, and/or escape user input before using it in your application.\n2. Use HTTP response headers to restrict the browser from executing any scripts in the response.\n3. Use Content Security Policy (CSP) to restrict the sources from which scripts can be loaded.\n4. Use modern JavaScript frameworks that automatically escape user input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code should be updated to sanitize the `id` and `params` before using them in the HTTP PUT request. Here is an example using the `escape-html` library:\n\n```javascript\nimport escape from 'escape-html';\n\nlet sanitizedId = escape(id);\nlet sanitizedParams = escape(params);\n\nreturn this.http.put(`${this.host}/${sanitizedId}`, sanitizedParams).pipe(map((response: any) => response.data), catchError((err) => { throw err }))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `rxjs` for the `pipe`, `map`, and `catchError` functions.\n- `@angular/common/http` for the `http` service.\n- `escape-html` for sanitizing user input.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d1630339-936c-3aed-8222-124ffd3032c9",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of unencrypted HTTP servers instead of HTTPS in JavaScript programming. This makes the application susceptible to Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks. In a MitM attack, the attacker intercepts the communication between two parties, alters the communication, and gains unauthorized access to sensitive information.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the TestBed is being configured with certain providers, but there is no explicit mention of using HTTPS for secure communication. This could potentially expose the application to MitM attacks if the communication is happening over an unencrypted HTTP connection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS instead of HTTP for communication. HTTPS ensures that the communication between the client and the server is encrypted and secure. This makes it difficult for an attacker to intercept and alter the communication.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe specific fix would depend on the overall context of the application. However, in general, you should ensure that all API calls or data transfers are done over HTTPS. If you're using a library like axios for HTTP requests, you can set the base URL to use HTTPS as shown below:\n\n```javascript\nconst axios = require('axios');\n\naxios.defaults.baseURL = 'https://your-api-url.com';\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using Angular's TestBed for configuring a module. The dependencies required would be:\n\n- `@angular/core` for TestBed\n- `@angular/common/http` for HttpClient\n- The `QuantityService` and `provideHttpClient` would be application-specific services and would depend on their respective implementations.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6e872905-8a68-339a-8966-e69145946caf",
              "name": "High-Risk Asymmetric Private Key Exposure Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nHigh-Risk Asymmetric Private Key Exposure Vulnerability in Docker refers to the insecure usage of a private key within the Docker application code. In the provided code snippet, the private key is hardcoded into the application. This is a serious security concern as anyone who has access to the codebase can now access the private key. This can lead to unauthorized access, data breaches, and other security incidents.\n\n## Security Concern\n\nPrivate keys are meant to be kept secret and secure. When a private key is exposed or hardcoded into an application, it can be easily discovered by malicious actors. This can lead to unauthorized access to sensitive data or systems, impersonation of a user or system, and other security incidents. In the context of Docker, this could potentially allow an attacker to gain unauthorized access to Docker containers or images, leading to further compromise of the system.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n- Never hardcode sensitive information, such as private keys, into your application code.\n- Use secure methods for storing and retrieving sensitive information, such as environment variables or secure key stores.\n- Regularly rotate and change keys to limit the potential damage if a key is compromised.\n- Implement proper access controls and permissions to limit who can access sensitive information.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of hardcoding the private key into the application, it should be stored securely and retrieved when needed. Here is an example of how you can use environment variables to store the private key:\n\n```javascript\nconst privateKey = process.env.PRIVATE_KEY;\n```\n\nIn this example, the private key is stored as an environment variable named `PRIVATE_KEY`. This keeps the private key out of the codebase and allows it to be securely stored and managed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not appear to require any specific library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-798",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "HIGH",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-798"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "be0124f4-754c-3a86-ac42-71573628bc6f",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. The \"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\" vulnerability in JavaScript refers to a situation where user-controlled data is directly used in an HTML string. This can allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts and execute them in the user's browser.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `id` and `params` variables are directly included in the HTTP request without any sanitization or encoding. If these variables contain user-controlled data, this could lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Validate and sanitize user input: Ensure that user input matches expected patterns and remove or escape any potentially harmful characters.\n\n2. Use context-specific output encoding: When inserting user-controlled data into HTML, use context-specific encoding to prevent the browser from interpreting it as code.\n\n3. Use Content Security Policy (CSP): CSP can help to mitigate the impact of any XSS vulnerabilities by restricting the sources from which scripts can be loaded.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code should be updated to sanitize the `id` and `params` variables before they are used in the HTTP request. This could be done using a library like DOMPurify:\n\n```javascript\nimport DOMPurify from 'dompurify';\n\nlet sanitizedId = DOMPurify.sanitize(id);\nlet sanitizedParams = DOMPurify.sanitize(params);\n\nreturn this.http.put(`${this.host}/${sanitizedId}`, sanitizedParams).pipe(map((response: any) => response.data), catchError((error) => { throw error }))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- An HTTP client library (such as Axios or the Fetch API)\n- An Observable library (such as RxJS)\n- A sanitization library (such as DOMPurify)\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
