            {
              "id": "67faf7f3-4198-36a2-80f0-77a689f5c2ec",
              "name": "Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnselected Field Database Query Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a developer does not specify the fields to be returned from a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data if the query is used to return data to a client, as the client may receive more data than they should have access to. This vulnerability is particularly relevant when using NoSQL databases like MongoDB, which do not require the selection of fields in a query.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always specify the fields to be returned in a database query. This can be done by passing an object to the `find` method that specifies the fields to be returned. If a field is not specified in this object, it will not be returned in the query results.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the vulnerability in the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nservice.find({}, { _id: 1, field1: 1, field2: 1 }).subscribe((data) => (res = data))\n```\n\nIn this code, only the `_id`, `field1`, and `field2` fields will be returned in the query results.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example provided seems to be using an Observable pattern, which is common in Angular applications. Therefore, the code would require the following libraries to execute properly:\n\n- `rxjs`: This library is used for creating and working with observables in JavaScript.\n\n- A library for interacting with the database, such as `mongoose` for MongoDB.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-209",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-209"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f8ea0b50-d71d-30e5-a142-ce5e6684bd3f",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of unencrypted HTTP servers instead of HTTPS in JavaScript programming. This makes the application susceptible to Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks. In a MitM attack, the attacker intercepts the communication between two parties, alters the communication, and gains unauthorized access to sensitive information.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the TestBed is being configured with certain providers, but it doesn't specify whether the communication with these providers is happening over HTTP or HTTPS. If it's over HTTP, then it's vulnerable to MitM attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS for communication. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it difficult for an attacker to read or modify the data. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code fix would depend on the implementation of the `provideHttpClient`, `withInterceptorsFromDi`, and `provideHttpClientTesting` functions. However, a general recommendation would be to ensure that these functions are set up to use HTTPS. \n\nFor example, if `provideHttpClient` is using Angular's `HttpClientModule`, you could set up an interceptor to ensure that all requests are sent over HTTPS:\n\n```javascript\nimport { Injectable } from '@angular/core';\nimport { HttpInterceptor, HttpRequest, HttpHandler } from '@angular/common/http';\n\n@Injectable()\nexport class HttpsInterceptor implements HttpInterceptor {\n  intercept(req: HttpRequest<any>, next: HttpHandler) {\n    const secureReq = req.clone({\n      url: req.url.replace('http://', 'https://')\n    });\n    return next.handle(secureReq);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThen, you would provide this interceptor in your TestBed configuration:\n\n```javascript\nTestBed.configureTestingModule({\n  imports: [],\n  providers: [\n    RecycleService, \n    provideHttpClient(withInterceptorsFromDi()), \n    provideHttpClientTesting(),\n    { provide: HTTP_INTERCEPTORS, useClass: HttpsInterceptor, multi: true }\n  ]\n})\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using Angular's testing utilities, so the following dependencies are required:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `@angular/common/http/testing`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
