            {
              "id": "655c807c-ce48-361e-b171-913b0b8cd614",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of unencrypted HTTP servers instead of HTTPS in JavaScript programming. This makes the application susceptible to Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks. In a MitM attack, the attacker intercepts the communication between two parties, alters the communication, and gains unauthorized access to sensitive information.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the TestBed is being configured with certain providers, but it doesn't specify whether the communication with these providers is happening over HTTP or HTTPS. If it's over HTTP, then it's vulnerable to MitM attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS for communication. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it difficult for an attacker to read or modify the data that is being transmitted.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code fix would depend on how the `provideHttpClient` and `provideHttpClientTesting` functions are implemented. However, a general recommendation would be to ensure that these functions are set up to use HTTPS. Here's an example of how you might enforce this:\n\n```javascript\nimport { HttpClient, HttpClientModule } from '@angular/common/http';\n\nTestBed.configureTestingModule({\n  imports: [HttpClientModule],\n  providers: [SecurityAnswerService, provideHttpClient(withInterceptorsFromDi()), provideHttpClientTesting()]\n})\n\nfunction provideHttpClient(interceptors) {\n  return {\n    provide: HttpClient,\n    useFactory: (backend: HttpBackend, defaultOptions: HttpRequest<any>) => {\n      defaultOptions.url = defaultOptions.url.replace('http://', 'https://');\n      return new HttpClient(backend, defaultOptions);\n    },\n    deps: [HttpBackend, HttpRequest]\n  };\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the `provideHttpClient` function is modified to replace 'http://' with 'https://' in the URL.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using Angular's testing utilities, so the following libraries would be required:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic/testing`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
