            {
              "id": "94860669-95ad-3558-b29b-f43189c8efbc",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over an unencrypted HTTP connection. This can lead to data being intercepted, read, and manipulated by attackers. In the context of the provided code, if the `SecurityQuestionService` or `provideHttpClient` methods are communicating with a server over HTTP, they could be exposing sensitive data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much harder for attackers to read or manipulate the data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe specific fix would depend on the implementation of `SecurityQuestionService` and `provideHttpClient`. However, in general, you should ensure that any URLs used to communicate with servers start with 'https://' rather than 'http://'. If these methods are using a base URL that is defined elsewhere, ensure that this base URL uses HTTPS.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to be using Angular's testing utilities, so the following dependencies are likely required:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `@angular/common/http/testing`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
