            {
              "id": "f4fa8e06-04e5-36db-8e55-133b4e6c8a64",
              "name": "URI Contains Username and Password Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"URI Contains Username and Password Vulnerability\" is a security issue where sensitive information such as usernames and passwords are included in the URI. This is a problem because URIs are often logged in various places such as browser history, web server logs, and network infrastructure logs. This could potentially expose sensitive information to unauthorized individuals.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the email parameter is being passed in the URI which could potentially expose sensitive information.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, sensitive information should never be included in the URI. Instead, sensitive information should be passed in a secure manner such as in the body of a POST request or in an HTTP header. Additionally, all communication should be done over HTTPS to ensure that the data is encrypted during transit.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nconst httpOptions = {\n  headers: new HttpHeaders({\n    'Content-Type':  'application/json'\n  })\n};\n\nconst body = JSON.stringify({ email: 'x@y.z' });\n\nconst req = httpMock.expectOne('http://localhost:3000/rest/user/security-question', body, httpOptions);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/common/http` for `HttpHeaders`\n- `@angular/common/http/testing` for `HttpTestingController`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-598: Information Exposure Through Query Strings in URL](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/598.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-598",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/598.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-598"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b44e4218-1b9c-3a50-b1d9-395d0d3c326c",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This vulnerability can lead to exposure of sensitive data to unauthorized parties, as the data is transmitted in plain text over the network. Attackers can easily intercept and read the data, leading to potential data breaches.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when transmitting sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for attackers to read the data even if they manage to intercept it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, it's not clear where the HTTP connection is being made. However, the general recommendation is to ensure that all HTTP connections are replaced with HTTPS. Here's an example of how to do this with Angular's HttpClient:\n\n```javascript\nimport { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';\n\nconstructor(private http: HttpClient) { }\n\ngetSecureData() {\n  return this.http.get('https://secure-api.example.com/data');\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the `get` method of HttpClient is used to make a secure HTTPS request to 'https://secure-api.example.com/data'.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using Angular's TestBed and HttpClient. The following dependencies are required:\n\n- `@angular/core`: for TestBed and dependency injection.\n- `@angular/common/http`: for HttpClient.\n- `@angular/common/http/testing`: for HttpClientTestingModule.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "082ac08d-549b-3d60-aa34-6d84060f13b8",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is using user-controlled data (`window.location.pathname`) in the construction of a path for a socket connection. If an attacker can manipulate this data, it could lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. **Validate Input**: User input should be validated and sanitized before it is processed by the application. This includes data from the URL, forms, cookies, and anything else provided by the user.\n\n2. **Encode Output**: User-controlled data should be HTML-encoded before it is included in an HTML response. This will prevent any potentially malicious code from being executed by the browser.\n\n3. **Use Appropriate Response Headers**: By setting the HTTP response headers correctly, you can instruct the browser to treat the content in a way that can prevent the execution of malicious scripts.\n\n4. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Implementing a CSP can effectively reduce the risk and impact of XSS attacks in modern browsers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code should be updated to sanitize the user-controlled data before using it:\n\n```javascript\nvar path = window.location.pathname;\npath = encodeURI(path.endsWith('/') ? path : path + '/');\nthis._socket = io(window.location.origin, {\n  path: path + 'socket.io'\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- [socket.io](https://socket.io/)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "250aa830-a343-3e3c-82ca-ab3a3d75cd56",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. An attacker can inject malicious scripts into the HTML content, which will be executed by the victim's browser. In the provided JavaScript code, the user-controlled data is directly used to construct an HTTP request URL, which can lead to an XSS vulnerability if the `params` is not properly sanitized.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate, sanitize, and escape untrusted input. In this case, the `params` should be validated and sanitized before being used to construct the HTTP request URL. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou can use the `encodeURIComponent` function to encode the `params` before using it in the URL. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```javascript\nreturn this.http.get(`${this.host}/${encodeURIComponent(params)}`).pipe(map((response: any) => response), catchError((error) => { throw error }))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using the `rxjs` library for handling asynchronous data streams. It also uses the `http` module, which could be from a variety of libraries such as `axios`, `http`, or `@angular/common/http`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Unvalidated_Redirects_and_Forwards_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6480976c-330c-3bf2-a0b6-ee283ba1f409",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over an unencrypted HTTP connection. This can lead to data being intercepted, read, and manipulated by attackers. In the context of the provided code, if the `TwoFactorAuthService` or the `HttpClient` are communicating with a server over HTTP, this vulnerability could be present.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) for any communication that involves sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for attackers to read or manipulate the data.\n\nIn addition, consider using HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), which tells the browser to only use HTTPS, even if the user or a link specifies HTTP. This can help protect against attacks that attempt to downgrade the connection from HTTPS to HTTP.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not show where or how the server URL is specified, so it's not possible to provide a specific code fix. However, here is a general example of how to use HTTPS with the `HttpClient` in Angular:\n\n```javascript\nimport { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';\n\nconstructor(private http: HttpClient) { }\n\ngetSecureData() {\n  return this.http.get('https://secure.example.com/data');\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the URL for the `get` request starts with `https://`, indicating that it uses HTTPS.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to be using Angular and its testing utilities. The required dependencies would likely include:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `@angular/common/http/testing`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "99906f16-f0cc-3948-8684-a24fa9297d29",
              "name": "\"Hardcoded Secret Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nHardcoded Secret Vulnerability refers to the practice of embedding sensitive information such as passwords, secret keys, or any form of secret credentials directly into the source code. This is a serious security risk because the source code can be easily accessible by unauthorized users or malicious attackers. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can be exploited if the source code is publicly available or if the attacker gains access to the source code.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the password, initialToken, and setupToken are hardcoded into the source code, which is a clear example of this vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid hardcoding sensitive information directly into the source code. Instead, use environment variables or secure configuration files to store this information. This way, even if an attacker gains access to the source code, they won't be able to access the sensitive information.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\n// Use environment variables to store sensitive information\nconst password = process.env.PASSWORD;\nconst initialToken = process.env.INITIAL_TOKEN;\nconst setupToken = process.env.SETUP_TOKEN;\n\nexpect(req.request.body).toEqual({ password: password, initialToken: initialToken, setupToken: setupToken })\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using Jest for testing, so the following library is required:\n\n- Jest\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Hardcoded Secret](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-798",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-798"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "46f2a8e3-d67a-39c1-a054-6b86f258b072",
              "name": "\"Hardcoded Secret Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nHardcoded Secret Vulnerability refers to the practice of embedding sensitive information such as passwords, encryption keys, and API keys directly into the source code. This is a security risk as it exposes these secrets to anyone who has access to the source code. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can be exploited if the source code is publicly available or if an attacker gains access to the source code.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the password 's3cr3t!' is hardcoded into the test assertion. This is a security risk as it exposes the password to anyone who has access to the source code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid hardcoding secrets into your source code. Instead, use environment variables or secure configuration files to store sensitive information. This ensures that the secrets are not exposed in the source code and can be changed easily if needed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of hardcoding the password into the test assertion, store it in an environment variable and reference the environment variable in the test assertion. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```javascript\nprocess.env['PASSWORD'] = 's3cr3t!';\nexpect(req.request.body).toEqual({ password: process.env['PASSWORD'] });\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet appears to be using the Jest testing framework. Therefore, the `jest` package is required for the code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Hardcoded Secrets](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-798",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-798"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a41d28be-0966-3e3f-b8f6-6223331587cf",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This vulnerability can lead to exposure of sensitive data to unauthorized parties, as the data is transmitted in plain text over the network. Attackers can easily intercept and read the data, leading to potential data breaches.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when transmitting sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for attackers to read the data even if they manage to intercept it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, it's not clear whether HTTP or HTTPS is being used, as the URLs are not shown. However, if HTTP is being used, it should be replaced with HTTPS. If the server doesn't support HTTPS, it should be configured to do so.\n\nHere's an example of how to use HTTPS with HttpClient in Angular:\n\n```javascript\nimport { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';\n\nconstructor(private http: HttpClient) { }\n\ngetSecureData() {\n  return this.http.get('https://secure.example.com/data');\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to be using Angular's TestBed and HttpClient modules, so the following dependencies are required:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic/testing`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
