            {
              "id": "4f1d8e1e-3c27-318f-818c-989f5fb417de",
              "name": "Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnselected Field Database Query Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a developer does not specify the fields to be returned from a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data if the query is used to return data to a client, as the client may receive more data than they should have access to. This vulnerability is particularly relevant when using NoSQL databases like MongoDB, which do not require the selection of fields in a query.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always specify the fields to be returned in a database query. This can be done by passing an object to the `find` method that specifies the fields to be returned. If a field is not specified in this object, it will not be returned in the query results.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the vulnerability in the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nservice.find({}, { _id: 1, field1: 1, field2: 1 }).subscribe((data) => (res = data))\n```\n\nIn this code, only the `_id`, `field1`, and `field2` fields will be returned in the query results.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example provided seems to be using an Observable pattern, which is common in Angular applications. Therefore, the code would require the following libraries to execute properly:\n\n- `rxjs`: This library is used for creating and working with observables in JavaScript.\n\n- A library for interacting with the database, such as `mongoose` for MongoDB.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-209",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-209"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ca0b68b1-d653-3846-a80d-8a2a796754b2",
              "name": "Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. An attacker can inject malicious scripts into the HTML content, which will be executed by the victim's browser. In this case, the vulnerability arises from user-controlled data in an HTML string.\n\nThe provided JavaScript code is vulnerable to XSS because it uses user-controlled data (`id`) in a HTTP GET request without proper sanitization or encoding. An attacker could potentially manipulate the `id` to inject malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. **Validate Input**: Ensure that the user-controlled data is what you expect. For example, if `id` should be a number, make sure it is a number.\n\n2. **Encode Output**: Ensure that user-controlled data is properly encoded before it is included in HTML content. This can prevent the browser from interpreting it as code.\n\n3. **Use Appropriate Response Headers**: Use HTTP headers like Content-Security-Policy (CSP) to restrict the sources from which scripts can be loaded.\n\n4. **Use Safe APIs**: Use APIs, such as the DOM `textContent` property or the jQuery `text` method, which automatically escape HTML markup.\n\n## Source Code Fix\n\nHere is a simple fix using the `encodeURIComponent` function to encode the `id`:\n\n```javascript\nreturn this.http.get(`${this.host}/${encodeURIComponent(id)}`).pipe(map((response: any) => response.data), catchError((err) => { throw err }))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using the following libraries:\n\n- RxJS for the `pipe`, `map`, and `catchError` functions.\n- Angular's HttpClient for the `http.get` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d1d86e47-a0c1-305d-bd94-0a305217144e",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability from user-controlled data in HTML string exists in the provided JavaScript code. This vulnerability occurs when untrusted data is included in a web page without proper validation or escaping, allowing an attacker to inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. These scripts can steal sensitive data like passwords, session cookies, or deface web sites, turning them into phishing sites.\n\nIn the provided code, the user-controlled data (`passwords.current`, `passwords.new`, `passwords.repeat`) is directly concatenated into the URL string without any sanitization or encoding. This could allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts by manipulating the password fields.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Avoid inserting untrusted data directly into the HTML document, JavaScript code, CSS blocks, etc.\n2. Use secure methods for inserting user-controlled data into HTML strings, such as `textContent` property or `createTextNode` method, which automatically escape HTML special characters.\n3. Use HTTP POST method instead of GET for sending sensitive data like passwords.\n4. Use parameterized queries or prepared statements to prevent SQL injection attacks.\n5. Validate and sanitize user inputs on the server side.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe recommended fix for the provided code is to use HTTP POST method with a request body instead of GET method with URL parameters:\n\n```javascript\nreturn this.http.post(this.hostServer + '/rest/user/change-password', passwords)\n  .pipe(map((response: any) => response.user), catchError((err) => { throw err.error }))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Angular's HttpClient module for making HTTP requests.\n- RxJS for handling asynchronous data streams.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top 10 - 2017: Injection](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A1_2017-Injection)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f4dc2d2b-6c62-3e1d-a5f8-46a46b2d3f27",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability can occur when user-controlled data is used in an HTML string. In the provided JavaScript code, the `accessToken` is directly concatenated into the URL string. If the `accessToken` is user-controlled and not properly sanitized, it can lead to an XSS attack. An attacker can inject malicious scripts into the `accessToken` which will be executed when the URL is accessed.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always sanitize user-controlled data before using it. This can be done by encoding the data to ensure that any special characters do not have their special meanings. In JavaScript, you can use the `encodeURIComponent()` function to encode user-controlled data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the fixed code:\n\n```javascript\nreturn this.http.get('https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v1/userinfo?alt=json&access_token=' + encodeURIComponent(accessToken))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using Angular's HttpClient module. So, the library dependencies required by the code example to execute properly are:\n\n- `@angular/common`: This package contains HttpClient module which provides a simplified API for HTTP functionality.\n- `rxjs`: This library is used for handling asynchronous data and can be used with HttpClient.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "63b0be20-9601-3bac-9f11-f65b9daf5d1d",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This vulnerability can lead to exposure of sensitive data to unauthorized parties, as the data can be easily intercepted and read by anyone who has access to the network.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the TestBed is configuring a module with an HTTP client that does not specify whether the connection should be encrypted or not. This could potentially lead to the usage of unencrypted HTTP connections, which is a security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use encrypted connections (HTTPS) when dealing with sensitive data. This ensures that the data is encrypted during transit and cannot be read by unauthorized parties.\n\nIn JavaScript, you can use the `https` module to create an HTTPS server. This module provides the same API as the `http` module, but uses SSL/TLS to encrypt the connection.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to create an HTTPS server in JavaScript:\n\n```javascript\nconst https = require('https');\nconst fs = require('fs');\n\nconst options = {\n  key: fs.readFileSync('test/fixtures/keys/agent2-key.pem'),\n  cert: fs.readFileSync('test/fixtures/keys/agent2-cert.pem')\n};\n\nhttps.createServer(options, (req, res) => {\n  res.writeHead(200);\n  res.end('hello world\\n');\n}).listen(8000);\n```\n\nIn this example, the `https` module is used to create an HTTPS server. The `fs` module is used to read the SSL certificate and private key from the file system. The server listens on port 8000 and responds with \"hello world\" to all requests.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/core/testing`: for the `TestBed` class\n- `@angular/common/http`: for the `HttpClient` class\n- `@angular/common/http/testing`: for the `HttpClientTestingModule` class\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "24937509-ebb7-33d5-9850-4bc4c73878dd",
              "name": "HTTP Server Usage Vulnerability Due to Lack of TLS Encryption",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"HTTP Server Usage Vulnerability Due to Lack of TLS Encryption\" refers to the situation where an application communicates over an unencrypted HTTP connection, which can expose sensitive information to unauthorized parties. In JavaScript, this can occur when using the HttpClient module to make HTTP requests without using TLS encryption.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the TestBed is configuring a module with the WalletService and HttpClient providers. If these services are used to send HTTP requests without TLS encryption, it could lead to this vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when sending requests. HTTPS is HTTP with encryption. The encryption is provided by Transport Layer Security (TLS). This ensures that the communication between the client and server is encrypted and safe from eavesdropping.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code fix would depend on how the `WalletService` and `HttpClient` are implemented. However, generally, you should ensure that all URLs used in HTTP requests start with `https://` instead of `http://`.\n\nFor example, if the `WalletService` makes a request to a server, it should look like this:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('https://example.com/api/data');\n```\n\nInstead of:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('http://example.com/api/data');\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using Angular's testing utilities. The following libraries are likely required:\n\n- `@angular/core`: for the `TestBed` class.\n- `@angular/common/http`: for the `HttpClient` class.\n- `@angular/common/http/testing`: for the `HttpClientTestingModule` class.\n- The `WalletService` and any dependencies it might have.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6d7c57ac-f43e-3c7b-b961-480f968d7cd1",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This vulnerability can lead to data being intercepted, read, and modified by attackers. In the context of the provided code, if the `HttpClient` is used to send requests to an unencrypted HTTP server, it could expose sensitive user data or application data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much harder for attackers to intercept and read the data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not show the usage of `HttpClient` to send requests, so it's not possible to provide a specific fix. However, when using `HttpClient` to send requests, always use HTTPS URLs. For example:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('https://secure.example.com/data')\n```\n\nInstead of:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('http://insecure.example.com/data')\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/core` for `TestBed` and `NO_ERRORS_SCHEMA`\n- `@angular/material` for `MatCardModule`\n- `@ngx-translate/core` for `TranslateModule` and `TranslateService`\n- `@ngx-gallery/core` for `GalleryModule`\n- The `AboutComponent` and `ConfigurationService` are likely custom modules or services and their dependencies would depend on their implementation.\n- The `provideHttpClient` and `provideHttpClientTesting` are not standard Angular functions and their dependencies would depend on their implementation.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
