            {
              "id": "1700c33e-8d6f-3690-be9b-14393ebc07c4",
              "name": "Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a type of security vulnerability that occurs when a developer does not explicitly specify the fields to be returned in a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data. For instance, if a query is made to fetch user data without specifying the fields, it might return sensitive information like passwords, email addresses, etc. This vulnerability is also known as \"NoSQL Injection\" when dealing with NoSQL databases.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always specify the fields to be returned in a database query. This can be done by using the projection parameter in the query function. Also, it's important to validate and sanitize all user inputs to prevent NoSQL injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming `feedbackService.find()` is a MongoDB query, you should specify the fields to be returned in the query. Here is an example:\n\n```javascript\nthis.feedbackService\n      .find({}, {password: 0, email: 0}) // This will exclude password and email fields from the returned data\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not provide enough context to determine the specific library dependencies. However, if it's a MongoDB query, it might require the `mongodb` or `mongoose` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-943: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Data Query Logic](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-943",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-943"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "22aee00a-f2ff-3a54-aad5-95d12f2d08ba",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks. This is because it directly includes user-controlled data (`feedbacks[i].comment`) in an HTML string without proper sanitization or encoding. An attacker could potentially inject malicious scripts into the `comment` field, which would then be executed in the browser of any user viewing the comment.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always sanitize and/or escape user-controlled data before including it in an HTML context. This can be done using built-in JavaScript functions or third-party libraries designed for this purpose.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix using the built-in `textContent` property, which automatically escapes any HTML characters:\n\n```javascript\nlet commentNode = document.createElement('p');\ncommentNode.style.marginBottom = '0';\ncommentNode.textContent = feedbacks[i].comment;\n\nlet starsNode = document.createElement('div');\nstarsNode.className = 'feedback-stars';\nstarsNode.textContent = `(${this.stars[feedbacks[i].rating]})`;\n\nlet figcaptionNode = document.createElement('figcaption');\nfigcaptionNode.appendChild(commentNode);\nfigcaptionNode.appendChild(starsNode);\n\nfeedbacks[i].comment = figcaptionNode.outerHTML;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not appear to have any library dependencies.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1e6ee1ba-4a5e-3138-8064-44826722153b",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from Bypassing Application Sanitization\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability can occur when an application sanitization process is bypassed. In the JavaScript code snippet provided, the `bypassSecurityTrustHtml` method is used, which can potentially allow malicious scripts to be injected and executed. This is because the method trusts the input as safe HTML, which can be dangerous if the input is not properly sanitized or validated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using methods that bypass security and trust user inputs as safe HTML. Instead, always sanitize and validate user inputs before using them. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `bypassSecurityTrustHtml`, use a method that sanitizes the input. Here is a simple fix:\n\n```javascript\nfeedbacks[i].comment = this.sanitizer.sanitize(SecurityContext.HTML, feedbacks[i].comment);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Angular's DomSanitizer\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "01d5a483-669d-38e9-ba27-4f7294bfc244",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This can lead to data being intercepted, read, and modified by attackers. This vulnerability is particularly dangerous when dealing with sensitive data such as user credentials, personal information, or financial data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for attackers to intercept and read the data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, there is no explicit usage of HTTP or HTTPS. However, if any of the services (ProductService, QuantityService, OrderHistoryService) or the HttpClient are making HTTP requests, they should be updated to use HTTPS. \n\nFor example, if ProductService is making an HTTP request, it might look something like this:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('http://example.com/api/products');\n```\n\nThis should be updated to use HTTPS:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('https://example.com/api/products');\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- @angular/core\n- @angular/platform-browser/animations\n- @angular/material\n- @angular/router/testing\n- @ngx-translate/core\n- ProductService\n- QuantityService\n- OrderHistoryService\n- MatSnackBar\n- HttpClient\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
