            {
              "id": "ee2d2e78-83f0-3e8b-a978-f5b2e5aebc94",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over an unencrypted HTTP connection. This can expose the data to potential interception, manipulation, or theft by attackers. In the context of JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when HTTP requests are made without using secure protocols like HTTPS.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS for all connections, especially when transmitting sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much harder for attackers to read or modify the data. \n\nIn addition, consider using HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), which instructs browsers to only use HTTPS, preventing HTTP connections. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not directly show the usage of HTTP or HTTPS. However, if there is any HTTP request being made in the application, it should be replaced with HTTPS. \n\nFor example, if you are using the `http` module in Node.js to make a request:\n\n```javascript\nconst http = require('http');\n\nhttp.get('http://example.com', (resp) => {\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\nYou should replace it with the `https` module:\n\n```javascript\nconst https = require('https');\n\nhttps.get('https://example.com', (resp) => {\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/router/testing`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `@angular/forms`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `AddressSelectComponent`\n- `AddressComponent`\n- `DeliveryMethodComponent`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
