            {
              "id": "94a71c48-96b0-31be-aae9-61f9e7084165",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This can lead to data being intercepted, read, and modified by attackers. This vulnerability is particularly dangerous when dealing with sensitive data such as user credentials, personal information, or payment details.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much harder for attackers to intercept and read the data. \n\nIn addition, consider using HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), which is a web security policy mechanism that helps to protect websites against protocol downgrade attacks and cookie hijacking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not directly show usage of HTTP or HTTPS. However, if there is any HTTP usage in the `AddressService` or any other services, it should be replaced with HTTPS. \n\nFor example, if there is a method in `AddressService` that makes an HTTP request:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('http://example.com/api/address');\n```\n\nIt should be replaced with:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('https://example.com/api/address');\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/router`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `@angular/forms`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `AddressComponent`\n- `AddressCreateComponent`\n- `AddressService`\n- `TranslateService`\n- `MatSnackBar`\n- `HttpClient`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "abcbe64d-cc74-3461-b54e-24f4cd83ff82",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attack that can occur when unencrypted HTTP servers are used instead of HTTPS. In JavaScript programming, this vulnerability can be exploited when data is transmitted over an insecure network. An attacker can intercept the data and alter it before it reaches its intended destination, potentially leading to unauthorized access or data theft.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always use HTTPS instead of HTTP for data transmission. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it difficult for an attacker to read or modify the data. Additionally, it is also recommended to use HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) which forces the browser to only use secure connections.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not directly deal with HTTP or HTTPS protocols, it's about TestBed configuration. The actual fix would be in the network communication part of your code, which is not provided here. However, here is a general example of how to use HTTPS in JavaScript:\n\n```javascript\nvar https = require('https');\nhttps.get('https://api.example.com/data', (resp) => {\n  let data = '';\n\n  // A chunk of data has been received.\n  resp.on('data', (chunk) => {\n    data += chunk;\n  });\n\n  // The whole response has been received.\n  resp.on('end', () => {\n    console.log(JSON.parse(data));\n  });\n\n}).on(\"error\", (err) => {\n  console.log(\"Error: \" + err.message);\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `MatTableModule`\n- `TranslateModule`\n- `MatDialogModule`\n- `MatPaginatorModule`\n- `MatDividerModule`\n- `MatCardModule`\n- `MatIconModule`\n- `AdministrationComponent`\n- `MatDialog`\n- `UserService`\n- `FeedbackService`\n- `HttpClient`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6a6cf539-ef75-34c6-89b6-af5e9e924693",
              "name": "Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that occurs when a developer does not explicitly specify the fields to be returned in a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data. For instance, if a user object in a database has fields like `password`, `creditCardNumber`, etc., and a query does not specify the fields to return, these sensitive fields could be included in the response.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always explicitly specify the fields to be returned in a database query. This is often referred to as \"projection\". By doing this, developers can ensure that only the necessary data is returned, and sensitive data is not inadvertently exposed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming `userService.find()` is a MongoDB query, you can specify the fields to return in the second argument of the `find()` method. Here's an example:\n\n```javascript\nthis.userService.find({}, { password: 0, creditCardNumber: 0 }).subscribe({\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\nIn this example, `password` and `creditCardNumber` are explicitly excluded from the results of the query.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using RxJS for handling asynchronous data streams. Therefore, the `rxjs` library is required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b1e09132-160e-329b-b470-4e04a3d776e2",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from Bypassing Built-in Sanitization\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability can occur when a developer bypasses built-in sanitization methods in JavaScript. In the provided code snippet, the `bypassSecurityTrustHtml` method is used, which can potentially allow malicious scripts to be injected into the application if the user input is not properly sanitized.\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks are a type of injection, in which malicious scripts are injected into otherwise benign and trusted websites. XSS attacks occur when an attacker uses a web application to send malicious code, generally in the form of a browser side script, to a different end user.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid bypassing the built-in sanitization methods provided by the framework. Instead, always sanitize user inputs before using them in your application. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nuser.email = this.sanitizer.sanitize(`<span class=\"${this.doesUserHaveAnActiveSession(user) ? 'confirmation' : 'error'}\">${user.email}</span>`)\n```\n\nIn the above code, the `sanitize` method is used instead of `bypassSecurityTrustHtml`. This ensures that the user input is properly sanitized before it is used in the application.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the Angular framework, specifically the `@angular/core` package which provides the `DomSanitizer` service.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d6090f0e-e06e-397f-902f-dfcaaba14b8a",
              "name": "Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\" in JavaScript typically occurs when a developer does not explicitly specify the fields to be returned in a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data if the query is used to return results directly to the client. For example, if a user object in the database contains a password field, and a query is made to retrieve user data without specifying the fields, the password field could be included in the response.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always explicitly specify the fields to be returned in a database query. This can be done using the `select` method in most database libraries. Additionally, sensitive data such as passwords should be hashed and not stored in plain text.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming the `find` method is from a MongoDB library like Mongoose, you can specify the fields to return in the query like this:\n\n```javascript\nthis.feedbackService.find({}, 'field1 field2 field3').subscribe({\n```\n\nIn this example, only `field1`, `field2`, and `field3` will be returned in the query.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using a MongoDB library like Mongoose and an Observable library like RxJS. The dependencies would be:\n\n- mongoose\n- rxjs\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "bc12c7f3-c813-365a-8805-2c9bc2069fe9",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from Bypassing Application Sanitization\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the given code snippet, the application is using Angular's DomSanitizer service to bypass security and trust the given value to be safe HTML. This can potentially lead to an XSS vulnerability if the `feedback.comment` is user-controlled and not properly sanitized.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `bypassSecurityTrustHtml` or any other method that bypasses Angular's built-in sanitization. Instead, rely on Angular's automatic sanitization for HTML content.\n\nIf you absolutely need to insert HTML into your application, you should sanitize it server-side before including it in the page. This way, you can ensure that the HTML is safe to be included in your page.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of bypassing Angular's sanitization, you should let Angular automatically sanitize the HTML content:\n\n```javascript\nfeedback.comment = this.sanitizer.sanitize(SecurityContext.HTML, feedback.comment);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Angular (version 2 or above)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0fe146d1-2c0c-3555-be60-f0e503dbc690",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This can lead to data being intercepted, read, and manipulated by attackers. In the context of the provided code, if any sensitive data is being sent or received, it could be vulnerable to this type of attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much harder for attackers to read or manipulate the data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not directly show any HTTP or HTTPS requests, so a specific fix cannot be provided. However, in general, when making HTTP requests in JavaScript, you should ensure to use 'https://' in the URL instead of 'http://'. If you are using a library like axios or fetch to make HTTP requests, they will support HTTPS just by changing the URL.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `@angular/router/testing`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `ngx-cookie`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `ngx-clipboard`\n- `@angular/animations`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic/testing`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
