            {
              "id": "7bd5a18d-df84-38ff-a8f2-55c4ac0a5c2e",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This can lead to the interception of data by malicious actors, who can then use this data for nefarious purposes. This vulnerability is particularly relevant when dealing with sensitive data such as login credentials, personal information, or financial data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) for all connections, especially when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data sent between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for attackers to intercept and read the data.\n\nIn addition, it is also recommended to use HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), which forces browsers to only use HTTPS connections. This can help prevent downgrade attacks, where an attacker tries to force a connection to use unencrypted HTTP instead of HTTPS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not directly show the usage of HTTP or HTTPS, but it is assumed that the `provideHttpClient` function is used to create HTTP connections. To fix this vulnerability, ensure that all connections created by this function use HTTPS. This can usually be done by specifying 'https://' in the URL when making requests.\n\n```javascript\nTestBed.configureTestingModule({\n  imports: [RouterTestingModule.withRoutes([\n    { path: '403', component: ErrorPageComponent }\n  ])],\n  providers: [LoginGuard, provideHttpClient(withInterceptorsFromDi('https://')), provideHttpClientTesting()]\n})\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/core` for `TestBed`\n- `@angular/router/testing` for `RouterTestingModule`\n- `LoginGuard`, `provideHttpClient`, `withInterceptorsFromDi`, `provideHttpClientTesting`, and `ErrorPageComponent` are custom dependencies and their implementation details are not provided.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9184e87c-1113-37b6-a2ca-b44304862ff6",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This vulnerability can lead to exposure of sensitive data to unauthorized parties, as the data can be easily intercepted and read by anyone who has access to the network.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the TestBed is configuring a module with routing and HTTP client services. However, there is no indication of whether the HTTP connections used by these services are encrypted or not. If these services are communicating with a server over an unencrypted HTTP connection, then they are vulnerable to this security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use encrypted connections (HTTPS) when sending sensitive data. This can be achieved by configuring your server to use HTTPS and ensuring that your client-side code connects to the server using the HTTPS protocol.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code snippet does not show how the HTTP client services are used to send requests to a server, so it's not possible to provide a specific source code fix. However, when using the HttpClient service to send requests, always use HTTPS URLs. For example:\n\n```javascript\nthis.httpClient.get('https://my-secure-server.com/data');\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/core` for the `TestBed` and `provide` functions.\n- `@angular/router/testing` for the `RouterTestingModule`.\n- `@angular/common/http` for the `HttpClient` service.\n- Any libraries that `AdminGuard`, `LoginGuard`, `ErrorPageComponent`, `withInterceptorsFromDi()`, and `provideHttpClientTesting()` are part of or depend on.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
