            {
              "id": "5fe170b3-1355-3f23-b5cd-e3a5c10db9c4",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability can occur when user-controlled data is used in an HTML string. In the provided JavaScript code, the user-controlled data is `conf.application.theme`. If an attacker can control this value, they can inject malicious scripts that will be executed in the context of the victim's session. This can lead to various attacks such as session hijacking, identity theft, defacement of the website, and other malicious activities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. **Validate Input**: Ensure that user-controlled data is properly validated. This can be done by using a whitelist of allowed values.\n\n2. **Encode Output**: Ensure that user-controlled data is properly encoded before it is used in an HTML string. This can prevent the browser from interpreting the data as code.\n\n3. **Use Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Implementing a Content Security Policy (CSP) can prevent the execution of inline scripts.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA possible fix for the provided code could be:\n\n```javascript\nlet theme = conf.application.theme;\nif (allowedThemes.includes(theme)) {\n    overlayContainer.getContainerElement().classList.add(theme + '-theme');\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `allowedThemes` is a list of allowed theme values. The user-controlled data is only used if it is in the list of allowed values.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using the following libraries:\n\n1. A DOM manipulation library (like jQuery or vanilla JavaScript) for the `classList.add` method.\n2. A configuration library (like `config` in Node.js) for the `conf.application.theme` value.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f3fd7fe4-a2f6-35d7-be62-2077466040f9",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of unencrypted HTTP servers instead of HTTPS in JavaScript programming. This makes the application susceptible to Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks. In a MitM attack, the attacker intercepts the communication between two parties, alters the communication, and relays it, without the knowledge of the two parties. When an application uses unencrypted HTTP servers, the data transmitted between the client and the server is in plain text, making it easy for an attacker to read and manipulate the data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use HTTPS instead of HTTP. HTTPS encrypts the data transmitted between the client and the server, making it difficult for an attacker to read or manipulate the data. Additionally, it is also recommended to use HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) which forces the browser to use HTTPS connections only, preventing the use of insecure HTTP connections.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe TestBed configuration does not directly deal with HTTP or HTTPS protocols. The protocol used is typically defined in the API endpoints used in the services. Therefore, to fix this vulnerability, you should ensure that all API endpoints used in your services are HTTPS and not HTTP.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `@angular/router/testing`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- `@angular/forms`\n- `@angular/material/form-field`\n- `@angular/material/input`\n- `@angular/material/card`\n- `@angular/material/table`\n- `@angular/material/button`\n- `@angular/material/expansion`\n- `@angular/material/dialog`\n- `@angular/material/button-toggle`\n- `@angular/material/snack-bar`\n- `BasketComponent`\n- `PurchaseBasketComponent`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1bbeb5f1-fdfb-36a6-85c9-0b9f3fa39ae2",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of unencrypted HTTP servers instead of HTTPS in JavaScript programming. This makes the application susceptible to Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks. In a MitM attack, the attacker intercepts the communication between two parties, alters the communication, and relays it, without the knowledge of the two parties. When an application uses unencrypted HTTP servers, the data transmitted between the client and the server is in plain text, making it easy for an attacker to read and manipulate the data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use HTTPS instead of HTTP. HTTPS encrypts the data transmitted between the client and the server, making it difficult for an attacker to read or manipulate the data. Additionally, it is also recommended to use HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) which forces the browser to only use HTTPS, preventing downgrade attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe TestBed configuration does not directly deal with HTTP or HTTPS. The actual HTTP or HTTPS calls would be made in the services provided in the TestBed configuration. Therefore, the fix would be in those services. For example, if `SocketIoService` is making HTTP calls, it should be changed to HTTPS.\n\n```javascript\n// Before\nthis.http.get('http://example.com');\n\n// After\nthis.http.get('https://example.com');\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `ngx-cookie`\n- `ngx-clipboard`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `socket.io-client`\n- `rxjs`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
