            {
              "id": "d21e0c36-e1e6-3605-8607-29e7d252e365",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" is a common vulnerability in JavaScript programming language. It occurs when a Promise is rejected but there is no error handling routine to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes and potential security risks. \n\nIn the provided code, the Promise returned by the `import` function is not handled properly. If the module `../../confetti` fails to load for any reason (e.g., network error, file not found, etc.), the Promise will be rejected and the error will not be caught, leading to an Unhandled Promise Rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always include error handling routines for Promises. This can be done using `.catch()` or by using `try/catch` with async/await. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nimport('../../confetti')\n  .then(module => {\n    module.shootConfetti()\n  })\n  .catch(error => {\n    console.error('Failed to load module: ', error);\n  });\n```\n\nIn this version, if the Promise is rejected, the error will be caught and logged to the console.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not explicitly require any library dependencies. However, it assumes that there is a module at `../../confetti` that exports a `shootConfetti` function.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are subject to change and may not be accessible in the future."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fade4350-9f56-3f75-86db-deccb0c90e2e",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of unencrypted HTTP servers instead of HTTPS in JavaScript programming. This makes the application susceptible to Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks. In a MitM attack, the attacker intercepts the communication between two parties, alters the communication, and relays it, without the knowledge of the two parties. This can lead to unauthorized access to sensitive data, data corruption, or even denial of service.\n\nWhen an application uses unencrypted HTTP servers, the data transmitted between the client and the server is in plain text. This makes it easy for an attacker to intercept and read the data. On the other hand, HTTPS uses SSL/TLS encryption to secure the data during transmission, making it difficult for an attacker to read the data even if they manage to intercept it.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS instead of HTTP for data transmission. This ensures that the data is encrypted during transmission, making it difficult for an attacker to read the data even if they manage to intercept it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe TestBed configuration does not directly deal with HTTP or HTTPS protocols. The protocol used is typically defined in the service that makes the HTTP requests. Therefore, you should check your services and ensure that they are using HTTPS for all requests.\n\nFor example, if you are using Angular's HttpClient, ensure that the URLs you are using start with 'https://' instead of 'http://'.\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('https://my-secure-server.com/data');\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `@ngx-translate/http-loader`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f75bd54e-2fd3-3195-b03c-69b9e19440b1",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, crashes, and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the Promise returned by `firstValueFrom(this.translate.get('CHALLENGE_SOLVED', { challenge: challenge.challenge }))` is not caught and handled properly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always include a `.catch()` block when working with Promises to handle any potential errors that may occur. This allows you to control the application flow even when an error occurs, preventing unexpected behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nfirstValueFrom(this.translate.get('CHALLENGE_SOLVED', { challenge: challenge.challenge }))\n  .then((message) => {\n    let country\n    if (this.showCtfCountryDetailsInNotifications && this.showCtfCountryDetailsInNotifications !== 'none') {\n      country = this.countryMap[challenge.key]\n    }\n    this.notifications.push({\n      message,\n      key: challenge.key,\n      flag: challenge.flag,\n      country,\n      copied: false\n    })\n    this.ref.detectChanges()\n  })\n  .catch((error) => {\n    console.error('An error occurred:', error);\n  });\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to require the following libraries:\n\n- An implementation of the `firstValueFrom` function, which is a part of the `rxjs` library.\n- A translation library that provides the `translate.get` function. This could be `@ngx-translate/core` or a similar library.\n- A change detection library that provides the `ref.detectChanges` function. This is likely Angular's `ChangeDetectorRef`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f1b6ecb1-40e6-3ef6-8ab1-519e29cc6f35",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This vulnerability can lead to exposure of sensitive data such as user credentials, personal information, or other confidential data. Attackers can easily intercept unencrypted data transmitted over HTTP, leading to potential data breaches.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP for transmitting sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it difficult for attackers to read the data even if they manage to intercept it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, there is no explicit usage of HTTP or HTTPS. However, if there is any HTTP usage in the `UserService` or `provideHttpClient` methods, it should be replaced with HTTPS. \n\nFor example, if `UserService` is making HTTP requests, they should be changed to HTTPS. If the base URL is defined in the `UserService`, it should look like this:\n\n```javascript\nconst BASE_URL = 'https://example.com';\n```\n\nInstead of:\n\n```javascript\nconst BASE_URL = 'http://example.com';\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/core` for `TestBed`\n- `@angular/forms` for `ReactiveFormsModule`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations` for `BrowserAnimationsModule`\n- `@angular/material` for `MatCardModule`, `MatFormFieldModule`, `MatInputModule`, `MatButtonModule`\n- `@ngx-translate/core` for `TranslateModule`\n- `UserService` (custom service)\n- `provideHttpClient` and `provideHttpClientTesting` (custom providers)\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9cf76486-cea8-3625-99dd-3f8f9a3d5183",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of unencrypted HTTP servers instead of HTTPS in JavaScript programming. This makes the application susceptible to Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks. In a MitM attack, the attacker intercepts the communication between two parties, alters the communication, and relays it, without either party knowing that they are not communicating with each other directly. When using unencrypted HTTP, the data is sent as plain text, making it easy for an attacker to read and modify the data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use HTTPS instead of HTTP. HTTPS encrypts the data sent between the client and the server, making it difficult for an attacker to read or modify the data. Additionally, it is also recommended to use HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) to ensure that the browser only communicates with the server using a secure HTTPS connection.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe specific fix would depend on the server-side technology being used. However, in general, you would need to configure your server to use HTTPS and redirect all HTTP requests to HTTPS. Here is an example of how you might do this using Express.js:\n\n```javascript\nvar express = require('express');\nvar http = require('http');\nvar https = require('https');\nvar app = express();\n\napp.use (function (req, res, next) {\n    if (req.secure) {\n        next();\n    } else {\n        res.redirect('https://' + req.headers.host + req.url);\n    }\n});\n\nhttp.createServer(app).listen(80);\nhttps.createServer(options, app).listen(443);\n```\n\nIn this example, `options` would be an object containing your SSL certificate information.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example provided seems to be a part of a larger Angular application. The libraries required by this code to execute properly would include:\n\n- `@angular/forms` for `ReactiveFormsModule`\n- `ngx-cookie` for `CookieModule`\n- `@ngx-translate/core` for `TranslateModule`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations` for `BrowserAnimationsModule`\n- `@angular/material` for `MatCardModule`, `MatFormFieldModule`, `MatInputModule`, and `MatButtonModule`\n- The custom `ChatbotComponent` and `ChatbotService`\n- The custom `TranslateService`\n- The custom `provideHttpClient` and `provideHttpClientTesting` functions\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3e16a4ce-9a8e-3def-a8a2-670b66f5b848",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Timing Attacks in String Comparisons with '===', '!==', '!=' and '==' \"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn JavaScript, the `===`, `!==`, `!=` and `==` operators are used for string comparisons. However, these operators are vulnerable to timing attacks because they compare strings character by character and stop as soon as they find a difference. This means that an attacker can guess the string one character at a time by measuring how long the comparison takes. This is particularly dangerous when these operators are used to compare sensitive data, such as passwords.\n\nIn the provided code example, the `!==` operator is used to compare two passwords. This makes the code vulnerable to a timing attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use a constant-time string comparison function. This function should always take the same amount of time to compare two strings, regardless of their content. This makes it impossible for an attacker to guess the string based on the time it takes to compare it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a constant-time string comparison function that you can use to fix the vulnerability in the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nfunction constantTimeStringCompare(a, b) {\n    if (a.length !== b.length) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    let result = 0;\n    for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {\n        result |= a.charCodeAt(i) ^ b.charCodeAt(i);\n    }\n    return result === 0;\n}\n\nif (!constantTimeStringCompare(password, passwordRepeat)) {\n    return { notSame: true }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-208: Observable Timing Discrepancy](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/208.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-208",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/208.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-208"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fcacce0a-2890-3311-96b4-63141c1d9552",
              "name": "\"Bracket Notation Object Injection Vulnerability at lineNumber - 1\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Bracket Notation Object Injection Vulnerability at lineNumber - 1\" is a type of security vulnerability in JavaScript that occurs when user-supplied input is used to construct object property accessors using bracket notation. This can lead to arbitrary property access or modification, potentially leading to unauthorized information disclosure, data corruption, or even remote code execution.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `lineNumber` variable is used to access an element in the `lineMarkers` array. If an attacker can control the value of `lineNumber`, they could potentially access or modify any property of the `this` object.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize user-supplied input before using it to access object properties. This can be done using a whitelist of allowed property names, or by ensuring that the input is of the correct type and within the expected range.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn this specific case, you should ensure that `lineNumber` is a positive integer and within the range of valid indices for the `lineMarkers` array. Here is a possible fix:\n\n```javascript\nif (Number.isInteger(lineNumber) && lineNumber > 0 && lineNumber <= this.lineMarkers.length) {\n  const marker = this.lineMarkers[lineNumber - 1];\n} else {\n  throw new Error('Invalid line number');\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not appear to require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Injection Theory](https://owasp.org/www-community/Injection_Theory)\n- [CWE-74: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Output Used by a Downstream Component ('Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/74.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-74",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/74.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-74"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "dc9b9074-67ff-390a-95ac-267f14a7eb5b",
              "name": "Unfiltered Database Query Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnfiltered Database Query Vulnerability, also known as NoSQL Injection, is a type of security vulnerability that occurs when an application doesn't properly validate or escape input before using it in a database query. This can allow an attacker to manipulate the query, potentially leading to unauthorized data access, data corruption, or even data loss.\n\nIn JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when using NoSQL databases like MongoDB. The provided code example is vulnerable because it directly uses user-supplied input in a database query without any form of validation or escaping:\n\n```javascript\nthis.differ = this.differs.find({}).create()\n```\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize user-supplied input before using it in a database query. This can be done using input validation libraries or built-in database features.\n\nIn addition, you should use parameterized queries or prepared statements whenever possible. These types of queries separate the data from the query itself, making it impossible for an attacker to manipulate the query structure.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's how you might fix the provided code example using input validation and parameterized queries:\n\n```javascript\nlet userInput = getUserInput(); // This should be the actual user input\nlet sanitizedInput = sanitize(userInput); // Use a proper sanitization function here\n\nthis.differ = this.differs.find(sanitizedInput).create()\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example seems to be using the `differs` object from the `@angular/core` package, so you would need to have that package installed in your project.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-943: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Data Query Logic](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-943",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-943"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f2c85148-45c6-35b5-bd9b-c5b2a55ae803",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attack that can occur when unencrypted HTTP servers are used instead of HTTPS. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can be exploited when data is transmitted over an insecure HTTP connection, allowing an attacker to intercept, read, and modify the data in transit. This can lead to unauthorized access to sensitive information, data corruption, or even system compromise.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP for data transmission. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it difficult for an attacker to read or modify the data. \n\nIn addition, consider implementing HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), which forces browsers to only use HTTPS, even if the application tries to use HTTP. This can prevent attackers from downgrading the connection to HTTP.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not directly show the use of HTTP or HTTPS, as it is a TestBed configuration. However, if there is any HTTP client service or any other service that makes HTTP requests, it should be updated to use HTTPS. \n\nFor example, if you have a service that uses HttpClient to make requests, ensure that the URLs start with 'https://' instead of 'http://'. \n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('https://example.com/api/data');\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/animations`\n- `ngx-cookie`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/common/http/testing`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b5ecf877-a6cc-36bc-ab8d-b9e24254dd20",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" is a common vulnerability in JavaScript programming language. It occurs when a Promise is rejected but there is no error handling routine to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes and potential security risks. \n\nIn the provided code, the Promise returned by the dynamic import statement is not handled for rejection. If the module import fails for any reason (e.g., the module does not exist, network error, etc.), the Promise will be rejected and the error will not be caught or handled, leading to an Unhandled Promise Rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always handle Promise rejections. This can be done by attaching a `.catch()` block to the Promise chain, or by using `try/catch` with async/await. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nimport('../../confetti')\n  .then(module => {\n    module.shootConfetti()\n  })\n  .catch(error => {\n    console.error('Error importing module:', error);\n  });\n```\n\nIn this version, if the Promise is rejected, the error will be caught and logged to the console.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not explicitly require any library dependencies. However, it assumes the existence of a module at `'../../confetti'` that exports a `shootConfetti` function.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ad812a10-4900-3ed9-b37a-ddf9935ea839",
              "name": "\"Crypto.pseudoRandomBytes()/Math.random() exhibits cryptographic weakness.\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `Math.random()` function in JavaScript is not suitable for generating cryptographic secrets. The values produced by `Math.random()` are predictable enough to be guessed and exploited by an attacker, which can lead to various security vulnerabilities such as information disclosure, session prediction, and other forms of attacks.\n\nThe `crypto.pseudoRandomBytes()` function in Node.js is also not suitable for generating cryptographic secrets. It generates pseudo-random bytes, but these are not cryptographically strong. This means that the output can be predicted to some extent, which is a security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use `crypto.randomBytes()` for generating cryptographic secrets in Node.js. This function generates cryptographically strong pseudo-random data that can be used for creating secure random tokens, keys, etc.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nconst crypto = require('crypto');\n\n.map((fix, index) => ({ fix, index, sort: crypto.randomBytes(1)[0]/256 }))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe fixed code requires the `crypto` built-in module in Node.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-338",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-338"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8e03de94-eee8-3a0f-ba09-55cfcd741c32",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability described here is related to the use of unencrypted HTTP servers instead of HTTPS in JavaScript programming. This can expose the application to Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks. In a MitM attack, the attacker intercepts the communication between two parties, alters the communication, and gains unauthorized access to sensitive information.\n\nIn the context of JavaScript programming, if the application is using HTTP servers for communication, the data transmitted between the client and the server is not encrypted and can be easily intercepted by attackers. This can lead to data breaches and unauthorized access to sensitive information.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use HTTPS servers instead of HTTP. HTTPS ensures that the data transmitted between the client and the server is encrypted and cannot be easily intercepted by attackers. \n\nIn addition, it is also recommended to implement HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) in the application. HSTS is a web security policy mechanism that helps to protect websites against protocol downgrade attacks and cookie hijacking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe TestBed configuration does not directly deal with HTTP or HTTPS protocols. The protocol used is typically defined in the service that makes the HTTP requests. Therefore, you should ensure that all your services are using HTTPS for communication. \n\nFor example, if you have a service that makes HTTP requests:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('http://example.com/data');\n```\n\nYou should change it to use HTTPS:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('https://example.com/data');\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/forms`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `UserService`\n- `MatSnackBar`\n- `FeedbackService`\n- `CaptchaService`\n- `TranslateService`\n- `provideHttpClient`\n- `provideHttpClientTesting`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b8d16881-7dba-3882-8eb6-ad6e1ed74896",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over an unencrypted HTTP connection. This can lead to the interception of data by malicious actors, leading to data breaches and other security issues. \n\nIn the provided code, the vulnerability isn't directly visible, but it could exist if the `HttpClient` used in `provideHttpClient(withInterceptorsFromDi())` and `provideHttpClientTesting()` is configured to make requests over unencrypted HTTP.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much harder for attackers to intercept and understand the data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nEnsure that all API endpoints used by the `HttpClient` are served over HTTPS. If you have control over the server, configure it to redirect all HTTP requests to HTTPS. If you're using a third-party API, make sure to use the HTTPS version of the API.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/forms` for `ReactiveFormsModule`\n- `ng2-file-upload` for `FileUploadModule`\n- `@ngx-translate/core` for `TranslateModule`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations` for `BrowserAnimationsModule`\n- `@angular/material/card` for `MatCardModule`\n- `@angular/material/form-field` for `MatFormFieldModule`\n- `@angular/material/input` for `MatInputModule`\n- `@angular/material/button` for `MatButtonModule`\n- The `ComplaintComponent` and services (`UserService`, `ComplaintService`, `TranslateService`) are likely custom and would need to be provided.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "544e3df8-1544-3def-8756-1b48152aca9f",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from Bypassing Application Sanitization\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is bypassing the Angular built-in sanitization function and trusting the HTML content from `data.image`. This can potentially lead to an XSS vulnerability if `data.image` contains malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Input Validation**: Validate input on arrival, make sure it's of the expected format. Use a secure allowlist of acceptable inputs.\n\n2. **Output Encoding**: Use libraries and templates that automatically provide this, especially for untrusted data.\n\n3. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Implementing CSP makes it harder for an attacker to inject malicious content.\n\n4. **Avoid Bypassing Sanitization**: Angular provides built-in sanitization for different contexts (HTML, style, URLs, resource URLs). Avoid bypassing these sanitizers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of bypassing the sanitizer, use the Angular built-in sanitizer to sanitize the HTML content:\n\n```javascript\nimport { DomSanitizer, SafeHtml } from '@angular/platform-browser';\n\nconstructor(private sanitizer: DomSanitizer) { }\n\nsanitizeImage(data: any): SafeHtml {\n  return this.sanitizer.sanitize(SecurityContext.HTML, data.image);\n}\n\nthis.captcha = this.sanitizeImage(data);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- Angular\n- @angular/platform-browser\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b4752ddc-0f76-3788-a27c-5e4596db577e",
              "name": "HTTP Server Usage Vulnerability Due to Lack of TLS Encryption",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"HTTP Server Usage Vulnerability Due to Lack of TLS Encryption\" refers to the situation where an application communicates over an unencrypted HTTP connection, which can expose sensitive information to unauthorized parties. In JavaScript, this can occur when using the HttpClient module to make HTTP requests without using TLS encryption.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when making requests. HTTPS is the secure version of HTTP, where the 'S' stands for 'Secure'. It means all communications between your browser and the website are encrypted. HTTPS is often used to protect highly confidential online transactions like online banking and online shopping order forms.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the TestBed configuration, ensure that all HTTP requests are made over HTTPS. This can be done by using the HttpClient module to make HTTP requests over HTTPS. Here is an example:\n\n```javascript\nimport { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';\n\nconstructor(private http: HttpClient) { }\n\ngetSecureData() {\n  return this.http.get('https://securewebsite.com/data');\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/forms`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `ImageCaptchaService`\n- `DataSubjectService`\n- `DataExportComponent`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d5dd1a79-5a84-3307-a924-c63916b39654",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code snippet, the application is vulnerable to XSS because it's directly embedding user-controlled data (`value`) into an HTML string without any sanitization or encoding.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape untrusted data based on the HTML context (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) that the data will be placed into. In JavaScript, you can use functions like `encodeURIComponent()`, `encodeURI()`, or `escape()` to encode user-controlled data.\n\n## Source Code Fix\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided code snippet using `encodeURIComponent()` function:\n\n```javascript\nreturn `${encodeURIComponent(value)}?`\n```\n\nThis will ensure that any special characters in `value` are properly encoded, preventing any potential XSS attacks.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6c8c1ba2-297c-3e6c-9671-a4731c8c8c94",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe usage of unencrypted HTTP servers vulnerability in JavaScript refers to the risk of transmitting sensitive data over an unencrypted connection. This can lead to the interception of the data by malicious actors, leading to data breaches and other security issues. In the provided code, the TestBed is configured with a route to a 'payment/shop' component, which suggests that sensitive payment information could be transmitted. If this is done over an unencrypted HTTP connection, it could be vulnerable to interception.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when transmitting sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for a third party to intercept and understand the data. \n\nIn addition, consider using HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), which is a web security policy mechanism that helps to protect websites against protocol downgrade attacks and cookie hijacking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code provided does not show the actual HTTP request, but assuming it is done using Angular's HttpClient, the fix would be to ensure that the URL used starts with 'https://' instead of 'http://'. \n\nFor example, if the PaymentComponent makes a request to an API like this:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('http://api.example.com/payment/shop')\n```\n\nIt should be changed to:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('https://api.example.com/payment/shop')\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- @angular/core\n- @angular/router\n- @angular/platform-browser/animations\n- @angular/forms\n- @angular/material\n- @ngx-translate/core\n- AddressService (custom service)\n- DeliveryService (custom service)\n- HttpClient and HttpClientTestingModule from @angular/common/http\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "50ac0d66-eb3f-35ad-b3fa-3e4b0b8e8df5",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of unencrypted HTTP servers instead of HTTPS in JavaScript programming. This can expose the application to Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks. In a MitM attack, the attacker intercepts the communication between two parties, alters the communication, and relays it, without the knowledge of the two parties. When unencrypted HTTP is used, the data transmitted is in plain text, making it easy for an attacker to read and manipulate the data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use HTTPS instead of HTTP. HTTPS encrypts the data transmitted between the client and the server, making it difficult for an attacker to read or manipulate the data. \n\nIn addition, it is also recommended to use HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) which forces the browser to use HTTPS connections only, preventing any attempt to use unencrypted connections.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not directly show the use of HTTP or HTTPS. However, if the application is using HTTP, it should be changed to HTTPS. This is usually done in the server configuration or in the code where the server URL is specified.\n\nIf the server URL is specified in the code, it should look something like this:\n\n```javascript\nconst serverUrl = 'https://myserver.com';\n```\n\nInstead of:\n\n```javascript\nconst serverUrl = 'http://myserver.com';\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `@angular/router/testing`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `@angular/forms`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `ngx-cookie-service`\n- `@angular/common/http/testing`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "430ad9cf-6600-38de-aad5-3bd82fbaa842",
              "name": "Checking Snap Interaction with Ethereum Object through `ethereum.request` in Endowment: Ethereum Provider",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `ethereum.request` method is a general-purpose method for making RPC requests to the Ethereum network. If not properly validated, it can lead to a vulnerability where an attacker can manipulate the Ethereum object and perform unauthorized actions. This is a form of Injection vulnerability, where untrusted data is sent to an interpreter as part of a command or query.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to validate and sanitize all inputs that are passed to the `ethereum.request` method. Avoid using user-supplied input directly in the method without proper validation and sanitization. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how you can validate and sanitize inputs before passing them to the `ethereum.request` method:\n\n```javascript\nconst method = userSuppliedInput.method;\nconst params = userSuppliedInput.params;\n\nif (validateMethod(method) && validateParams(params)) {\n    await ethereum.request({ method, params });\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, `validateMethod` and `validateParams` are functions that you would implement to validate and sanitize the method and params inputs respectively.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `ethereum`: This is the Ethereum object injected by MetaMask into the browser context.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Input Validation Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-74: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Output Used by a Downstream Component ('Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/74.html)\n- [CWE-116: Improper Encoding or Escaping of Output](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/116.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-74",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/74.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-116",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/116.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-74",
                  "CWE-116"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "395728b4-caff-3e51-9ef2-5888d49a358e",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability can occur when user-controlled data is used in an HTML string. In the provided JavaScript code, the user-controlled data is `conf.application.theme`. If an attacker can control this value, they can inject malicious scripts that will be executed in the context of the victim's session. This can lead to various attacks such as session hijacking, identity theft, defacement of the website, and other malicious activities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. **Validate Input**: Ensure that user-controlled data is properly validated. This can be done by using a whitelist of allowed values.\n\n2. **Encode Output**: Ensure that user-controlled data is properly encoded before it is used in an HTML string. This can prevent the browser from interpreting the data as code.\n\n3. **Use Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Implementing a Content Security Policy (CSP) can prevent the execution of inline scripts.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA possible fix for the provided code could be:\n\n```javascript\nlet theme = conf.application.theme;\nif (allowedThemes.includes(theme)) {\n    overlayContainer.getContainerElement().classList.add(theme + '-theme');\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `allowedThemes` is a list of allowed theme values. The user-controlled data is only used if it is in the list of allowed values.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using the following libraries:\n\n1. A DOM manipulation library (like jQuery or vanilla JavaScript) for the `classList.add` method.\n2. A configuration library (like `config` in Node.js) for the `conf.application.theme` value.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a22670da-56da-3922-b3fc-70e572cec812",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attack that can occur when unencrypted HTTP servers are used instead of HTTPS. In JavaScript programming, this vulnerability can be exploited when sensitive data is transmitted over an insecure connection. An attacker can intercept the data and potentially modify it before it reaches its intended destination.\n\nIn the provided code, the TestBed is configuring a module with various imports and providers. If any of these services communicate with a server over HTTP, they could be vulnerable to MitM attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS for any server communication. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for an attacker to intercept and read the data. \n\nIn addition, consider implementing HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), which forces browsers to only use HTTPS, even if the application tries to use HTTP. This can prevent attacks that attempt to downgrade the connection from HTTPS to HTTP.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not show any explicit use of HTTP or HTTPS, so it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, if any of the services (like `SecurityQuestionService` or `UserService`) are using HTTP to communicate with a server, they should be modified to use HTTPS instead.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/forms`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `ngx-translate/core`\n- `SecurityQuestionService` (custom service)\n- `UserService` (custom service)\n- `provideHttpClient` (custom function)\n- `provideHttpClientTesting` (custom function)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4f769b1b-67c5-36df-8a89-5de9ce5e7fac",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This can lead to data being intercepted, read, and modified by attackers. This vulnerability is particularly dangerous when dealing with sensitive data such as user credentials, personal information, or financial data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for attackers to intercept and read the data.\n\nIn addition, consider using HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), which is a web security policy mechanism that helps to protect websites against protocol downgrade attacks and cookie hijacking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not directly show the usage of HTTP or HTTPS. However, if it is using HTTP, you should switch to HTTPS. The exact changes depend on how the HTTP client is configured. Here is a general example:\n\n```javascript\n// Before\nhttp.get('http://example.com');\n\n// After\nhttps.get('https://example.com');\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `@angular/common/http/testing`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8e2fa0c7-dd2b-3a33-abcd-9670e1c1abd6",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from Bypassing Application Sanitization\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is using a sanitizer to bypass security and trust the HTML that is being input. This can potentially lead to an XSS vulnerability if the `payload.data.lastLoginIp` is controlled by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, it is recommended to:\n\n- Use frameworks that automatically escape XSS by design, such as the latest Ruby on Rails, React JS.\n- Escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) will resolve Reflected and Stored XSS vulnerabilities.\n- Apply context-sensitive encoding when modifying the browser document on the client side acts against DOM XSS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, you should not trust the HTML input. Instead, you should sanitize the input. Here is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nthis.lastLoginIp = this.sanitizer.sanitize(SecurityContext.HTML, `<small>${payload.data.lastLoginIp}</small>`);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using Angular's DomSanitizer, so the library dependencies required would be:\n\n- @angular/platform-browser\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "129b1a9b-5cd1-3e69-98da-01bb4b327924",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Timing Attacks in String Comparisons with '===', '!==', '!=' and '==' \"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn JavaScript, the `===`, `!==`, `!=` and `==` operators are used for string comparisons. However, these operators are vulnerable to timing attacks because they compare strings character by character and stop as soon as they find a difference. This means that an attacker can guess the string one character at a time by measuring how long the comparison takes. This is particularly dangerous when these operators are used to compare sensitive data, such as passwords.\n\nIn the provided code example, the `!==` operator is used to compare two passwords. This makes the code vulnerable to a timing attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use a constant-time string comparison function. This function should always take the same amount of time to compare two strings, regardless of their content. This makes it impossible for an attacker to guess the string based on the time it takes to compare it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a constant-time string comparison function that you can use to fix the vulnerability in the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nfunction constantTimeStringCompare(a, b) {\n    if (a.length !== b.length) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    let result = 0;\n    for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {\n        result |= a.charCodeAt(i) ^ b.charCodeAt(i);\n    }\n    return result === 0;\n}\n\nif (!constantTimeStringCompare(password, passwordRepeat)) {\n    return { notSame: true }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-208: Observable Timing Discrepancy](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/208.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-208",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/208.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-208"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b76663f3-52e3-37c5-b690-30033ca3d15c",
              "name": "HTTP Server Usage Vulnerability Due to Lack of TLS Encryption",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"HTTP Server Usage Vulnerability Due to Lack of TLS Encryption\" refers to the situation where an application communicates sensitive information over an unencrypted HTTP connection. This can expose the data to potential interception, manipulation, or theft. In JavaScript, this can occur when using the HTTP module to create a server without implementing Transport Layer Security (TLS) encryption.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use HTTPS instead of HTTP for all communications, especially when transmitting sensitive data. HTTPS is HTTP with encryption. The encryption is provided by TLS (Transport Layer Security). \n\nIn JavaScript, you can use the HTTPS module to create a server with TLS encryption. You will need to provide a private key and a certificate when creating the server.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to create an HTTPS server in JavaScript:\n\n```javascript\nconst https = require('https');\nconst fs = require('fs');\n\nconst options = {\n  key: fs.readFileSync('test/fixtures/keys/agent2-key.pem'),\n  cert: fs.readFileSync('test/fixtures/keys/agent2-cert.pem')\n};\n\nhttps.createServer(options, (req, res) => {\n  res.writeHead(200);\n  res.end('hello world\\n');\n}).listen(8000);\n```\n\nIn this example, the server is created with a private key and a certificate, which are used to establish the TLS encryption.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example provided does not directly relate to the vulnerability discussed. However, it seems to be a part of a larger Angular application and requires the following libraries to execute properly:\n\n- `@angular/router`\n- `@angular/forms`\n- `ngx-cookie`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `UserService` (custom service)\n- `WindowRefService` (custom service)\n- `CookieService` (custom service)\n- `HttpClient` (custom service)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "372ce0a3-2fc2-3142-aded-f5e93f916e29",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This can lead to data being intercepted, read, and modified by attackers. This vulnerability is particularly dangerous when dealing with sensitive data such as user credentials, personal information, or confidential business data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP for any communication that involves sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for attackers to intercept and read the data.\n\nIn addition, consider using HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), which is a web security policy mechanism that helps to protect websites against protocol downgrade attacks and cookie hijacking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not directly show usage of HTTP or HTTPS, as it is configuring TestBed. However, if there is any HTTP communication happening in the services (AdministrationService, ConfigurationService, UserService, ChallengeService, CookieService, SocketIoService), it should be changed to HTTPS.\n\nFor example, if you are using HttpClient to make HTTP requests in any of these services, you should change the URL from 'http://' to 'https://'.\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('https://example.com/api/data');\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- @angular/router\n- ngx-cookie\n- @ngx-translate/core\n- @angular/platform-browser/animations\n- @angular/material\n- socket.io-client\n- @angular/common/http\n- @angular/core/testing\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
