            {
              "id": "dd3ed4f9-5d15-35a0-a2ee-16b7f7f45ac2",
              "name": "Google OAuth URL Vulnerability Detected",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Google OAuth URL Vulnerability Detected\" vulnerability in JavaScript refers to a situation where an attacker can exploit the Google OAuth URL to gain unauthorized access to sensitive data. This can occur when the client ID is publicly exposed in the code, as shown in the provided code snippet. This vulnerability can lead to serious security breaches, including unauthorized access to user data and potential data loss.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Never expose your client ID or any other sensitive data in your code. These should be stored in environment variables or secure vaults.\n2. Always validate and sanitize inputs to prevent Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks.\n3. Implement proper error handling to prevent information leakage.\n4. Use secure communication protocols like HTTPS to protect data in transit.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of hardcoding the client ID in your code, store it in an environment variable. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```javascript\n// Store your client ID in an environment variable\nprocess.env.GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID = 'your-client-id';\n\n// Then, you can access it in your code like this\npublic clientId = process.env.GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not provide enough context to determine the specific library dependencies required for it to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 Security Risks](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
