            {
              "id": "35cff64d-8c48-343b-8ce7-b75d1cc42bd8",
              "name": "\"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that occurs when a developer does not explicitly specify the fields to be returned from a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data. In the provided code, the `find` method is used to query the database without specifying the fields to be returned. This means that all fields associated with the 'Score Board' will be returned, potentially including sensitive data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always specify the fields to be returned from a database query. This can be done by passing an object to the `find` method that specifies the fields to be returned. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nthis.challengeService.find({ name: 'Score Board' }, { fields: { name: 1 } }).subscribe({\n```\n\nIn this fixed version, the `find` method is passed an object that specifies that only the 'name' field should be returned. This ensures that no sensitive data is exposed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to use the following libraries:\n\n- A database library that provides the `find` method. This could be a library like Mongoose for MongoDB.\n- An Observable library that provides the `subscribe` method. This is likely RxJS.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Query Parameterization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Query_Parameterization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-209",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-209"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "093d1a58-0f50-3f31-8fcd-565994c458a2",
              "name": "XSS Vulnerability in User-Controlled HTML Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the given code snippet, the application is using template literals to include user-controlled data (`version`) in HTML. If the `version` variable can be controlled by an attacker, it could lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Use frameworks that automatically escape XSS by design, such as Ruby on Rails, React JS, etc.\n- Escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) will resolve Reflected and Stored XSS vulnerabilities.\n- Apply context-sensitive encoding when modifying the browser document on the client side acts against DOM XSS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the given code snippet, you should ensure that the `version` variable is properly escaped before it is included in the HTML. Here is a simple fix using the `encodeURI` function:\n\n```javascript\nthis.version = `v${encodeURI(version)}`;\n```\n\nThis will ensure that any special characters in the `version` variable are properly escaped, preventing them from being interpreted as code.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe given code snippet does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a7ec205f-e754-3b49-ae80-028eb76916e0",
              "name": "Potential Open Redirect Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nAn Open Redirect Vulnerability, also known as Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards, is a type of web application security vulnerability that enables an attacker to redirect a user to a malicious website of their choice. This is typically achieved by manipulating the URL in a way that causes the web application to redirect the user to an untrusted site. In JavaScript, this can occur when the `window.location.replace()` function is used with user-controlled input.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `window.location.replace()` function is used to redirect the user to a new page. The URL for the new page is constructed by concatenating a host server address stored in `environment.hostServer` with the string '/profile'. If an attacker can control the value of `environment.hostServer`, they can potentially redirect the user to a malicious site.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using user-controlled input to construct URLs without proper validation and sanitization. If redirection is necessary, use a whitelist of trusted URLs to redirect to, and do not allow redirection to any other URLs.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\n// Define a list of trusted hosts\nconst trustedHosts = ['https://trustedhost1.com', 'https://trustedhost2.com'];\n\n// Check if the host server is in the list of trusted hosts\nif (trustedHosts.includes(environment.hostServer)) {\n  window.location.replace(environment.hostServer + '/profile');\n} else {\n  // Handle untrusted host server\n  console.error('Untrusted host server:', environment.hostServer);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not require any additional library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Unvalidated_Redirects_and_Forwards_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-601",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-601"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5bd755b0-6954-3c4c-81fe-42b2b8ffed86",
              "name": "Potential Open Redirect Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nAn Open Redirect Vulnerability, also known as Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards, is a type of web application security vulnerability that enables an attacker to redirect a user to a malicious website of their choice. This is typically achieved by manipulating the URL in a way that causes the web application to redirect the user to an untrusted site. In JavaScript, this can occur when user-supplied input is used to construct a URL for redirection without proper validation.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is using the `window.location.replace()` function to redirect the user to a new URL. The URL is constructed by concatenating a host server address (`environment.hostServer`) and a path (`/dataerasure`). If an attacker can control the `environment.hostServer` value, they can potentially redirect users to a malicious site.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using user-supplied input to construct URLs without proper validation and sanitization. If redirection is necessary, use a whitelist of trusted URLs and only redirect to those. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\n// Define a list of trusted hosts\nconst trustedHosts = ['https://trustedhost1.com', 'https://trustedhost2.com'];\n\n// Check if the host server is in the trusted list\nif (trustedHosts.includes(environment.hostServer)) {\n    window.location.replace(environment.hostServer + '/dataerasure');\n} else {\n    // Handle untrusted host server\n    console.error('Untrusted host server:', environment.hostServer);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not require any additional library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Unvalidated_Redirects_and_Forwards_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-601",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-601"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eeae6445-d057-3f57-a102-5cf6c566d10e",
              "name": "Usage of HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability arises when an application uses HTTP servers instead of HTTPS servers. HTTP is not secure, and information sent over HTTP can be intercepted by attackers. This is particularly dangerous if sensitive information such as login credentials or personal data is being transmitted. In contrast, HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for attackers to intercept and understand the data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS instead of HTTP, especially when transmitting sensitive data. This ensures that the data is encrypted and cannot be easily intercepted by attackers. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, there is no explicit usage of HTTP or HTTPS. However, if there is any part of the application that is using HTTP, it should be replaced with HTTPS. For example, if there is a line of code that looks like this:\n\n```javascript\nhttp.get('http://example.com/data')\n```\n\nIt should be replaced with:\n\n```javascript\nhttp.get('https://example.com/data')\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/router`\n- `@angular/forms`\n- `ngx-cookie`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `OAuthComponent`\n- `LoginComponent`\n- `UserService`\n- `provideHttpClient`\n- `provideHttpClientTesting`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ebc91f5a-ba46-3dc1-a209-23357d62e28a",
              "name": "Detected Hardcoded Secret Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Hardcoded Secret Vulnerability\" is a security flaw that occurs when sensitive data, such as passwords, API keys, or cryptographic key material, is embedded directly into the source code. This is a bad practice because it exposes the secret to anyone who has access to the source code, and it makes it difficult to change the secret without modifying and redeploying the application.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the password and passwordRepeat fields are hardcoded as Base64-encoded strings. This is a security risk because anyone who can view the source code can also view and decode these secrets.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid hardcoding secrets directly into your source code. Instead, use secure methods of storing and retrieving secrets, such as environment variables, secure configuration files, or secret management services.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's how you might modify the provided code to remove the hardcoded secrets:\n\n```javascript\nlet password = process.env.USER_PASSWORD;\nlet passwordRepeat = process.env.USER_PASSWORD_REPEAT;\n\nexpect(userService.save).toHaveBeenCalledWith({ email: 'test@test.com', password: password, passwordRepeat: passwordRepeat })\n```\n\nIn this version of the code, the password and passwordRepeat values are retrieved from environment variables, which are set outside of the application and can be securely managed and changed without modifying the application code.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to be a Jest test, so the following libraries would be required:\n\n- Jest\n- Any libraries required by the `userService.save` function\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP: Password Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not always be accessible. Always refer to the latest OWASP and CWE guidelines for the most up-to-date information."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-798",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-798"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c863c873-f7fb-3079-89d5-d1ed5c147e7e",
              "name": "\"Hardcoded Secret Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nHardcoded Secret Vulnerability refers to the practice of embedding sensitive information, such as passwords, encryption keys, and database connection strings, directly into the source code. This is a security risk because the secret is exposed to anyone who has access to the source code. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when developers hardcode secrets into their scripts.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the password is hardcoded as a Base64 encoded string. This is a security risk because anyone who has access to the source code can easily decode the password.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid hardcoding secrets into your source code. Instead, use secure methods of storing and retrieving secrets, such as environment variables, secure configuration files, or secret management services.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of hardcoding the password, retrieve it from a secure source at runtime. Here's an example of how you might do this using environment variables:\n\n```javascript\nexpect(userService.login).toHaveBeenCalledWith({ \n    email: 'test@test.com', \n    password: process.env.USER_PASSWORD, \n    oauth: true \n})\n```\n\nIn this example, `USER_PASSWORD` is an environment variable that contains the password. You would set this environment variable in a secure way outside of your source code.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example appears to be using the Jasmine testing framework, which provides the `expect` function and the `toHaveBeenCalledWith` matcher.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Hardcoded Secrets](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html#hardcoded-secrets)\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-798",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-798"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e1112350-f8ec-39db-9e07-0f8d49243f29",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This can lead to data being intercepted, read, and manipulated by attackers. This vulnerability is particularly dangerous when dealing with sensitive data such as user credentials, personal information, or payment details.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much harder for attackers to intercept and read the data. \n\nIn addition, consider using HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), which forces browsers to only use HTTPS, even if the application tries to use HTTP. This can help protect against downgrade attacks, where an attacker tries to force the connection to use HTTP instead of HTTPS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not directly show the usage of HTTP or HTTPS. However, if the services provided (like `TrackOrderService`, `BasketService`, etc.) are using HTTP to communicate with the server, they should be updated to use HTTPS. \n\nFor example, if `TrackOrderService` is using Angular's `HttpClient` to make HTTP requests, it should be updated to use HTTPS:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('https://example.com/api/trackOrder');\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/router/testing`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `OrderCompletionComponent` (custom component)\n- `TrackOrderService` (custom service)\n- `ActivatedRoute` (from `@angular/router`)\n- `BasketService` (custom service)\n- `ConfigurationService` (custom service)\n- `AddressService` (custom service)\n- `provideHttpClient` (custom function)\n- `provideHttpClientTesting` (custom function)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f02b1121-6187-3afb-a3ad-3043e8c07f99",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. The \"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\" vulnerability in JavaScript refers to a situation where user-controlled data is directly included in an HTML string without proper sanitization or encoding. This can allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts into the web page, which can then be executed in the context of the victim's browser.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `tweet` variable is directly included in the returned string. If `tweet` contains user-controlled data, this could potentially lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this type of vulnerability, you should always properly sanitize and/or encode user-controlled data before including it in an HTML string. This can be done using various methods, such as HTML encoding, JavaScript encoding, or using safe APIs.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA simple fix for the provided code snippet could be to use the `textContent` property to safely include the `tweet` variable in the HTML string. This would look something like this:\n\n```javascript\nlet tweetElement = document.createElement('span');\ntweetElement.textContent = tweet.substring(0, maxLength);\nreturn `${tweetElement.innerHTML}${showDots ? '...' : ''}`;\n```\n\nThis code creates a new HTML element and sets its `textContent` property to the `tweet` variable. The `textContent` property automatically encodes any HTML special characters, preventing any potential XSS attacks.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not appear to have any library dependencies.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP: Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a09605ee-2a13-311b-8256-e492d8644b99",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attack that can occur when unencrypted HTTP servers are used instead of HTTPS. In a MitM attack, the attacker intercepts the communication between two parties, alters the communication, and relays it, without the knowledge of the two parties. When unencrypted HTTP servers are used, the data transmitted is not secure and can be easily intercepted by attackers. This is a common vulnerability in JavaScript programming language.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use HTTPS instead of HTTP. HTTPS encrypts the data transmitted between the client and the server, making it difficult for attackers to intercept and alter the data. Additionally, it is also recommended to use HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) which forces the browser to use HTTPS connections only.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe TestBed configuration does not directly deal with HTTP or HTTPS protocols. The protocol used is determined by the server and the client making the request. However, you can ensure that all requests made from the client are over HTTPS by configuring your server to redirect all HTTP requests to HTTPS.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `@angular/router/testing`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `OrderHistoryComponent` (Assuming it's a custom component)\n- `ProductService` (Assuming it's a custom service)\n- `OrderHistoryService` (Assuming it's a custom service)\n- `MatDialog` (Assuming it's a custom dialog)\n- `provideHttpClient` (Assuming it's a custom function)\n- `provideHttpClientTesting` (Assuming it's a custom function)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "862b6734-17c9-3171-a91d-d85ba78c7f36",
              "name": "Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `redirectUrl` is constructed using a template string that includes `orderId`, which could potentially be user-controlled data. If `orderId` is not properly sanitized and can contain arbitrary user input, this could lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape untrusted HTTP request data, even when the variable is included in a URL. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix using the `encodeURIComponent` function to ensure that the `orderId` is properly URL-encoded:\n\n```javascript\nconst orderIdSafe = encodeURIComponent(orderId);\nconst redirectUrl = `${this.basketService.hostServer}/ftp/order_${orderIdSafe}.pdf`;\n```\n\nThis code ensures that any special characters in `orderId` are properly escaped, preventing any potential XSS attacks.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not appear to have any specific library dependencies.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "dc4f51b4-345b-3fe7-b247-28a6eecf82e4",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attack that can occur when unencrypted HTTP servers are used instead of HTTPS. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can be exploited when data is transmitted over an insecure network. An attacker can intercept the data and alter it before it reaches its intended destination, potentially leading to unauthorized access or data theft.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use HTTPS instead of HTTP. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it difficult for an attacker to read or modify the data. Additionally, it is also recommended to use HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) which forces the browser to use HTTPS connections only, preventing any attempt to connect via an insecure HTTP connection.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe TestBed configuration does not directly deal with HTTP or HTTPS protocols. The protocol used is typically defined in the service that makes the HTTP requests. Therefore, you should ensure that all your services are using HTTPS for their requests. \n\nFor example, if you have a service that makes HTTP requests:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('http://example.com/data');\n```\n\nYou should change it to use HTTPS:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('https://example.com/data');\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `@angular/router/testing`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- `@angular/forms`\n- `@angular/material/input`\n- `@angular/material/card`\n- `@angular/material/table`\n- `@angular/material/button`\n- `@angular/material/button-toggle`\n- `@angular/material/icon`\n- `@angular/material/tooltip`\n- `@angular/material/snack-bar`\n- `BasketService`\n- `AddressService`\n- `PaymentService`\n- `DeliveryService`\n- `DeluxeGuard`\n- `MatSnackBar`\n- `provideHttpClient`\n- `provideHttpClientTesting`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b6145a40-07d6-3598-b445-976b1424f134",
              "name": "\"Plain Text Hardcoded Password Detected: Secure in Environment Variable\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Plain Text Hardcoded Password Detected: Secure in Environment Variable\" is a security vulnerability that occurs when sensitive data, such as passwords, are hardcoded into the application's source code. This is a serious issue because anyone who has access to the source code can easily read or change the password. This vulnerability is especially dangerous in JavaScript, where the source code is often easily accessible to end users.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nThe best way to mitigate this vulnerability is to avoid hardcoding sensitive data into your application's source code. Instead, you should store such data in a secure location, such as environment variables, and access them programmatically. This way, even if someone gains access to your source code, they won't be able to see or change your sensitive data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of hardcoding the password into your source code, you should store it in an environment variable and access it programmatically. Here's how you can do it in JavaScript:\n\n```javascript\nprocess.env['PASSWORD'] = 'your-password'; // Set the password\nlet password = process.env['PASSWORD']; // Get the password\n```\n\nIn this example, `process.env` is a global variable in Node.js that allows you to access your environment's variables. You can set the password in your environment's variables and then access it in your code using `process.env`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example provided does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Password Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-259: Use of Hard-coded Password](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/259.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-259",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/259.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-259"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ed873a1e-18e3-3016-b9d7-4b6c8d21ccf4",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of unencrypted HTTP servers instead of HTTPS in JavaScript programming. This makes the application susceptible to Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks. In a MitM attack, the attacker intercepts the communication between two parties, alters the communication, and gains unauthorized access to sensitive information.\n\nIn the provided code, the TestBed is configuring a module with various imports and providers. However, it doesn't specify whether the HTTP communication is encrypted or not. If the HTTP communication is unencrypted, it could lead to a MitM attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS instead of HTTP for all communications. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it difficult for an attacker to read or modify the data that is being transmitted.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code does not show any explicit use of HTTP or HTTPS. However, if there is any HTTP communication happening in the `PaymentService` or `TranslateService`, it should be replaced with HTTPS. \n\nFor example, if you are using Angular's `HttpClient` for HTTP requests in these services, you should always use HTTPS URLs. Here is an example:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('https://example.com/api/data');\n```\n\nInstead of:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('http://example.com/api/data');\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/forms`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `PaymentMethodComponent` (custom component)\n- `PaymentService` (custom service)\n- `TranslateService` (custom service)\n- `MatSnackBar` (custom service)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
