            {
              "id": "b23a57de-73c6-3e29-98ea-8fd99de29e20",
              "name": "Usage of HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability arises when an application uses HTTP servers instead of HTTPS servers. HTTP is not secure, and any data sent between the user and the server can be intercepted. This can lead to sensitive information being exposed, such as usernames, passwords, and credit card numbers.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the TestBed is configuring a module with various components and services. However, it does not specify whether these services are using HTTP or HTTPS. If any of these services are using HTTP, it could potentially expose sensitive user data.\n\n## Mitigation\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS instead of HTTP. HTTPS encrypts the data sent between the user and the server, making it much more difficult for an attacker to intercept and read the data.\n\n## Code Fix\n\nThe code fix would depend on the specific services being used. If a service is using HTTP, it should be changed to use HTTPS. For example, if the `BasketService` was using HTTP, it could be changed to use HTTPS like so:\n\n```javascript\nconst basketService = new BasketService('https://example.com');\n```\n\n## Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/router`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `@angular/forms`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `@angular/common/http/testing`\n- `ngx-cookie-service`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d1c0223e-0e66-35ec-b8b9-e8053ed8930a",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This can lead to the interception of data by malicious actors, leading to data breaches and other security issues. This vulnerability is particularly relevant when dealing with sensitive data such as user credentials, personal information, or financial data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always use encrypted connections (HTTPS) when transmitting sensitive data. HTTPS ensures that the data is encrypted during transmission, making it much more difficult for attackers to intercept and read the data.\n\nIn addition, it is also recommended to use HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) which is a web security policy mechanism that helps to protect websites against protocol downgrade attacks and cookie hijacking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not show any HTTP requests, so it's not possible to provide a specific fix. However, in general, when making HTTP requests in JavaScript, you should ensure to use 'https://' in the URL instead of 'http://'. \n\nFor example, if you are using the `fetch` API to make a request, it should look like this:\n\n```javascript\nfetch('https://example.com/data', {\n  method: 'GET',\n})\n.then(response => response.json())\n.then(data => console.log(data));\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
