            {
              "id": "1ba8a680-9997-385b-a4ab-b139b033f8fb",
              "name": "\"Bracket Notation Object Injection Vulnerability through this.couponControl.value\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Bracket Notation Object Injection Vulnerability through this.couponControl.value\" is a type of security vulnerability in JavaScript that occurs when user input is used directly to access object properties using bracket notation. This can allow an attacker to inject properties into the object, potentially leading to unauthorized access or other security issues.\n\nIn the provided code example, `this.couponControl.value` is used directly to access a property of `this.campaigns`. If `this.couponControl.value` is user-controlled and not properly sanitized, an attacker could potentially manipulate the value to access arbitrary properties of `this.campaigns`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it's important to validate and sanitize all user input before using it to access object properties. This can be done using a whitelist of allowed property names, or by using a function that checks the property name against a list of allowed values before accessing it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's a simple fix for the provided code example:\n\n```javascript\nconst allowedCampaigns = ['campaign1', 'campaign2', 'campaign3']; // replace with your actual campaign names\nconst campaignName = this.couponControl.value;\n\nif (allowedCampaigns.includes(campaignName)) {\n  const campaign = this.campaigns[campaignName];\n} else {\n  // handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first define a whitelist of allowed campaign names. Then, before accessing the property, we check if the provided campaign name is in the whitelist. If it is, we proceed with accessing the property. If it's not, we handle the error.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example does not appear to require any specific library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: Injection](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A1_2017-Injection)\n- [CWE-74: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Output Used by a Downstream Component ('Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/74.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are subject to change and may not be accessible at all times."
              },
              "properties": {
