            {
              "id": "65170eb8-83b0-3fce-8bd1-ba2e37d30c9d",
              "name": "SQL Injection Vulnerability from Untrusted Input and Raw SQL Query Concatenation",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSQL Injection Vulnerability from Untrusted Input and Raw SQL Query Concatenation is a common security vulnerability in web applications. It occurs when an application includes untrusted data in a SQL query without proper validation or escaping, allowing an attacker to manipulate the query, leading to unauthorized data access, data corruption, or even data loss.\n\nIn JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when using libraries or frameworks that allow raw SQL queries, such as Node.js with a MySQL or PostgreSQL database. The specific vulnerability sink in the provided code is not directly related to SQL injection, as it seems to be part of a test setup using Angular's testing utilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate SQL Injection vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n1. Use Prepared Statements or Parameterized Queries: These methods ensure that the parameters passed into SQL queries are treated strictly as data, not part of the SQL command.\n\n2. Use a Safe API: Some APIs provide a higher level of abstraction and do not allow the use of raw SQL, thus preventing SQL injection.\n\n3. Least Privilege: Limit the permissions of the database account used by the web application. Don't use the database root account.\n\n4. Input Validation: Validate input data thoroughly. Use a whitelist of accepted characters, or a blacklist of dangerous characters.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAs the provided code does not seem to be directly related to SQL injection, a specific fix cannot be provided. However, here is a general example of how to use parameterized queries with the `pg-promise` library in Node.js:\n\n```javascript\nconst db = require('pg-promise')();\n\ndb.any('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = $1', [userInput])\n  .then(data => {\n    console.log(data);\n  })\n  .catch(error => {\n    console.log(error);\n  });\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be part of an Angular test setup, so the required libraries would likely include:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/platform-browser`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
