                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSQL Injection is a code injection technique that attackers can use to exploit vulnerabilities in a web application's database layer. Specifically, it involves the injection of malicious SQL statements into an entry field for execution. \n\nIn JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when untrusted input is used in a raw SQL query concatenation. This allows an attacker to manipulate the SQL query to perform unauthorized actions, such as viewing, modifying, or deleting data.\n\nThe provided code example does not directly involve SQL queries or database interaction, but it does involve handling user input, which can be a source of SQL Injection if not properly sanitized before use in a database query.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate SQL Injection vulnerabilities, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Use Prepared Statements (Parameterized Queries): This ensures that the parameters (user-supplied inputs) are separate from the query, preventing attackers from manipulating the query.\n\n2. Use Stored Procedures: These are defined and stored in the database itself, and called from the application.\n\n3. Whitelist Input Validation: This involves checking user inputs against a set of defined rules for length, type, syntax, etc.\n\n4. Least Privilege: Ensure that database accounts used by web applications have the least privileges necessary.\n\n5. Error Handling: Do not reveal error details to users, which could provide clues to attackers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAs the provided code does not directly involve SQL queries, a specific fix cannot be provided. However, if this user input were to be used in a SQL query, it should be properly sanitized or parameterized.\n\nFor example, using parameterized queries with a library like `pg-promise` would look like this:\n\n```javascript\nconst db = require('pg-promise')();\n\n// ...\n\ndb.any('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = $1', [userInput])\n  .then(data => {\n    // handle data\n  })\n  .catch(error => {\n    // handle error\n  });\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example appears to be using Angular's testing utilities, so the following dependencies are likely required:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/platform-browser`\n\nIf the code were to be modified to interact with a SQL database as suggested in the fix recommendation, a library like `pg-promise` would also be required.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
