                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSQL Injection Vulnerability from Untrusted Input and Raw SQL Query Concatenation is a common security vulnerability in web applications. It occurs when an application uses untrusted data to build a SQL query without properly sanitizing or escaping it. This allows an attacker to manipulate the query, leading to unauthorized data access, data corruption, or even data loss.\n\nIn JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when using libraries or frameworks that allow direct execution of SQL queries, such as Node.js with SQLite or MySQL libraries.\n\nThe provided code snippet doesn't directly show this vulnerability, as it's a part of Angular testing suite and doesn't involve SQL queries. However, if the `fixture.debugElement.query(By.css('div.review-text'))` part is used to get user input that is then concatenated into a SQL query, it could potentially lead to this vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n- Never build SQL queries by concatenating user input. Always use parameterized queries or prepared statements.\n- Validate and sanitize user input. Use a whitelist of allowed input, not a blacklist of disallowed input.\n- Limit the privileges of the database account used by the application. It should only have the minimum necessary privileges.\n- Regularly update and patch your database management system to fix any known vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAs the provided code doesn't directly involve SQL queries, a specific fix can't be provided. However, here's an example of how to use parameterized queries with the `mysql` library in Node.js:\n\n```javascript\nvar mysql = require('mysql');\nvar connection = mysql.createConnection({\n  host     : 'localhost',\n  user     : 'me',\n  password : 'secret',\n  database : 'my_db'\n});\n\nconnection.connect();\n\nlet userProvidedValue = \"userInput\"; // This should be sanitized and validated\nconnection.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [userProvidedValue], function (error, results, fields) {\n  if (error) throw error;\n  console.log(results);\n});\n\nconnection.end();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet is part of an Angular test, and would require the following libraries:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/platform-browser`\n\nIf it's part of a larger application that uses SQL queries, additional libraries such as `mysql` or `sqlite3` might be required.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
