              "id": "b32ab054-e015-3e0e-93c4-e9aba93eab52",
              "name": "Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is logging a string that includes user-controlled data (`review._id`). If this data is not properly sanitized, it could lead to a potential XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (HTML body, HTML attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) will land. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nconsole.log('Liked ' + encodeURIComponent(review._id));\n```\n\nIn this fix, `encodeURIComponent` is used to encode the user-controlled data, which will neutralize any potentially malicious scripts that could be injected through `review._id`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0ec58e63-c2a1-339f-8757-8e3a4336fd4f",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attack that can occur when unencrypted HTTP servers are used instead of HTTPS. In JavaScript programming, this vulnerability can be exploited when sensitive data is transmitted over an unencrypted connection. An attacker can intercept the data and potentially modify it before it reaches its intended destination.\n\nIn the provided code, the TestBed is configuring a module with various imports and providers. If any of these services communicate with a server over HTTP, they could be vulnerable to MitM attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS for any server communication. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for an attacker to intercept and read the data. \n\nIn addition, consider using HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), which forces the browser to only use HTTPS, even if the server initially tries to use HTTP. This can prevent downgrade attacks, where an attacker forces the connection to use HTTP instead of HTTPS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not show any explicit use of HTTP or HTTPS, so it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, if any of the services (TranslateService, BasketService, etc.) are using HTTP to communicate with a server, they should be modified to use HTTPS instead.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- `@angular/forms`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `BasketService`\n- `UserService`\n- `DeluxeGuard`\n- `HttpClient`\n- `HttpClientTestingModule`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7445e1aa-1bde-3616-96a8-45f59e501248",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attack that can occur when unencrypted HTTP servers are used instead of HTTPS. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can be exploited when data is transmitted over an insecure network. An attacker can intercept the data and alter it before it reaches its intended destination. This can lead to unauthorized access, data theft, and other security breaches.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always use HTTPS instead of HTTP. HTTPS encrypts the data that is sent between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for an attacker to intercept and alter the data. Additionally, it is also recommended to use HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) which forces the browser to only use secure connections.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not directly show the use of HTTP or HTTPS, as it is a TestBed configuration for Angular testing. However, if there is any HTTP communication happening in the services (RecycleService, UserService, ConfigurationService), it should be replaced with HTTPS.\n\nFor example, if you have a service that makes HTTP requests:\n\n```javascript\nimport { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';\n\n@Injectable()\nexport class UserService {\n  constructor(private http: HttpClient) { }\n\n  getUser() {\n    return this.http.get('http://example.com/user');\n  }\n}\n```\n\nYou should replace 'http://' with 'https://':\n\n```javascript\nimport { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';\n\n@Injectable()\nexport class UserService {\n  constructor(private http: HttpClient) { }\n\n  getUser() {\n    return this.http.get('https://example.com/user');\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- @angular/core\n- @angular/common/http\n- @angular/forms\n- @angular/material\n- @angular/platform-browser/animations\n- @angular/router/testing\n- @ngx-translate/core\n- TestBed from @angular/core/testing\n- RecycleComponent, AddressComponent (custom components)\n- RecycleService, UserService, ConfigurationService, TranslateService, MatSnackBar (custom services)\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-300: Channel Accessible by Non-Endpoint ('Man-in-the-Middle')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/300.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-300",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/300.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-300"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d7eaff90-4645-3591-a5b4-7769d79ba8f0",
              "name": "XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is using a template literal to construct a string that includes user-controlled data (`config.application.recyclePage.topProductImage`). If an attacker can control the `topProductImage` value, they could potentially inject malicious scripts, leading to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Use frameworks that automatically escape XSS by design, such as Ruby on Rails, React JS, etc.\n- Escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL).\n- Apply context-sensitive encoding when modifying the browser document on the client side.\n- Validate, filter, or sanitize user-generated inputs.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn this specific case, you should sanitize the `topProductImage` value before using it in the template literal. Here's an example using the DOMPurify library:\n\n```javascript\nimport DOMPurify from 'dompurify';\n\nlet sanitizedTopProductImage = DOMPurify.sanitize(config.application.recyclePage.topProductImage);\nthis.topImage = `assets/public/images/products/${sanitizedTopProductImage}`;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- DOMPurify: A XSS sanitizer for HTML, MathML and SVG.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
