            {
              "id": "fa3144aa-131b-3fd9-8f50-ab12ff3cb1e9",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability can occur when user-controlled data is used in an HTML string. In the provided JavaScript code, the `bottomProductImage` property of the `config.application.recyclePage` object is directly embedded into an HTML string. If an attacker can control the value of `bottomProductImage`, they could inject malicious scripts that would be executed when the HTML string is rendered in a web browser.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always sanitize user-controlled data before using it in an HTML string. This can be done by using a library that provides HTML encoding functions, such as the `he` library for JavaScript.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using the `he` library:\n\n```javascript\nconst he = require('he');\n\nthis.bottomImage = `assets/public/images/products/${he.encode(config.application.recyclePage.bottomProductImage)}`;\n```\n\nIn this version of the code, the `he.encode` function is used to HTML encode the `bottomProductImage` property. This ensures that any potentially harmful characters are safely encoded.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe fixed version of the code requires the `he` library. You can install it using npm:\n\n```\nnpm install he\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "dd0af62d-beb6-3224-992f-0ab24ec14e68",
              "name": "Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that occurs when a developer does not specify the fields to be returned in a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data. For instance, if a query is made to fetch user data without specifying the fields, the returned data might include sensitive information such as passwords, which should not be exposed.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always specify the fields to be returned in a database query. This is also known as \"projection\" in MongoDB. By doing this, only the necessary data is returned, reducing the risk of exposing sensitive information.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming the `find()` function is from a MongoDB library, you should specify the fields to be returned in the query. Here is an example:\n\n```javascript\nthis.recycleService.find({}, { password: 0 }).subscribe({\n  // rest of the code\n});\n```\n\nIn the above code, `{ password: 0 }` is the projection. It tells MongoDB to return all fields except the password field.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using a MongoDB library for Node.js, such as Mongoose, and an Observable library such as RxJS. Here are the dependencies:\n\n- mongoose: MongoDB object modeling tool designed to work in an asynchronous environment.\n- rxjs: A library for composing asynchronous and event-based programs by using observable sequences.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Node.js Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "36ca103f-5f77-3581-bb79-ae2cacb2ab37",
              "name": "Unfiltered Database Query Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnfiltered Database Query Vulnerability, also known as NoSQL Injection, is a type of security vulnerability that occurs when an application's database query is constructed using user-supplied data without proper sanitization or filtering. This can allow an attacker to manipulate the query, leading to unauthorized data access, data corruption, or even data loss.\n\nIn JavaScript, this vulnerability is particularly relevant when using NoSQL databases like MongoDB, which use JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) for queries. If user-supplied data is directly used in a query without proper validation, an attacker can inject malicious code into the query.\n\nIn the provided sink `this.findAll()`, if the method `findAll` is used to fetch data from a database without any filtering or validation, it can lead to this vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. **Validate Input:** Always validate user-supplied data before using it in a query. This can be done using built-in JavaScript functions or third-party libraries.\n\n2. **Use Parameterized Queries:** Instead of constructing queries manually, use parameterized queries or prepared statements. These methods automatically handle escaping and quoting of parameters, preventing injection attacks.\n\n3. **Limit Database Permissions:** Limit the permissions of the database user used by the application. This can limit the potential damage in case of an injection attack.\n\n4. **Use a Database Firewall:** A database firewall can detect and block suspicious queries, providing an additional layer of protection.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming `findAll` is a method of a Sequelize Model, a safer version of the method would look like this:\n\n```javascript\nconst { Op } = require('sequelize');\n\nthis.findAll({\n  where: {\n    userSuppliedField: {\n      [Op.eq]: sanitizedUserInput\n    }\n  }\n});\n```\n\nIn this example, `sanitizedUserInput` should be the user-supplied data after it has been properly sanitized. The `Op.eq` operator ensures that only an exact match will be returned, preventing injection attacks.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `sequelize` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-943: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Data Query Logic](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-943",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-943"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9ee90231-92d0-3f58-a0a2-30dac4a04b5d",
              "name": "\"Plain Text Hardcoded Password Detected: Secure in Environment Variable\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Plain Text Hardcoded Password Detected: Secure in Environment Variable\" is a security vulnerability that occurs when sensitive data, such as passwords, are hardcoded into the source code. This is a common issue in JavaScript and other programming languages. Hardcoding passwords in plain text can lead to serious security risks as it exposes the sensitive data to anyone who has access to the source code. This can lead to unauthorized access, data breaches, and other security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nThe best way to mitigate this vulnerability is to avoid hardcoding passwords in the source code. Instead, sensitive data should be stored in environment variables or secure configuration files that are not included in the source code repository. This ensures that the sensitive data is not exposed to unauthorized users.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of hardcoding the password in the source code, you should store it in an environment variable. Here is how you can do it in JavaScript:\n\n```javascript\nlet password = process.env.PASSWORD;\n```\n\nIn this code, `process.env.PASSWORD` is an environment variable that stores the password. You can set the value of this environment variable in your server's configuration.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017 Category A2: Broken Authentication](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A2_2017-Broken_Authentication)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-259: Use of Hard-coded Password](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/259.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-259",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/259.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-259"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0f9f6abe-91a9-33ec-9bef-a2d031939eef",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This can lead to data being intercepted, read, and modified by attackers. In the context of the provided code, if any of the services (SecurityAnswerService, SecurityQuestionService, UserService) are communicating with a server over HTTP, they are potentially exposing sensitive data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much harder for attackers to read or modify the data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not directly show any HTTP or HTTPS requests, so it's not possible to provide a specific code fix. However, if any of the services are making HTTP requests, they should be changed to HTTPS. For example, if the UserService is making an HTTP request like this:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('http://example.com/user');\n```\n\nIt should be changed to:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('https://example.com/user');\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- @angular/router (for RouterTestingModule)\n- @angular/forms (for ReactiveFormsModule)\n- @angular/platform-browser/animations (for BrowserAnimationsModule)\n- @angular/material (for MatCardModule, MatFormFieldModule, MatCheckboxModule, MatInputModule, MatSelectModule, MatButtonModule, MatIconModule, MatSnackBarModule, MatTooltipModule, MatSlideToggleModule)\n- @ngx-translate/core (for TranslateModule)\n- The services (SecurityAnswerService, SecurityQuestionService, UserService) and their dependencies are not specified.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "22bbd398-e9d5-36ed-a233-4aa99cc0a199",
              "name": "\"Plain Text Hardcoded Password Detected: Secure in Environment Variable\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Plain Text Hardcoded Password Detected: Secure in Environment Variable\" is a security vulnerability that occurs when sensitive data, such as a password, is hardcoded into a JavaScript application. This is a serious issue because anyone who has access to the source code can easily read the password. This can lead to unauthorized access to systems or data, and potential data breaches.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, never hardcode sensitive data into your application. Instead, use environment variables to store this data. Environment variables are a type of dynamic-named value that can affect the way running processes behave on a computer. They are part of the environment in which a process runs.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of hardcoding the password into your application, you should store it in an environment variable. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```javascript\nconst password = process.env.PASSWORD;\n```\n\nIn this example, `PASSWORD` is an environment variable that you need to set in your environment before running your application. You can set it in your shell like this:\n\n```bash\nexport PASSWORD=yourpassword\n```\n\nOr you can set it in your application's environment configuration.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-259: Use of Hard-coded Password](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/259.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-259",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/259.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-259"
                ]
              }
