            {
              "id": "28c6051b-6677-3fd2-a1a3-2ad3ab673dea",
              "name": "\"Hardcoded Credentials Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nHardcoded credentials vulnerability refers to the practice of embedding plaintext credentials directly into the source code. This is a security risk as it exposes sensitive information, such as usernames, passwords, and keys, to anyone who has access to the source code. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when credentials are hardcoded into a script, as shown in the provided code example.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid hardcoding credentials directly into your source code. Instead, use environment variables, configuration files, or secure vaults to store sensitive information. These methods allow you to separate your credentials from your source code, making it harder for unauthorized users to gain access to them.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of hardcoding the password directly into your script, you can use an environment variable to store it. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```javascript\nconst password = process.env.PASSWORD;\n```\n\nIn this example, `PASSWORD` is an environment variable that stores your password. You can set this variable in your environment before running your script.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Hardcoded Password](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html#hardcoded-password)\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-798",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-798"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2065300e-60b5-33d9-8983-dff4e7607f97",
              "name": "\"Hardcoded Credentials Vulnerability Detected\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nHardcoded credentials vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when sensitive data such as usernames, passwords, and security questions/answers are embedded directly in the source code. This is a serious security risk as it exposes these credentials to anyone who has access to the code, making the system vulnerable to unauthorized access and data breaches.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid hardcoding sensitive data in your source code. Instead, use environment variables, secure configuration files, or secure databases to store this information. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of hardcoding the user's credentials, you can use environment variables to store this sensitive information. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```javascript\nconst user = { \n    email: process.env.USER_EMAIL, \n    password: process.env.USER_PASSWORD, \n    passwordRepeat: process.env.USER_PASSWORD_REPEAT, \n    securityQuestion: { id: 1, question: process.env.SECURITY_QUESTION }, \n    securityAnswer: process.env.SECURITY_ANSWER \n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `process.env` is a global variable injected by Node.js at runtime for your application to use and it represents the state of the system environment your application is in when it starts. \n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017 Category A2: Broken Authentication](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A2_2017-Broken_Authentication)\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-798",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-798"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "65b303fc-2e68-3fc3-85b8-61cc0f742719",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Timing Attacks in String Comparisons with '===', '!==', '!=' and '==' \"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn JavaScript, the `===`, `!==`, `!=` and `==` operators are used for string comparisons. However, these operators are vulnerable to timing attacks because they compare strings character by character and stop as soon as they find a difference. This means that an attacker can guess the string one character at a time by measuring how long the comparison takes. This is particularly dangerous when these operators are used to compare sensitive data, such as passwords.\n\nIn the provided code example, the `!==` operator is used to compare two passwords. This makes the code vulnerable to a timing attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use a constant-time string comparison function. This function should always take the same amount of time to compare two strings, regardless of their content. This makes it impossible for an attacker to guess the string based on the time it takes to compare it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a constant-time string comparison function that you can use to fix the vulnerability in the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nfunction constantTimeStringCompare(a, b) {\n    if (a.length !== b.length) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    let result = 0;\n    for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {\n        result |= a.charCodeAt(i) ^ b.charCodeAt(i);\n    }\n    return result === 0;\n}\n\nif (!constantTimeStringCompare(password, passwordRepeat)) {\n    return { notSame: true }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-208: Observable Timing Discrepancy](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/208.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-208",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/208.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-208"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eaf24d17-a42e-3c7d-976c-78b51d8f0aac",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This can lead to data being intercepted, read, and modified by attackers. This vulnerability is particularly dangerous when dealing with sensitive data such as user credentials, personal information, or financial data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when transmitting sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for attackers to intercept and read the data. \n\nIn addition, consider using HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), which instructs browsers to only use HTTPS, even if the application tries to use HTTP. This can help protect against downgrade attacks, where an attacker tries to force a connection to use HTTP instead of HTTPS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not directly show usage of HTTP or HTTPS, as it is a TestBed configuration for testing Angular components. However, if the application is using HTTP to make requests, you should switch to using HTTPS. In Angular, this can be done by changing the base URL in your `HttpClient` configuration.\n\nFor example, if your current base URL is `http://example.com`, change it to `https://example.com`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/common`\n- `@angular/forms`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `@angular/router/testing`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `@angular/common/http/testing`\n- Custom components: `SavedAddressComponent`, `AddressComponent`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b58f6a86-9a73-389d-af71-34fb6443fa0f",
              "name": "HTTP Server Usage Vulnerability Due to Lack of TLS Encryption",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"HTTP Server Usage Vulnerability Due to Lack of TLS Encryption\" refers to the situation where an application communicates over an unencrypted HTTP connection, which can expose sensitive information to unauthorized parties. In JavaScript, this can occur when using the `http` module to create a server instead of the `https` module, which supports Transport Layer Security (TLS) encryption.\n\nIn the provided code, the TestBed is being configured with various modules and services. However, there is no explicit mention of using HTTPS or any form of encryption for the HTTP communications. This could potentially lead to the mentioned vulnerability if sensitive data is being transmitted.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS for any communication that involves sensitive data. HTTPS uses TLS (Transport Layer Security) to encrypt the data that is sent between the client and the server, preventing unauthorized access to the information.\n\nIn JavaScript, you can use the `https` module to create a server with TLS encryption. You will need to provide a TLS certificate and a private key.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not show the creation of an HTTP server, so it's not possible to provide a specific fix. However, here is a general example of how to create an HTTPS server in Node.js:\n\n```javascript\nconst https = require('https');\nconst fs = require('fs');\n\nconst options = {\n  key: fs.readFileSync('test/fixtures/keys/agent2-key.pem'),\n  cert: fs.readFileSync('test/fixtures/keys/agent2-cert.pem')\n};\n\nhttps.createServer(options, (req, res) => {\n  res.writeHead(200);\n  res.end('hello world\\n');\n}).listen(8000);\n```\n\nIn this example, `https.createServer` is used instead of `http.createServer`, and a TLS certificate and private key are provided.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/core/testing`\n- `@angular/router/testing`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- Custom components and services (e.g., `ScoreBoardComponent`, `ChallengeService`)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d953eac5-13b4-3ea0-aecb-9f19bced0a96",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This vulnerability can lead to exposure of sensitive data such as user credentials, personal information, or other confidential data. Attackers can easily intercept unencrypted data transmitted over HTTP, leading to potential data breaches.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP for transmitting sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it difficult for attackers to read the data even if they manage to intercept it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not directly show usage of HTTP or HTTPS. However, if there is any HTTP usage in the `provideHttpClient(withInterceptorsFromDi())` or `provideHttpClientTesting()` functions, it should be replaced with HTTPS.\n\nFor example, if you are using Angular's HttpClient and making HTTP requests like this:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('http://example.com/api/data');\n```\n\nYou should replace it with HTTPS:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('https://example.com/api/data');\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/core` for `TestBed`\n- `@ngx-translate/core` for `TranslateModule` and `TranslateService`\n- `@angular/forms` for `ReactiveFormsModule`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations` for `BrowserAnimationsModule`\n- `@angular/material` for various Material modules\n- `@angular/common/http` if `provideHttpClient` and `provideHttpClientTesting` are using Angular's HttpClient\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
