            {
              "id": "b0ce1087-1e08-3cf4-ba70-4f60ceaa8e66",
              "name": "Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnselected Field Database Query Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a developer does not explicitly specify the fields to be returned in a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data if the query is used to return results directly to the client. An attacker can potentially access all the data fields of the records returned by the query, including those that the developer did not intend to expose.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always explicitly specify the fields to be returned in a database query. This can be done by using the projection parameter of the query function, which allows you to specify the fields to be included or excluded from the results.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the vulnerability in the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nthis.challengeService.find({ sort: 'name' }, { fields: { sensitiveField1: 0, sensitiveField2: 0 } });\n```\n\nIn this example, `sensitiveField1` and `sensitiveField2` are the names of the fields that you want to exclude from the results. You should replace them with the actual names of the sensitive fields in your database.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using some kind of ORM or database abstraction library, but it's not clear which one. It could be Mongoose for MongoDB, Sequelize for SQL databases, or something else. You would need to install the appropriate library for your database system.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Query Parameterization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Query_Parameterization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-209",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-209"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ad969733-4d0d-34ee-a171-eb7c25572889",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from Bypassing Built-in Sanitization\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the given code snippet, the `bypassSecurityTrustHtml` method is used, which bypasses Angular's built-in sanitization to trust the value as HTML. This can potentially lead to XSS attacks if the `challenge.description` is user-controlled and not properly sanitized.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `bypassSecurityTrustHtml` or similar methods that bypass Angular's built-in sanitization. Instead, always sanitize user input before using it in your application. Angular provides a DomSanitizer service that can sanitize potentially unsafe values for use in different DOM contexts.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of bypassing the sanitization, use the `sanitize` method provided by Angular's DomSanitizer service:\n\n```javascript\nimport { DomSanitizer, SafeHtml } from '@angular/platform-browser';\n\nconstructor(private sanitizer: DomSanitizer) { }\n\nsanitizeDescription(challengeDescription: string): SafeHtml {\n  return this.sanitizer.sanitize(SecurityContext.HTML, challengeDescription);\n}\n```\n\nThen, in your template, use the sanitized value:\n\n```html\n<div [innerHTML]=\"sanitizeDescription(challenge.description)\"></div>\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Angular (version 2 or later)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7b2833c5-e9c5-3848-8530-f5ee383cbd04",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This can lead to data being intercepted, read, and modified by attackers. This vulnerability is particularly dangerous when dealing with sensitive data such as user credentials, personal information, or financial data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when transmitting sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for attackers to intercept and read the data. \n\nIn addition, consider using HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), which forces browsers to only use HTTPS, even if the user or a link specifies HTTP. This can help protect against downgrade attacks, where an attacker tries to force a connection to use unencrypted HTTP instead of HTTPS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not directly deal with HTTP or HTTPS connections, so it's not possible to provide a specific code fix. However, in general, when making HTTP requests in JavaScript, you should always use 'https://' in your URLs. For example:\n\n```javascript\nhttp.get('https://example.com/data')\n```\n\nInstead of:\n\n```javascript\nhttp.get('http://example.com/data')\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/router/testing`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `@angular/platform-browser`\n- `socket.io-client`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "546cc747-aed3-30e4-9685-c17375657493",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from Bypassing Application Sanitization\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is using Angular's DomSanitizer service to bypass security and trust the given value to be safe HTML. This can potentially lead to an XSS vulnerability if the `tableData[i].description` contains malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Input Validation**: Validate input on arrival, using a whitelist approach, reject any inputs that do not strictly conform to specifications.\n2. **Output Encoding**: Ensure that all user-supplied data is properly escaped before it is included in the output page.\n3. **Use Appropriate Response Headers**: To prevent XSS in HTTP responses that aren't intended to contain any HTML or JavaScript, you can use the Content-Type and X-Content-Type-Options headers to ensure that browsers interpret the responses the way you intend.\n4. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Implementing CSP makes it possible for server administrators to reduce or eliminate the vectors by which XSS can occur.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of bypassing the sanitizer, use the `sanitize` method to sanitize the HTML string:\n\n```javascript\ntableData[i].description = this.sanitizer.sanitize(SecurityContext.HTML, tableData[i].description);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Angular (for the `DomSanitizer` service)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "425b85bb-8dbb-3eb2-ac49-617d785afedd",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from Bypassing Built-in Sanitization\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `bypassSecurityTrustHtml` method is used, which bypasses Angular's built-in sanitization and marks the value as trusted HTML. This can potentially lead to an XSS vulnerability if the `queryParam` contains malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using the `bypassSecurityTrustHtml` method unless it's absolutely necessary and you're sure that the content you're marking as trusted is safe to be interpreted as HTML. Instead, use Angular's built-in sanitization to sanitize the `queryParam` before using it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nthis.searchValue = this.sanitizer.sanitize(SecurityContext.HTML, queryParam);\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, the `sanitize` method is used to sanitize the `queryParam` before using it. This will prevent any potential XSS attacks.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- Angular\n- @angular/platform-browser\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6e50c4ce-7fa9-3451-a0cb-7cf16b171926",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the variable `challengeName` is directly embedded into a string that is logged to the console. If `challengeName` is user-controlled and not properly sanitized, this could potentially lead to a Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape untrusted data based on the HTML context (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) that the data will be placed into. In this case, since the data is being placed into a JavaScript context, you should use a proper JavaScript encoding function to ensure that any potentially malicious characters are properly escaped.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nconsole.log(`Starting instructions for challenge \"${encodeURI(challengeName)}\"`)\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, the `encodeURI` function is used to encode the `challengeName` variable. This function encodes special characters, except: , / ? : @ & = + $ #. This will prevent any potentially malicious scripts from being executed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not require any additional library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0b3c9b88-5744-317c-b501-c64eded8fb2e",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe usage of unencrypted HTTP servers vulnerability in JavaScript refers to the risk of transmitting sensitive data over an unencrypted connection. This can lead to data being intercepted and manipulated by attackers. In the provided code, the TestBed is configuring a module with various imports and providers, but there is no indication of using a secure HTTPS connection for data transmission.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS for data transmission. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it harder for attackers to intercept and read the data. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe specific fix would depend on the context of the application and the server setup. However, in general, you should ensure that all requests are made over HTTPS. If the server supports it, you can use HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) to enforce the use of HTTPS.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic`\n- `@angular/platform-browser`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `ngx-cookie`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `socket.io-client`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1e5e65d9-b859-3911-8110-b723f60f0b20",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, crashes, and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the promise returned by the `import()` function is not properly handled in case of rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always include error handling mechanisms when working with promises. This can be done by chaining a `.catch()` method to the promise, which will be invoked when the promise is rejected. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nimport(/* webpackChunkName: \"tutorial\" */ '../../hacking-instructor')\n  .then(module => {\n    module.startHackingInstructorFor(challengeName)\n  })\n  .catch(error => {\n    console.error('Error importing module:', error);\n  });\n```\n\nIn this version, if the promise returned by `import()` is rejected, the error will be caught and logged to the console.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not explicitly import any libraries, but it does rely on the dynamic `import()` function, which is a part of ECMAScript (the standard upon which JavaScript is based). It also appears to rely on Webpack, a static module bundler for modern JavaScript applications, as indicated by the `webpackChunkName` comment.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3c92aa61-f071-3337-bcc8-df10ee432ea2",
              "name": "Usage of HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe usage of HTTP servers instead of HTTPS servers vulnerability in JavaScript refers to the insecure communication between the client and the server. HTTP is not secure, and it exposes the application to potential man-in-the-middle attacks, where an attacker can intercept the communication and gain access to sensitive information. This is particularly dangerous if the application handles sensitive data such as user credentials, personal information, or payment details.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) for all types of communication between the client and the server. HTTPS is a secure version of HTTP, where the communication is encrypted using SSL/TLS. This makes it much harder for an attacker to intercept and read the communication.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not directly show the usage of HTTP or HTTPS. However, if it's using HTTP, you should switch to HTTPS. This is usually done in the configuration of the server or the API endpoints. For example, if you have an API endpoint like `http://example.com/api`, you should change it to `https://example.com/api`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `ngx-cookie`\n- `@angular/router/testing`\n- `socket.io-client`\n- `rxjs`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "33765f8f-5655-39cd-9523-0eae25622dd6",
              "name": "Potential Open Redirect Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nAn Open Redirect Vulnerability, also known as Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards, is a type of web application security vulnerability that enables an attacker to redirect a user to a malicious website of their choice. This is typically achieved by manipulating the URL in a way that causes the web application to redirect the user to an untrusted site. In JavaScript, this can occur when the `window.location.replace()` function is used with user-controlled input.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `window.location.replace()` function is used to redirect the user to a new page. The URL for the new page is constructed by concatenating a host server address stored in `environment.hostServer` with the string '/profile'. If an attacker can control the value of `environment.hostServer`, they can potentially redirect the user to a malicious site.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using user-controlled input to construct URLs without proper validation and sanitization. If redirection is necessary, use a whitelist of trusted URLs to redirect to, and do not allow redirection to any other URLs.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\n// Define a list of trusted hosts\nconst trustedHosts = ['https://trustedhost1.com', 'https://trustedhost2.com'];\n\n// Check if the host server is in the list of trusted hosts\nif (trustedHosts.includes(environment.hostServer)) {\n  window.location.replace(environment.hostServer + '/profile');\n} else {\n  // Handle untrusted host server\n  console.error('Untrusted host server:', environment.hostServer);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not require any additional library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Unvalidated_Redirects_and_Forwards_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-601",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-601"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fc7ebad3-8ade-3b9c-b15d-bdd4cf872a5f",
              "name": "XSS Vulnerability in User-Controlled HTML Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the given code snippet, the application is using template literals to include user-controlled data (`version`) in HTML. If the `version` variable can be controlled by an attacker, it could lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Use frameworks that automatically escape XSS by design, such as Ruby on Rails, React JS, etc.\n- Escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) will resolve Reflected and Stored XSS vulnerabilities.\n- Apply context-sensitive encoding when modifying the browser document on the client side acts against DOM XSS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the given code snippet, you should ensure that the `version` variable is properly escaped before it is included in the HTML. Here is a simple fix using the `encodeURI` function:\n\n```javascript\nthis.version = `v${encodeURI(version)}`;\n```\n\nThis will ensure that any special characters in the `version` variable are properly escaped, preventing them from being interpreted as code.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe given code snippet does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7e888a2e-c1a6-3b82-9551-f7635e35f647",
              "name": "\"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that occurs when a developer does not explicitly specify the fields to be returned from a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data. In the provided code, the `find` method is used to query the database without specifying the fields to be returned. This means that all fields associated with the 'Score Board' will be returned, potentially including sensitive data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always specify the fields to be returned from a database query. This can be done by passing an object to the `find` method that specifies the fields to be returned. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nthis.challengeService.find({ name: 'Score Board' }, { fields: { name: 1 } }).subscribe({\n```\n\nIn this fixed version, the `find` method is passed an object that specifies that only the 'name' field should be returned. This ensures that no sensitive data is exposed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to use the following libraries:\n\n- A database library that provides the `find` method. This could be a library like Mongoose for MongoDB.\n- An Observable library that provides the `subscribe` method. This is likely RxJS.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Query Parameterization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Query_Parameterization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-209",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-209"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "240a5e06-490b-3136-820e-c9323e466ad6",
              "name": "Potential Open Redirect Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nAn Open Redirect Vulnerability, also known as Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards, is a type of web application security vulnerability that enables an attacker to redirect a user to a malicious website of their choice. This is typically achieved by manipulating the URL in a way that causes the web application to redirect the user to an untrusted site. In JavaScript, this can occur when user-supplied input is used to construct a URL for redirection without proper validation.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is using the `window.location.replace()` function to redirect the user to a new URL. The URL is constructed by concatenating a host server address (`environment.hostServer`) and a path (`/dataerasure`). If an attacker can control the `environment.hostServer` value, they can potentially redirect users to a malicious site.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using user-supplied input to construct URLs without proper validation and sanitization. If redirection is necessary, use a whitelist of trusted URLs and only redirect to those. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\n// Define a list of trusted hosts\nconst trustedHosts = ['https://trustedhost1.com', 'https://trustedhost2.com'];\n\n// Check if the host server is in the trusted list\nif (trustedHosts.includes(environment.hostServer)) {\n    window.location.replace(environment.hostServer + '/dataerasure');\n} else {\n    // Handle untrusted host server\n    console.error('Untrusted host server:', environment.hostServer);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not require any additional library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Unvalidated_Redirects_and_Forwards_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-601",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-601"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d5f2efd7-3da2-3040-8073-1afd0432f4e7",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the promise returned by the `import()` function is not handled properly. If the import fails for any reason (e.g., the module does not exist, network error, etc.), the rejection of the promise will not be caught and handled, leading to an Unhandled Promise Rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always have a `.catch()` block or use `try/catch` with async/await to handle any errors that may occur during the execution of a promise. This allows you to control the error handling flow and prevent unexpected application behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nimport(/* webpackChunkName: \"tutorial\" */ '../../hacking-instructor')\n  .then(module => {\n    module.startHackingInstructorFor('Score Board')\n  })\n  .catch(error => {\n    console.error('Error importing module:', error);\n  });\n```\n\nIn this version, a `.catch()` block is added to handle any errors that may occur during the import.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using dynamic imports, which is a feature of modern JavaScript and does not require any specific library. However, the code seems to be using Webpack for module bundling, as indicated by the `webpackChunkName` comment.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1df3f4d5-a694-33c9-b887-af7879aec9cd",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of unencrypted HTTP servers instead of HTTPS in JavaScript programming. This makes the application susceptible to Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks. In a MitM attack, the attacker intercepts the communication between two parties, alters the communication, and gains unauthorized access to sensitive information.\n\nIn the provided code, the TestBed is being configured with various modules and services. However, it doesn't specify whether the HTTP communication is encrypted or not. If the HTTP communication is unencrypted, it can lead to MitM attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS instead of HTTP for communication. HTTPS ensures that the communication between the client and the server is encrypted and secure. This makes it difficult for an attacker to intercept and alter the communication.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix this vulnerability in the provided code, ensure that all HTTP communications are done over HTTPS. This can be done by configuring the HttpClient to use HTTPS. Here is an example:\n\n```javascript\nimport { HttpClientModule } from '@angular/common/http';\n\nTestBed.configureTestingModule({\n  imports: [HttpClientModule, /* other modules */],\n  providers: [\n    { provide: TrackOrderService, useValue: trackOrderService },\n    { provide: DomSanitizer, useValue: sanitizer },\n    provideHttpClient(withInterceptorsFromDi()),\n    provideHttpClientTesting()\n  ]\n})\n.compileComponents();\n```\n\nIn the above code, HttpClientModule is imported from '@angular/common/http' and added to the imports array. This ensures that all HTTP communications are done over HTTPS.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `@angular/router/testing`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)\n\nPlease note that the links are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d4b2f142-b094-3026-a2e4-c1ff251e0dc2",
              "name": "XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is using Angular's DomSanitizer to bypass security and trust an HTML string that includes user-controlled data (`results.data[0].orderId`). This can lead to an XSS vulnerability if the user-controlled data is not properly validated or escaped.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Use frameworks that automatically escape XSS by design, such as the latest AngularJS, or ReactJS.\n- Escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) will resolve Reflected and Stored XSS vulnerabilities.\n- Apply context-sensitive encoding when modifying the browser document on the client side acts against DOM XSS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn this specific case, you should avoid using `bypassSecurityTrustHtml` method which tells Angular to trust an HTML string. Instead, you should sanitize the user input. Here is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nthis.results.orderNo = this.sanitizer.sanitize(SecurityContext.HTML, `<code>${results.data[0].orderId}</code>`);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Angular (for the `DomSanitizer` service)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e07f29d8-352c-395f-bb86-ad25000fffbd",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attack that can occur when unencrypted HTTP servers are used instead of HTTPS. In a MitM attack, the attacker intercepts the communication between two parties, potentially altering the communication or stealing sensitive information. When unencrypted HTTP servers are used, the data transmitted is not secure and can be easily intercepted by attackers.\n\nIn the context of JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when making HTTP requests to servers. If the server is not using HTTPS, the data sent in the request can be intercepted and potentially altered or stolen by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS instead of HTTP when making requests to servers. HTTPS encrypts the data sent in the request, making it much more difficult for an attacker to intercept and understand the data.\n\nIn addition, consider using HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), which forces the browser to only use HTTPS, even if the server supports HTTP. This can help prevent downgrade attacks, where an attacker forces the communication to use HTTP instead of HTTPS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code does not show any HTTP requests being made, so it's not possible to provide a specific source code fix. However, in general, when making HTTP requests in JavaScript, you should always use HTTPS. For example, if you're using the `fetch` API, make sure the URL starts with `https://` and not `http://`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/router`\n- `@angular/forms`\n- `ngx-cookie`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `UserService`\n- `TwoFactorAuthService`\n- `CookieService`\n- `WindowRefService`\n- `HttpClient`\n- `HttpClientTesting`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cd89e8b8-7afa-3240-a81f-49dd378a2aea",
              "name": "Usage of HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"Usage of HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS Servers\" refers to the insecure practice of transmitting data over HTTP instead of HTTPS. HTTP is not secure, and data sent over HTTP can be intercepted by attackers. This is particularly dangerous if sensitive data such as passwords, credit card numbers, or personal information is being transmitted. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when making HTTP requests to servers.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS instead of HTTP when transmitting data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for attackers to intercept and read the data. \n\nIn addition, consider using HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS), which is a web security policy mechanism that helps to protect websites against protocol downgrade attacks and cookie hijacking. It allows web servers to declare that web browsers (or other complying user agents) should only interact with it using secure HTTPS connections, and never via the insecure HTTP protocol.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe TestBed.configureTestingModule code provided does not directly show the usage of HTTP or HTTPS. However, if there is any HTTP usage in the `ConfigurationService` or `TwoFactorAuthService`, it should be replaced with HTTPS. \n\nFor example, if you have a code like this in your services:\n\n```javascript\nhttp.get('http://example.com/data')\n```\n\nYou should replace it with:\n\n```javascript\nhttp.get('https://example.com/data')\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- ReactiveFormsModule\n- TranslateModule\n- BrowserAnimationsModule\n- MatCheckboxModule\n- MatFormFieldModule\n- MatCardModule\n- MatIconModule\n- MatInputModule\n- MatTableModule\n- MatPaginatorModule\n- MatDialogModule\n- MatDividerModule\n- MatButtonModule\n- QrCodeModule\n- MatSnackBarModule\n- MatTooltipModule\n- TwoFactorAuthComponent\n- ConfigurationService\n- TwoFactorAuthService\n- HttpClient\n- HttpClientTesting\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "13d18d35-a56c-31eb-b924-a1e2814a67a7",
              "name": "\"Hardcoded Credentials Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nHardcoded credentials vulnerability refers to the practice of embedding plaintext credentials directly into the source code. This is a common security anti-pattern that can lead to unauthorized access if the source code is ever exposed. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when sensitive data such as API keys, database credentials, or in this case, a setup token, are hardcoded into the code.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `setupToken` is hardcoded as `'12345'`. This is a security risk as anyone who gains access to the source code will also gain access to this token.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid hardcoding sensitive data directly into the source code. Instead, use environment variables or secure configuration files to store this data. This way, the data is not exposed in the source code and can be easily changed if necessary.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nconst setupToken = process.env.SETUP_TOKEN;\n\ntwoFactorAuthService.status.and.returnValue(of({ setup: false, email: 'email', secret: 'secret', setupToken: setupToken }))\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `setupToken` is retrieved from an environment variable instead of being hardcoded. This way, the token is not exposed in the source code.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example appears to use the following libraries:\n\n- `rxjs`: The `of` function is a part of the `rxjs` library.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: A2-Broken Authentication](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A2_2017-Broken_Authentication)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-798",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-798"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "bef8198f-67e2-3a8a-9f93-0324093c51c4",
              "name": "\"Hardcoded Credentials Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nHardcoded credentials vulnerability in JavaScript refers to the practice of embedding plaintext credentials (such as usernames, passwords, tokens, or keys) directly into the source code. This is a security risk as it exposes sensitive data to anyone who has access to the source code. In the provided code snippet, the `setupToken` is hardcoded, which is a potential security vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid hardcoding sensitive data in your source code. Instead, use environment variables, configuration files, or secure vaults to store and retrieve these values. These methods allow you to keep sensitive data out of the source code, making it harder for an attacker to gain access to this information.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of hardcoding the `setupToken`, you can store it in an environment variable and retrieve it when needed. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```javascript\nconst setupToken = process.env.SETUP_TOKEN;\n\ntwoFactorAuthService.status.and.returnValue(of({ setup: true, email: 'email', secret: 'secret', setupToken: setupToken }))\n```\n\nIn this example, `process.env.SETUP_TOKEN` retrieves the `SETUP_TOKEN` value from the environment variables. Remember to set the `SETUP_TOKEN` environment variable in your server environment.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using the `of` function from the RxJS library. Therefore, the RxJS library is required for the code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Hardcoded Password](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html#hardcoded-passwords)\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-798",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-798"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "921c5553-6f58-30c9-aa5c-d8073dc43994",
              "name": "Detected hardcoded secret vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected hardcoded secret vulnerability\" is a common security issue in JavaScript and other programming languages. It occurs when sensitive data such as passwords, API keys, or cryptographic key material are embedded directly into the source code. This is a bad practice because it exposes these secrets to anyone who has access to the code. If the code is in a public repository, this could potentially be anyone on the internet. Even in private repositories, it increases the risk of secrets being exposed through logs, version control history, or by a malicious insider.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the secret is hardcoded as 'secret' and the setupToken as '12345'. This is a security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nThe best way to handle secrets is to not include them in the code at all. Instead, they should be accessed through a controlled means such as environment variables, which are not included in the code repository and can be controlled and secured separately. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\ntwoFactorAuthService.status.and.returnValue(of({ setup: true, email: process.env.EMAIL, secret: process.env.SECRET, setupToken: process.env.SETUP_TOKEN }))\n```\n\nIn the above code, 'process.env.EMAIL', 'process.env.SECRET', and 'process.env.SETUP_TOKEN' should be set in the environment where the code is running. \n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using Jasmine for testing and RxJS for handling asynchronous data. The specific dependencies are:\n\n- Jasmine\n- RxJS\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: A2-Broken Authentication](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A2_2017-Broken_Authentication)\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-798",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-798"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "623b7f54-6088-3271-85b7-d20991c36359",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This can lead to data being intercepted, read, and modified by attackers. In the provided code, the vulnerability may arise if the `UserService` or `HttpClient` are making HTTP requests to unencrypted servers.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much harder for attackers to intercept and read the data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe specific fix would depend on how the `UserService` and `HttpClient` are implemented. However, generally, you should ensure that all URLs used for HTTP requests are HTTPS URLs. If the URLs are hardcoded, simply replace `http://` with `https://`. If the URLs are configurable, ensure that the configuration uses HTTPS URLs.\n\nFor example, if `UserService` makes a request to a hardcoded URL, it might look like this:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('http://example.com/userdata');\n```\n\nYou should change it to:\n\n```javascript\nthis.http.get('https://example.com/userdata');\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to require the following libraries:\n\n- `@angular/core` for `TestBed`\n- `@angular/material` for `MatDividerModule` and `MatDialogModule`\n- `@ngx-translate/core` for `TranslateModule`\n- `@angular/common/http` for `HttpClient`\n- The `UserService`, `MatDialogRef`, `MAT_DIALOG_DATA`, `provideHttpClient`, and `provideHttpClientTesting` appear to be custom providers and would require their respective modules.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "21e41fce-e28b-3469-88cd-e95b698e65f1",
              "name": "XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is adding a class to an element based on user-controlled data (`conf.application.theme`). If an attacker can control the `theme` value, they could potentially inject malicious scripts, leading to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (HTML body, HTML attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) that the data lands. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA simple fix for this specific vulnerability would be to whitelist the `theme` values. This can be done by creating an array of allowed themes and checking if the user-provided theme is in this array before adding it as a class. If the theme is not in the array, a default theme can be used.\n\n```javascript\nconst allowedThemes = ['light', 'dark', 'blue', 'green']; // Add all allowed themes here\nlet theme = conf.application.theme;\n\nif (!allowedThemes.includes(theme)) {\n    theme = 'default'; // Fallback to a default theme\n}\n\noverlayContainer\n    .getContainerElement()\n    .classList.add(theme + '-theme');\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using the Angular Material library, specifically the `OverlayContainer` service. Therefore, the `@angular/material` package is required for this code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8eaafd9f-7b39-3468-8224-028c4723b165",
              "name": "Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Usage of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the security risk associated with sending sensitive data over unencrypted HTTP connections. This can lead to data being intercepted, read, and manipulated by attackers. In the context of the provided code, if the `WalletService` or any other service communicates with a server over HTTP, it could expose sensitive data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it much harder for attackers to read or manipulate the data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe specific fix would depend on how the `WalletService` or other services are implemented. However, generally, you should ensure that all server URLs use `https://` instead of `http://`. If the server URLs are hardcoded in the services, you can simply replace `http://` with `https://`. If the server URLs are configured elsewhere, you should update the configuration to use HTTPS.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/forms`\n- `@angular/material`\n- `@angular/platform-browser/animations`\n- `@angular/router/testing`\n- `@ngx-translate/core`\n- `rxjs`\n- `wallet.js` (assuming this is where `WalletService` is defined)\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "56d13e78-cd8b-3fa2-b994-3007fe0d294b",
              "name": "\"Bracket Notation Object Injection through selectedCompilerVersion\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Bracket Notation Object Injection through selectedCompilerVersion\" is a type of vulnerability in JavaScript where an attacker can inject properties into an object through bracket notation. This can lead to various security issues such as unauthorized data access, data manipulation, or even remote code execution.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the vulnerability arises from the use of `this.selectedCompilerVersion` as a key to access the `compilerReleases` object. If an attacker can control the value of `selectedCompilerVersion`, they can potentially access or modify any property of the `compilerReleases` object.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using user-controlled input as property keys in bracket notation. If this is unavoidable, you should validate or sanitize the input before using it. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nif (typeof this.selectedCompilerVersion === 'string' && this.selectedCompilerVersion in compilerReleases) {\n  const selectedVersion = compilerReleases[this.selectedCompilerVersion] as string;\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first check if `this.selectedCompilerVersion` is a string and if it is a property of `compilerReleases` before using it.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not appear to require any specific library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: Injection](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A1_2017-Injection)\n- [CWE-74: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Output Used by a Downstream Component ('Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/74.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are subject to change and may not be accessible in the future."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-74",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/74.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-74"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fa3dce9f-81a4-351c-ab98-dafc47d079b6",
              "name": "\"Bracket Notation Object Injection Vulnerability through selectedContractName\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Bracket Notation Object Injection Vulnerability through selectedContractName\" is a type of security vulnerability in JavaScript that occurs when user-supplied input is used to access object properties using bracket notation. This can lead to potential security risks as an attacker can manipulate the input to access or modify unintended properties or even execute arbitrary code.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `selectedContractName` is used to access a property of the `compiledContracts` object. If an attacker can control the value of `selectedContractName`, they could potentially access or modify any property of the `compiledContracts` object.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it's important to validate and sanitize user-supplied input before using it to access object properties. This can be done by checking if the property exists and is intended to be accessible, and by ensuring the input does not contain any special characters or sequences that could be used for code injection.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's a simple way to fix the vulnerability in the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nif (this.compiledContracts.hasOwnProperty(this.selectedContractName)) {\n    const selectedContract = this.compiledContracts[this.selectedContractName];\n} else {\n    // Handle error: property does not exist or is not accessible\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, `hasOwnProperty` is used to check if the property exists and is intended to be accessible before accessing it.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not appear to require any specific library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: Injection](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A1_2017-Injection)\n- [CWE-74: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Output Used by a Downstream Component ('Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/74.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active at the time of writing this report."
              },
