            {
              "id": "78bd9388-e8f8-3d49-9a50-8277fdd5cad5",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the promise returned by the `import()` function is not handled properly. If the import fails for any reason (e.g., the module does not exist, network error, etc.), the rejection of the promise will not be caught and handled, leading to an Unhandled Promise Rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always have a `.catch()` block or use `try/catch` with async/await to handle any errors that may occur during the execution of a promise. This allows you to control the error handling flow and prevent unexpected application behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nimport(/* webpackChunkName: \"tutorial\" */ '../../hacking-instructor')\n  .then(module => {\n    module.startHackingInstructorFor('Score Board')\n  })\n  .catch(error => {\n    console.error('Error importing module:', error);\n  });\n```\n\nIn this version, a `.catch()` block is added to handle any errors that may occur during the import.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using dynamic imports, which is a feature of modern JavaScript and does not require any specific library. However, the code seems to be using Webpack for module bundling, as indicated by the `webpackChunkName` comment.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5898fb32-89ce-32f7-bb2c-8f0a871b10ed",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of unencrypted HTTP servers instead of HTTPS in JavaScript programming. This makes the application susceptible to Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks. In a MitM attack, the attacker intercepts the communication between two parties, alters the communication, and gains unauthorized access to sensitive information.\n\nIn the provided code, the TestBed is configuring a module with various imports and providers. However, it doesn't specify whether the HTTP communication is encrypted or not. If the HTTP communication is unencrypted, it can lead to MitM attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS instead of HTTP for communication. HTTPS ensures that the communication between the client and the server is encrypted and secure. This makes it difficult for an attacker to intercept and alter the communication.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix this vulnerability in the provided code, ensure that all HTTP communications are done over HTTPS. This can be done by configuring the HttpClient to use HTTPS. Here is an example:\n\n```javascript\nimport { HttpClient, HttpHandler } from '@angular/common/http';\n\nexport function provideHttpClient(httpHandler: HttpHandler) {\n  return new HttpClient(httpHandler, { withCredentials: true, useHttps: true });\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `@angular/core`\n- `@angular/common/http`\n- `ngx-translate/core`\n- `ngx-cookie`\n- `@angular/material/dialog`\n- `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic/testing`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "89d53b9d-9a39-3528-92bf-1b5b02bba0bf",
              "name": "\"XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in 'b' Anti-Pattern\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe XSS (Cross-Site Scripting) Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in 'b' Anti-Pattern is a security flaw in JavaScript where an attacker can inject malicious scripts into webpages viewed by other users. This vulnerability occurs when user input is used directly in the creation of HTML or JavaScript without proper sanitization. In the provided code, the user-controlled data is directly injected into the 'b' element without any form of sanitization or encoding, making it vulnerable to XSS attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize user input: Ensure that all user input is validated and sanitized before it is used in your application. This can help to prevent malicious scripts from being injected into your webpages.\n\n2. Use Content Security Policy (CSP): CSP is a security feature that can help to prevent XSS attacks by restricting the sources from which scripts can be loaded.\n\n3. Use output encoding libraries: Libraries like OWASP's Java Encoder for Java or their JavaScript equivalent can automatically encode user input to make it safe for use in HTML or JavaScript.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, the user-controlled data is directly injected into the 'b' element. This can be fixed by sanitizing the user input before it is used. Here is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nvar dat=dat||{};dat.gui=dat.gui||{};dat.utils=dat.utils||{};dat.controllers=dat.controllers||{};dat.dom=dat.dom||{};dat.color=dat.color||{};dat.utils.css=function(){return{load:function(e,a){a=a||document;var b=a.createElement(\"link\");b.type=\"text/css\";b.rel=\"stylesheet\";b.href=e;a.getElementsByTagName(\"head\")[0].appendChild(b)},inject:function(e,a){a=a||document;var b=document.createElement(\"style\");b.type=\"text/css\";b.innerHTML=encodeURI(e);a.getElementsByTagName(\"head\")[0].appendChild(b)}}}();\n```\n\nIn this fixed version, the `encodeURI` function is used to sanitize the user input before it is used.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not appear to have any library dependencies.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7e34dabe-be3e-373b-bc82-2a6f3776fe89",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. The vulnerability here is that the user-controlled data is directly used in HTML string, which can lead to potential XSS attacks if the user-controlled data contains malicious scripts. In this specific case, the JavaScript code is vulnerable to XSS attacks as it directly uses user-controlled data in the HTML string without proper sanitization or encoding.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate user input: Ensure that user-controlled data is validated and sanitized before using it in your application. This can help to prevent malicious scripts from being executed.\n\n2. Use appropriate output encoding: When user-controlled data is used in HTML strings, it should be properly encoded to ensure that it is safe to display.\n\n3. Use Content Security Policy (CSP): CSP can help to mitigate the impact of XSS attacks by restricting the sources from which scripts can be loaded.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in the provided code cannot be identified without more context. However, a general fix would be to sanitize the user-controlled data before using it in the HTML string. For example, if `d` is user-controlled data, it should be sanitized before being used in the RegExp:\n\n```javascript\nd = sanitize(d);\nreturn RegExp(\"(?:^|\\\\s+)\" + d + \"(?:\\\\s+|$)\").test(a.className) || !1;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not appear to have any specific library dependencies.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
