              "id": "43ea310d-cf19-3e80-85a3-d0eb29d75765",
              "name": "\"XSS Vulnerability Risk in User-Controlled Data in `this.__button` Anti-Pattern\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe XSS (Cross-Site Scripting) Vulnerability Risk in User-Controlled Data in `this.__button` Anti-Pattern is a security flaw in JavaScript programming language. This vulnerability occurs when user-controlled data is used to create a new HTML element without proper sanitization or encoding. In this case, the `this.__button.innerHTML` is directly assigned a value that could be controlled by the user. If an attacker can control this value, they can inject malicious scripts that will be executed in the context of the victim's browser.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always sanitize and/or encode user-controlled data before using it to create or modify HTML elements. This can be done using built-in JavaScript functions or third-party libraries.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```javascript\ndat.controllers.FunctionController=function(e,a,b){\n    var d=function(b,c,e){\n        d.superclass.call(this,b,c);\n        var k=this;\n        this.__button=document.createElement(\"div\");\n        this.__button.textContent=void 0===e?\"Fire\":e; // Use textContent instead of innerHTML\n        a.bind(this.__button,\"click\",function(a){\n            a.preventDefault();\n            k.fire();\n            return!1\n        });\n        a.addClass(this.__button,\"button\");\n        this.domElement.appendChild(this.__button)\n    };\n    d.superclass=e;\n    b.extend(d.prototype,e.prototype,{\n        fire:function(){\n            this.__onChange&&this.__onChange.call(this);\n            this.__onFinishChange&&this.__onFinishChange.call(this,\n    });\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, `this.__button.textContent` is used instead of `this.__button.innerHTML`. The `textContent` property does not parse HTML, so it is safe to use with user-controlled data.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not provide enough context to determine the exact library dependencies. However, it seems to use a custom library or framework that provides functions such as `a.bind` and `a.addClass`.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
