              "id": "567201a2-d31a-31a7-a126-e683b40dc7d5",
              "name": "Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\" vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when user-controlled data is used to create HTML strings. This can lead to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks, where an attacker can inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. These scripts can steal sensitive data, manipulate web content, or perform actions on behalf of the user without their consent.\n\nIn the provided code, the vulnerability lies in the `toString` function. If the input `a` is user-controlled and not properly sanitized, it can lead to an XSS attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always sanitize user-controlled data before using it to create HTML strings. This can be done by using functions that encode special characters, such as `encodeURIComponent` in JavaScript.\n\nAdditionally, you should use context-specific output encoding when modifying the browser document on the client side. For example, use element.textContent (not innerHTML) when inserting text into a <script> or <style> element.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ndat.color.toString = function(e) {\n    return function(a) {\n        if (1 == a.a || e.isUndefined(a.a)) {\n            for (a = encodeURIComponent(a.hex.toString(16)); 6 > a.length;) a = \"0\" + a;\n            return \"#\" + a\n        }\n        return \"rgba(\" + Math.round(a.r) + \",\" + Math.round(a.g) + \",\" + Math.round(a.b) + \",\" + a.a + \")\"\n    }\n}(dat.utils.common);\n```\n\nIn this fix, `encodeURIComponent` is used to sanitize the user-controlled data `a.hex`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to depend on a library named `dat`. However, without more context, it's hard to determine the exact dependencies.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
