            {
              "id": "444d7955-89c0-310b-9f1a-e8a4c1c31325",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to a Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attack. This is a type of injection, in which malicious scripts are injected into otherwise benign and trusted websites. XSS attacks occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript.\n\nIn this case, the vulnerability arises from the fact that user-controlled data is being used in an HTML string without proper sanitization or encoding. This could allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts, which would then be executed in the context of the victim's browser.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. **Escape User Input**: Always escape user-controlled data before including it in HTML output. This ensures that any characters that have special meaning in HTML are properly encoded, preventing them from being interpreted as code.\n\n2. **Use Safe APIs**: Use APIs, such as `element.textContent` (and avoid `element.innerHTML`), which automatically escape HTML syntax and can help prevent XSS attacks.\n\n3. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Implementing CSP can effectively reduce the risk of XSS attacks by specifying the domains that the browser should consider to be valid sources of executable scripts.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code does not provide enough context to provide a specific fix. However, as a general recommendation, user input should be sanitized before use. For instance, if user input is being used to construct HTML, it should be properly escaped to prevent any potentially malicious code from being executed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet does not provide enough information to determine the specific library dependencies.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
