              "id": "79a40598-8a64-3f91-b5e2-e0d14a99e0e0",
              "name": "\"User-Controlled Data in 'b' as an XSS Vulnerability Anti-Pattern\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"User-Controlled Data in 'b' as an XSS Vulnerability Anti-Pattern\" vulnerability in JavaScript refers to a situation where user-controlled data is directly used in a sensitive context without proper sanitization or validation. In the provided code snippet, the user-controlled data is directly used to set the `innerHTML` property of an HTML element. This can lead to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks if the user-controlled data contains malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always sanitize and validate user-controlled data before using it in a sensitive context. This can be done by using built-in functions or libraries that are designed to sanitize and validate user input. Additionally, it is also recommended to use text content assignment methods like `textContent` or `innerText` instead of `innerHTML` to prevent the execution of potentially malicious scripts.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the line:\n\n```javascript\nb.innerHTML=c.property;\n```\n\nwith:\n\n```javascript\nb.textContent=c.property;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to depend on the following libraries:\n\n- dat.GUI\n- dat.gui.GUI\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
