            {
              "id": "15f39223-ee4c-3dee-81ec-a43c16673a0a",
              "name": "\"XSS Vulnerabilities from User-Controlled Data in 'C'\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the vulnerability lies in the `E` and `D` functions where the `innerHTML` property is used to directly insert user-controlled data into the HTML document. This can lead to XSS attacks if the user-controlled data contains malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Use frameworks that automatically escape XSS by design, such as Ruby on Rails, React JS, etc.\n- Escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) will resolve Reflected and Stored XSS vulnerabilities.\n- Apply context-sensitive encoding when modifying the browser document on the client side acts against DOM XSS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe recommended fix for the provided code is to use `textContent` instead of `innerHTML` when inserting user-controlled data into the HTML document. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```javascript\nfunction E(a,b,d){\n    var c=document.createElement(\"option\");\n    c.textContent=b;\n    c.value=b;\n    a.__preset_select.appendChild(c);\n    d&&(a.__preset_select.selectedIndex=a.__preset_select.length-1)\n}\n\nfunction D(a,b){\n    var d=a.__preset_select[a.__preset_select.selectedIndex];\n    d.textContent=b?d.value+\"*\":d.value\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using the jQuery library, as indicated by the `h.each` function which is a common jQuery method.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c269c8e6-2ec5-3359-a795-97f006ea8dd2",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\" in JavaScript refers to a situation where an application includes untrusted data in a new HTML string and sends it to a web browser without proper validation or escaping. This can lead to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks, where attackers can inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. These scripts can steal sensitive data, manipulate web content, or redirect users to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code, the potential vulnerability lies in the use of `localStorage.setItem()` function where user-controlled data (`document.location.href`) is used as a key. If an attacker can control the `href` value, they can potentially inject malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate user input: Ensure that user-controlled data is safe before using it. Use a secure method to validate the input against a set of defined rules.\n\n2. Escape user input: Use functions that can escape special characters from the user-controlled data before using it in your HTML string.\n\n3. Use Content Security Policy (CSP): Implement CSP to prevent the execution of inline scripts.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA simple fix would be to sanitize the `document.location.href` before using it as a key in `localStorage.setItem()` function. Here is an example of how you can do it:\n\n```javascript\nvar sanitizedHref = encodeURIComponent(document.location.href);\nlocalStorage.setItem(sanitizedHref + \".gui\", JSON.stringify(c.getSaveObject()));\n```\n\nIn this code, `encodeURIComponent()` function is used to encode the `href` value, which effectively sanitizes it.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using the following libraries:\n\n1. jQuery (for the `h.each()` and `h.defer()` functions)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
