            {
              "id": "7660f58b-6e3a-350f-b7eb-801c46d029d3",
              "name": "\"Q's User-Controlled Data as an Anti-Pattern Leading to XSS Vulnerabilities\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Q's User-Controlled Data as an Anti-Pattern Leading to XSS Vulnerabilities\" is a type of Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability that occurs when user-controlled data is used in a way that allows for the injection of malicious scripts. In the provided JavaScript code, the vulnerability arises from the use of user-controlled data in the `set` function of the `name` property. The user input is directly assigned to `a.name` and then used to set the `innerHTML` of an element, which can lead to XSS attacks if the user input is not properly sanitized.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always sanitize user-controlled data before using it in a context that can lead to script execution. This can be done by using functions that encode special characters, such as `encodeURIComponent` or `encodeURI` in JavaScript.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nname: {\n  get: function() {\n    return a.name;\n  },\n  set: function(b) {\n    a.name = b;\n    q && (q.innerHTML = encodeURIComponent(a.name));\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `encodeURIComponent` function is used to encode special characters in the user input, which prevents the possibility of script injection.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not contain enough information to determine the library dependencies.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
