            {
              "id": "f75596bc-cee8-383e-8ffd-6bca37ca080c",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejections Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejections Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, a promise is created that will remove a canvas element after a timeout of 6000 milliseconds. However, there is no error handling in place to catch any potential rejections of this promise.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always have error handling in place for promises. This can be done using `.catch()` blocks or by using `try/catch` with async/await. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\ntimeout(6000).then(() => {\n    canvas.remove()\n}).catch((error) => {\n    console.error('An error occurred:', error);\n});\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, if the promise is rejected, the error will be caught and logged to the console.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not specify what `timeout` or `canvas` are, so it's not possible to list any specific library dependencies. However, in general, this code would likely require a library or function that provides the `timeout` function, and a context (like a web page) where a `canvas` element exists.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "785cb183-7d01-3952-86e8-d1938a9b7079",
              "name": "Exclusion of Unreadable Files from Scans Due to High False Positive Rate; Recommendation for Storage in CDN or Cloud Buckets",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This vulnerability refers to the potential risk of including unreadable files in scans, which can lead to a high false positive rate. This can occur when a JavaScript application attempts to read or scan files that are not properly formatted or encoded, leading to errors or false positives. This can be particularly problematic when the application is designed to scan user-uploaded files or content, as it can be exploited by an attacker to upload malicious files or trigger false alarms.\n\nMitigation advice for this vulnerability includes:\n\n- Exclude unreadable files from scans. This can be done by implementing checks to ensure that files are in a readable format before attempting to scan them.\n- Store files in a Content Delivery Network (CDN) or cloud buckets. This can help to reduce the risk of false positives by ensuring that files are stored in a secure and reliable manner.\n- Implement proper error handling. This can help to prevent the application from crashing or behaving unpredictably when it encounters an unreadable file.\n\nHere is a general recommendation for fixing the vulnerability in the provided code:\n\n```javascript\n// Check if the file is readable before scanning\nif (file.isReadable()) {\n    // Scan the file\n    scan(file);\n} else {\n    // Log the error and continue\n    console.error('File is not readable: ' + file.path);\n}\n```\n\nThis code checks if the file is readable before attempting to scan it. If the file is not readable, it logs an error and continues with the next file.\n\nLibrary dependencies: This code does not appear to require any specific library dependencies to execute properly.\n\nFor more information on this vulnerability and how to prevent it, you can refer to the following resources:\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 - A6: Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be accessible to all users."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-20",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-20"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f715d2b6-11e7-3b4f-a389-fd274d3db66e",
              "name": "\"Secure Connection and Data Privacy Check in Fetch(x) Function\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `fetch()` function in JavaScript is used to make HTTP requests to the server. This function is vulnerable to security issues if it's not used properly. One of the main security concerns is the lack of secure connection and data privacy check. If the data is sent over an insecure connection, it can be intercepted by attackers. This can lead to data leakage, identity theft, and other security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS instead of HTTP in the URL. HTTPS ensures that the data is encrypted and secure during transmission. Also, validate and sanitize the input data to prevent injection attacks. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the fixed code:\n\n```javascript\nconst res = await fetch('https://yourdomain.com/rest/admin/application-configuration')\n```\n\nIn the above code, we have replaced HTTP with HTTPS in the URL.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `fetch()` function is a built-in function in modern browsers and does not require any library dependencies.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)\n\nPlease note that all the links are active and accessible for anonymous users."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f3cd0d44-ecb3-302a-ba1c-69e414bf8b8d",
              "name": "\"Exclusion of Unreadable Files from Scans Due to High False Positive Rates\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"Exclusion of Unreadable Files from Scans Due to High False Positive Rates\" is not directly related to JavaScript programming language. However, the context provided seems to be about Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability, which is a common security issue in web applications, including those written in JavaScript. XSS attacks occur when an attacker uses a web application to send malicious code, generally in the form of a browser side script, to a different end user. The end user’s browser has no way to know that the script should not be trusted, and will execute the script. Because it thinks the script came from a trusted source, the malicious script can access any cookies, session tokens, or other sensitive information retained by the browser and used with that site.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo prevent XSS attacks, you should:\n\n1. Use a safe API which avoids the use of the interpreter entirely or provides a parameterized interface.\n2. Use appropriate response headers to prevent any HTML/JavaScript injection.\n3. Input validation: Treat all input as untrusted. Validate input: length, format, and type.\n4. Output encoding: Ensure that all user-supplied input is either properly escaped, or better yet, output encoded using a trusted library.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf you are using Express.js, you can use the `xss-clean` library to sanitize the input. Here is an example:\n\n```javascript\nconst express = require('express');\nconst xss = require('xss-clean');\nconst app = express();\n\napp.use(xss());\n\napp.get('/', (req, res) => {\n  res.send('Hello World!');\n});\n\napp.listen(3000, () => {\n  console.log('App listening on port 3000!');\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- express\n- xss-clean\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d2b138e6-d2bc-3c8e-a800-f454f01a8044",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability can occur when user-controlled data is used in an HTML string. In JavaScript, this can happen when user input is directly embedded into the code without proper sanitization or encoding. In the provided code snippet, the variable `elementSelector` is directly embedded into a string that is logged to the console. If `elementSelector` contains malicious script, it could lead to an XSS attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it's important to properly sanitize or encode user-controlled data before using it in your code. This can be done using various methods, such as HTML encoding, JavaScript encoding, or using safe APIs that automatically perform these operations.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn this specific case, since the user-controlled data is being logged to the console, the risk of an XSS attack is relatively low. However, it's still a good practice to sanitize the data. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```javascript\nlet sanitizedElementSelector = elementSelector.replace(/</g, \"&lt;\").replace(/>/g, \"&gt;\");\nconsole.warn(`Could not find Element with selector \"${sanitizedElementSelector}\"`);\n```\n\nThis code replaces any HTML tags in `elementSelector` with their corresponding HTML entities, preventing any potential script from being executed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "630875c4-66ea-3336-b970-0d8ae32e72ae",
              "name": "\"Secure Connection and Data Privacy Check in Fetch(x) Function\"",
