            {
              "id": "5e1197f8-6bdd-3639-96b4-b00673a11dcd",
              "name": "Redundant Assignment of 'delay'",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Redundant Assignment of 'delay'\" vulnerability in JavaScript refers to the unnecessary or repeated assignment of a variable. In the provided code snippet, the variable `delay` is assigned a value twice. First, it is assigned the value of `playbackDelays[config.hackingInstructor.hintPlaybackSpeed]`, and then it is assigned a default value of `1.0` if the first assignment results in a null or undefined value. This redundancy can lead to confusion, make the code harder to read and maintain, and potentially introduce bugs.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid redundant assignments. In this case, you can use the logical nullish assignment (`??=`) operator to assign a default value to `delay` only if it is null or undefined. This ensures that `delay` is assigned a value only once, eliminating the redundancy.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's how you can fix the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nlet delay = playbackDelays[config.hackingInstructor.hintPlaybackSpeed] ?? 1.0;\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, `delay` is assigned the value of `playbackDelays[config.hackingInstructor.hintPlaybackSpeed]` if it is not null or undefined. Otherwise, it is assigned a default value of `1.0`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not appear to require any specific library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-563: Unused Variable](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/563.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-563",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/563.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-563"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ed643f60-c552-35eb-98a4-77e2e3e13f77",
              "name": "\"XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in TextBox InnerHTML Anti-Pattern\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn this specific case, the vulnerability arises from the use of `innerHTML` property to insert user-controlled data into the HTML content of a `textBox` element. The `snarkdown` function is used to convert Markdown syntax into HTML, but it does not sanitize the input, which can lead to XSS attacks if the input contains malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape untrusted data based on the HTML context (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) that the data will be placed into. In this case, you should use a safe method to insert the user-controlled data into the HTML content, such as `textContent` property, or use a library that automatically escapes the data for you.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the vulnerable line with the following code:\n\n```javascript\ntextBox.textContent = snarkdown(hint.text);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `snarkdown` library to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2d9b452d-2fbf-3481-a049-a9ff635250f5",
              "name": "XSS Vulnerability due to Dynamic Input in innerHTML",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn this specific case, the vulnerability is due to dynamic input in `innerHTML`. The `innerHTML` property can be used to modify the content of an HTML element, but it's also a common source of XSS, because it interprets the passed string as HTML (including any scripts it might contain).\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Use the principle of least privilege.\n- Validate input: Ensure that all user-supplied input is validated before it is processed.\n- Encode output: Ensure that user-supplied input is properly encoded when it is displayed.\n- Use appropriate response headers: To prevent XSS attacks, ensure that your application uses appropriate response headers, such as `Content-Type` and `X-Content-Type-Options`.\n- Content Security Policy (CSP): Implementing CSP can effectively reduce the risk and impact of XSS attacks in modern browsers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `innerHTML`, use `textContent` or `innerText` which do not interpret the passed string as HTML but as plain text:\n\n```javascript\ncancelButton.textContent = '×';\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f3c4fbe3-5cf1-3792-8d8b-faedfc146ac6",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Man-in-the-Middle Attacks due to Use of Unencrypted HTTP Servers Instead of HTTPS\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attack that can occur when unencrypted HTTP servers are used instead of HTTPS. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can be exploited when data is transmitted over an insecure network. An attacker can intercept the data and alter it before it reaches its intended destination. This can lead to unauthorized access to sensitive information, data corruption, or even denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use HTTPS instead of HTTP. HTTPS encrypts the data between the client and the server, making it difficult for an attacker to read or modify the data. Additionally, it is also recommended to use HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) which forces the browser to use HTTPS connections only.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, the `HttpLoaderFactory` is used which might be using HTTP for data transmission. To fix this, ensure that all requests made by `HttpLoaderFactory` are over HTTPS. If the server supports HTTPS, simply changing the URL from `http://` to `https://` should suffice.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- BrowserModule\n- Routing\n- TranslateModule\n- CookieModule\n- ReactiveFormsModule\n- GalleryModule\n- NgxTextDiffModule\n- QrCodeModule\n- FileUploadModule\n- ClipboardModule\n- MatToolbarModule\n- MatIconModule\n- FormsModule\n- MatFormFieldModule\n- MatSelectModule\n- MatButtonModule\n- MatSidenavModule\n- MatRippleModule\n- MatTableModule\n- MatPaginatorModule\n- MatCardModule\n- MatInputModule\n- MatCheckboxModule\n- MatDialogModule\n- MatDividerModule\n- MatDatepickerModule\n- MatNativeDateModule\n- MatExpansionModule\n- MatProgressBarModule\n- MatTooltipModule\n- MatMenuModule\n- MatListModule\n- MatButtonToggleModule\n- LayoutModule\n- MatGridListModule\n- MatBadgeModule\n- MatRadioModule\n- MatSnackBarModule\n- MatSliderModule\n- MatTabsModule\n- MatSlideToggleModule\n- MatChipsModule\n- MatAutocompleteModule\n- HighlightModule\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-319",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-319"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "76830c1d-2f39-3444-aba8-a5894d7a6ef0",
              "name": "Exclusion of Unreadable Files from Scans Due to High False Positive Rate; Recommendation for Storage in CDN or Cloud Buckets",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"Exclusion of Unreadable Files from Scans Due to High False Positive Rate; Recommendation for Storage in CDN or Cloud Buckets\" refers to a situation where certain files are excluded from security scans due to their unreadability, leading to a high rate of false positives. This can occur in JavaScript programming when certain file types or encodings are not properly handled by the scanning tool. As a result, these files are excluded from the scan, potentially allowing vulnerabilities to go undetected. \n\nStoring files in a Content Delivery Network (CDN) or cloud buckets is often recommended as a mitigation strategy. This is because these platforms often have built-in security measures, including regular and comprehensive file scanning, that can help to reduce the risk of vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Ensure that all files, regardless of type or encoding, are included in security scans.\n2. Use a scanning tool that is capable of reading and analyzing all file types and encodings used in your project.\n3. Regularly update and patch your scanning tools to ensure they are capable of detecting the latest vulnerabilities.\n4. Consider storing files in a CDN or cloud bucket to take advantage of their built-in security measures.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code snippet does not contain any specific vulnerability sink related to the described vulnerability. It is a list of imported modules in an Angular application. Therefore, no specific source code fix can be recommended based on the provided code.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- CookieModule\n- ReactiveFormsModule\n- GalleryModule\n- NgxTextDiffModule\n- QrCodeModule\n- FileUploadModule\n- ClipboardModule\n- MatToolbarModule\n- MatIconModule\n- FormsModule\n- MatFormFieldModule\n- MatSelectModule\n- MatButtonModule\n- MatSidenavModule\n- MatRippleModule\n- MatTableModule\n- MatPaginatorModule\n- MatCardModule\n- MatInputModule\n- MatCheckboxModule\n- MatDialogModule\n- MatDividerModule\n- MatDatepickerModule\n- MatNativeDateModule\n- MatExpansionModule\n- MatProgressBarModule\n- MatTooltipModule\n- MatMenuModule\n- MatListModule\n- MatButtonToggleModule\n- LayoutModule\n- MatGridListModule\n- MatBadgeModule\n- MatRadioModule\n- MatSnackBarModule\n- MatSliderModule\n- MatTabsModule\n- MatSlideToggleModule\n- MatChipsModule\n- MatAutocompleteModule\n- HighlightModule\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)](https://cwe.mitre.org/)\n\nPlease note that the specific CWE for this vulnerability could not be identified as the provided information does not match any specific CWE."
              },
