            {
              "id": "5168c287-ee12-387a-8797-0c43a8eca11b",
              "name": "\"Hard-Coded Secret in Application Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nHard-Coded Secret in Application Vulnerability refers to the practice of embedding sensitive information, such as passwords, encryption keys, and database connection strings, directly into the source code. This is a common vulnerability in many programming languages, including JavaScript. This practice is insecure because it exposes sensitive information to anyone who has access to the source code. If the source code is ever exposed, either through a version control system or by being published on a public repository, the hard-coded secrets can be easily discovered and exploited.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nThe best way to mitigate this vulnerability is to avoid hard-coding secrets in your source code. Instead, use environment variables or a secure configuration management system to store and retrieve sensitive information. This way, the sensitive information is not exposed in the source code and can be managed securely and separately from the application code.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of hard-coding the database connection information, you can use environment variables to store this information. Here is an example of how you can do this in JavaScript using the Sequelize library:\n\n```javascript\nconst { DB_NAME, DB_USER, DB_PASSWORD } = process.env;\n\nconst sequelize = new Sequelize(DB_NAME, DB_USER, DB_PASSWORD, {\n  // other options...\n});\n```\n\nIn this example, the database connection information is stored in environment variables `DB_NAME`, `DB_USER`, and `DB_PASSWORD`. These environment variables can be set in your server environment or in a `.env` file that is not committed to your version control system.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 - 2017: A2-Broken Authentication](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A2_2017-Broken_Authentication)\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-798",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-798"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d58a04c7-c2ca-3a9b-a5e2-c2db45e4058e",
              "name": "Sensitive Data Exposure in API Response",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSensitive Data Exposure in API Response is a vulnerability that occurs when an application does not adequately protect sensitive information from being disclosed in API responses. In JavaScript, this can happen when sensitive data such as authentication tokens, user emails, or other personally identifiable information (PII) is included in the response body of an API call.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is returning a JSON object that includes an authentication token, a basket id, and a user email. This could potentially expose sensitive user information to unauthorized parties, especially if the API response is not properly secured.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Minimize the amount of sensitive data that is included in API responses. Only return the data that is absolutely necessary for the client to function.\n2. Implement proper access controls to ensure that only authorized users can access sensitive data.\n3. Use secure communication protocols such as HTTPS to encrypt the data in transit.\n4. Regularly review and update your application's security settings to keep up with the latest threats and vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, you could remove the `token` and `umail` from the response body to reduce the amount of sensitive data that is exposed:\n\n```javascript\nres.json({ authentication: { bid: basket.id } })\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet appears to be using the Express.js library for handling HTTP requests and responses. Therefore, the Express.js library would be required for this code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP API Security: Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-api-security/)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0850db20-7ad6-3f31-8fb3-7d357be82d0c",
              "name": "Sensitive Data Exposure in API Response",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSensitive Data Exposure in API Response is a security vulnerability that occurs when an application unintentionally exposes sensitive data in its responses. This can happen when an application does not properly protect sensitive data, such as user credentials, personal information, or internal system details, in its responses. In the provided JavaScript code, the `secret` and `setupToken` are being exposed in the API response, which could potentially be exploited by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid sending sensitive data in API responses unless absolutely necessary. If you must send sensitive data, ensure it is properly encrypted and that the client is authorized to receive it. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, you should remove the `secret` and `setupToken` from the API response. If these values are necessary for the client, consider implementing a secure method of retrieving them, such as through a secure, authenticated endpoint.\n\n```javascript\nres.json({\n    setup: false,\n    email: user.email\n})\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to be using the Express.js library for handling HTTP responses, and a custom `security` library for generating a setup token. The exact dependencies would depend on the rest of the application's code.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP API Security: Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-api-security/)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
