              "id": "287fb8f2-cc9c-3f3b-bf2b-f3f1fc1f22cf",
              "name": "NoSQL Injection Vulnerability in findOne() Function Due to Untrusted User Input",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability is a NoSQL Injection vulnerability in the `findOne()` function due to untrusted user input. This occurs when the application uses user-supplied input to construct a NoSQL query without properly sanitizing the input first. An attacker can manipulate the query, leading to unauthorized data exposure, data modification, or even data deletion.\n\nIn the provided code, the `id` parameter from the request is directly used in the `findOne()` function without any validation or sanitization. This makes the application vulnerable to NoSQL injection attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize user inputs: Ensure that the user input matches the expected format. Use a whitelist of acceptable inputs.\n\n2. Use parameterized queries or prepared statements: This ensures that user input is always treated as literal data and not part of the SQL command.\n\n3. Limit the privileges of the database account used by the application: This can limit the potential damage.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe `id` parameter should be validated and sanitized before being used in the `findOne()` function. Here is a simple fix using a regular expression to ensure that the `id` is a number:\n\n```javascript\nconst id = req.params.id\nif (!/^\\d+$/.test(id)) {\n  return res.status(400).send('Invalid id');\n}\nBasketModel.findOne({ where: { id }, include: [{ model: ProductModel, paranoid: false, as: 'Products' }] })\n  // rest of the code\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: For handling HTTP requests and responses.\n- Sequelize: For interacting with the database.\n- A NoSQL database driver: Depending on the database being used.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Query Parameterization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Query_Parameterization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
