            {
              "id": "8c279b38-2e5e-314a-99e7-ac0cd262d3bd",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the promise returned by `BasketModel.findOne()` is not properly handled in case of rejection. If the promise is rejected, the error is caught and passed to an empty error handler, which does nothing with it. This can lead to unhandled promise rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always include error handling logic when working with promises. This can be done by chaining a `.catch()` block to the promise, which will be executed if the promise is rejected. The error handler should take appropriate action based on the nature of the error, such as logging the error for debugging purposes, or returning a user-friendly error message.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nBasketModel.findOne({ where: { id }, include: [{ model: ProductModel, paranoid: false, as: 'Products' }] })\n  .then((basket: BasketModel | null) => {\n    // existing code...\n  })\n  .catch((error: Error) => {\n    console.error(error); // log the error\n    res.status(500).json({ error: 'An error occurred while processing your request.' }); // return a user-friendly error message\n  });\n```\n\nIn the above code, if the promise is rejected, the error is logged to the console and a user-friendly error message is returned in the response.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to be using the following libraries:\n\n- Sequelize (for the `findOne()` method)\n- Express (for the `res.json()` method)\n- Possibly a localization library (for the `req.__()` method)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_security_cheat_sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "54c3da41-a5ed-3dac-93f8-af7f6e24bccd",
              "name": "Sensitive Data Exposure in API Response",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSensitive Data Exposure in API Response is a security vulnerability that occurs when an application unintentionally exposes sensitive information in its responses. This can happen when an application does not properly protect sensitive data, such as credit card numbers, social security numbers, or user passwords. In JavaScript, this can occur when data is returned from an API without proper sanitization or filtering.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always validate, sanitize, and filter the data before sending it in the response.\n2. Use HTTPS for all API communication to ensure data is encrypted during transit.\n3. Implement proper access controls to ensure only authorized users can access sensitive data.\n4. Avoid exposing sensitive data in API responses unless absolutely necessary.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `utils.queryResultToJson(basket)` function is converting the query result to JSON and sending it in the response. If the `basket` contains sensitive data, it will be exposed in the API response.\n\nA potential fix would be to filter out the sensitive data from the `basket` before converting it to JSON. Here's an example:\n\n```javascript\nlet safeBasket = basket.map(item => {\n  return {\n    id: item.id,\n    name: item.name,\n    // other non-sensitive properties\n  };\n});\n\nres.json(utils.queryResultToJson(safeBasket));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not provide enough context to determine the exact library dependencies. However, it's likely that it requires:\n\n- A web framework like Express.js for handling HTTP requests and responses.\n- A utility library or custom utility functions for converting query results to JSON.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP API Security: Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-api-security/)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
