            {
              "id": "4bdd6bfb-3bb0-39aa-98a9-da620b21c903",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the promise returned by `basketItemInstance.save()` is not properly handled. If the promise is rejected, the error is caught and passed to a catch block, but nothing is done with the error.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always have error handling mechanisms in place for promises. This can be done using `.catch()` blocks or by using `try/catch` with async/await. In the catch block, you should handle the error appropriately, which could include logging the error, returning a generic error message to the user, or retrying the operation, depending on the specific use case.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nbasketItemInstance.save().then((addedBasketItem: BasketItemModel) => {\n  res.json({ status: 'success', data: addedBasketItem })\n}).catch((error: Error) => {\n  console.error(error);\n  res.status(500).json({ status: 'error', message: 'An error occurred while saving the basket item.' });\n});\n```\n\nIn this fix, if an error occurs, it is logged to the console and a generic error message is returned to the user.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to use the following libraries:\n\n- A promise-based library for database operations (e.g., Sequelize, Mongoose)\n- Express.js for handling HTTP requests and responses\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_security_cheat_sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d3796c6b-3cd3-3f39-88c3-443569550e75",
              "name": "\"Exposure of Error Details Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nExposure of Error Details Vulnerability in JavaScript refers to the situation where an application reveals too much information in its error messages. This can lead to potential security risks as attackers can use this information to exploit the system. For instance, if the error message reveals details about the internal workings of the application, such as file paths, server names, or other backend information, it can provide a roadmap for an attacker.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is sending a JSON response with the status of the operation and the data of the added basket item. If an error occurs during this operation, the error details might be included in the response, exposing sensitive information.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to handle errors properly and avoid sending detailed error information in the response. Instead, log the detailed error information server-side where it can be reviewed by developers or administrators. Send a generic error message in the response to the client.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\ntry {\n    // code that might throw an error\n    res.json({ status: 'success', data: addedBasketItem });\n} catch (error) {\n    // log the error server-side\n    console.error(error);\n    // send a generic error message in the response\n    res.json({ status: 'error', message: 'An error occurred' });\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using the Express.js library for handling HTTP requests and responses. Therefore, the Express.js library is required for the code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 - A6:2017-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-209",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-209"
                ]
              }
