            {
              "id": "ceade641-7416-3c40-a33c-8479341be47c",
              "name": "NoSQL Injection Vulnerability in findOne() Function Due to Untrusted User Input",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to NoSQL Injection due to the use of untrusted user input in the `findOne()` function. This function is used to retrieve a single document from a NoSQL database. If the input to this function is not properly sanitized, an attacker can manipulate the query to retrieve unauthorized data, modify data, or even delete data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to validate and sanitize all user inputs before using them in database queries. This can be achieved by using input validation libraries such as `validator.js` or `joi`. Additionally, use parameterized queries or prepared statements to ensure that user input is treated as literal values and not part of the SQL command.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nimport { check, validationResult } from 'express-validator';\n\napp.use([\n  check('id').isInt(),\n  // other validations...\n], (req, res, next) => {\n  const errors = validationResult(req);\n  if (!errors.isEmpty()) {\n    return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() });\n  }\n  next();\n});\n\nBasketItemModel.findOne({ where: { id: req.params.id } }).then((item: BasketItemModel | null) => {\n  // rest of the code...\n});\n```\n\nIn the above code, `express-validator` is used to validate that `id` is an integer. If the validation fails, an error response is sent back to the client.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- Express.js\n- Sequelize (or any other ORM library)\n- express-validator (for the fix)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Node.js Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "affd899b-1927-3d38-adb4-2b3595620904",
              "name": "\"Crypto.pseudoRandomBytes()/Math.random() exhibits cryptographic weakness.\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `Math.random()` function in JavaScript is not suitable for generating cryptographic secrets. The random values it generates are predictable and can be reproduced if the initial seed value used by the algorithm is known. This makes it a weak choice for generating cryptographic keys, nonces, or any other type of secret that needs to be unpredictable.\n\nThe `crypto.pseudoRandomBytes()` function in Node.js is a better choice for generating cryptographic secrets, but it still has some weaknesses. It uses a pseudorandom number generator (PRNG) which is not truly random and can be predictable if the initial seed value is known.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse a function designed for generating cryptographic secrets. In Node.js, the `crypto.randomBytes()` function is a good choice. It generates random bytes using a cryptographically strong random number generator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of `Math.random()` with `crypto.randomBytes()`. Here's how you can generate a random integer between 1 and 10 using `crypto.randomBytes()`:\n\n```javascript\nconst crypto = require('crypto');\n\nconst firstTerm = crypto.randomBytes(1)[0] % 10 + 1;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `crypto` module, which is built into Node.js. No additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Insecure Randomness](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Insecure_Randomness)\n- [CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-330",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-330"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "16b49f9b-c7ea-3367-a68a-0e8e0e34f5d1",
              "name": "\"Crypto.pseudoRandomBytes()/Math.random() exhibits cryptographic weakness.\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `Math.random()` function in JavaScript is not suitable for generating cryptographically secure random numbers. This is because it uses a pseudorandom number generator with a relatively small period, which means that after a certain number of outputs, it will start to repeat the same sequence of numbers. This makes it predictable and therefore insecure for cryptographic purposes.\n\nThe `crypto.pseudoRandomBytes()` function in Node.js is also not suitable for generating cryptographically secure random numbers. This is because it uses a pseudorandom number generator that is not guaranteed to be cryptographically secure. This means that the numbers it generates may be predictable, which is a serious security risk in a cryptographic context.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use a function that is designed to generate cryptographically secure random numbers. In Node.js, you can use the `crypto.randomBytes()` function, which uses a cryptographically secure pseudorandom number generator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can modify the code to use `crypto.randomBytes()`:\n\n```javascript\nconst crypto = require('crypto');\n\nconst secondTerm = Math.floor((crypto.randomBytes(1).readUInt8() / 256) * 10) + 1;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `crypto` module, which is a built-in module in Node.js. Therefore, no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-338",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html"
