            {
              "id": "b0ac8473-c11f-3c8c-9e1d-b0a56167105c",
              "name": "\"Cryptographic Weakness in Crypto.pseudoRandomBytes()/Math.random Random Number Generator\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `Math.random()` function in JavaScript is not suitable for generating cryptographically secure random numbers. This is because it uses a pseudorandom number generator with a relatively small period, which means that it can produce predictable sequences of numbers if an attacker can observe enough outputs. Similarly, the `crypto.pseudoRandomBytes()` function in Node.js is also not suitable for generating cryptographically secure random numbers, as it uses a pseudorandom number generator that is not guaranteed to be secure.\n\nThis can lead to a variety of security vulnerabilities, depending on how the random numbers are used. For example, if they are used as session identifiers, an attacker could predict future session identifiers and hijack sessions. If they are used as cryptographic keys, an attacker could predict the keys and decrypt encrypted data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a function that generates cryptographically secure random numbers. In Node.js, you can use the `crypto.randomBytes()` function. In the browser, you can use the `window.crypto.getRandomValues()` function.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the code:\n\n```javascript\nconst crypto = require('crypto');\n\nconst thirdTerm = parseInt(crypto.randomBytes(1).toString('hex'), 16) % 10 + 1;\n```\n\nThis code generates a cryptographically secure random byte, converts it to a hexadecimal string, parses it as an integer, takes the remainder when divided by 10 to get a number between 0 and 9, and then adds 1 to get a number between 1 and 10.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `crypto` module, which is built into Node.js, so no additional library dependencies are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Insecure Randomness](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Insecure_Randomness)\n- [CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html)"
              },
