            {
              "id": "ea24a4a0-4f86-3fa5-9804-bf29e62d6e7f",
              "name": "\"Crypto.pseudoRandomBytes()/Math.random() exhibits cryptographic weakness.\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `Math.random()` function in JavaScript is not suitable for generating cryptographically secure random numbers. This is because it is a pseudorandom number generator that is not designed to be unpredictable and can be vulnerable to attacks. An attacker could potentially predict the output of `Math.random()` and use this information to compromise the security of the application.\n\nThe `crypto.pseudoRandomBytes()` function in Node.js is also not suitable for generating cryptographically secure random numbers. It is a pseudorandom number generator that uses a deterministic algorithm and can be vulnerable to attacks. An attacker could potentially predict the output of `crypto.pseudoRandomBytes()` and use this information to compromise the security of the application.\n\n## Mitigation\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a function that generates cryptographically secure random numbers. In Node.js, you can use the `crypto.randomBytes()` function. This function generates random bytes using a cryptographically strong random number generator.\n\n## Code Fix\n\nHere is how you can fix the code:\n\n```javascript\nconst crypto = require('crypto');\n\nconst firstOperator = operators[crypto.randomBytes(1).readUInt8(0) % 3];\n```\n\nThis code generates a random index for the `operators` array using `crypto.randomBytes()`. The `readUInt8(0)` method reads a byte from the generated random bytes and returns a number between 0 and 255. The `% 3` operation ensures that the result is a number between 0 and 2, which are valid indices for the `operators` array.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `crypto` module, which is a built-in module in Node.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Insecure Randomness](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Insecure_Randomness)\n- [CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html)"
              },
