            {
              "id": "0e9bb4fd-be5a-3cea-9caa-e72340b4c654",
              "name": "\"Bracket Notation Object Injection Vulnerability through Math.floor((Math.random() * 3))\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Bracket Notation Object Injection Vulnerability through Math.floor((Math.random() * 3))\" is a potential security vulnerability in JavaScript. It arises when user-supplied input is used to construct property accessors using bracket notation. This can lead to an attacker being able to inject properties into objects and potentially execute arbitrary code.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `Math.floor((Math.random() * 3))` is used to generate a random index to access an element from the `operators` array. If the `operators` array is somehow influenced by user input, this could lead to an object injection vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using user-supplied input to construct property accessors. If this is unavoidable, the input should be properly sanitized and validated to ensure it does not contain malicious code. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming `operators` is an array of known safe values, the code is not directly vulnerable. However, if `operators` can be influenced by user input, you should validate and sanitize the input. Here is a simple example of how you might do this:\n\n```javascript\nlet index = Math.floor((Math.random() * 3));\nif (Number.isInteger(index) && index >= 0 && index < operators.length) {\n  const secondOperator = operators[index];\n} else {\n  throw new Error('Invalid index');\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if the index is an integer, and then if it falls within the valid range of indices for the `operators` array. If it does not, an error is thrown.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [CWE-74: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Output Used by a Downstream Component ('Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/74.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
