            {
              "id": "8b7d92a5-d999-332e-8892-9279901e7181",
              "name": "Sensitive Data Exposure in API Response",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSensitive Data Exposure in API Response is a vulnerability that occurs when an application does not adequately protect sensitive information from being disclosed in API responses. In JavaScript, this can happen when sensitive data is included in the response object without proper sanitization or encryption. This can lead to unauthorized access to sensitive data such as user credentials, personal information, or other confidential data.\n\nIn the given code snippet `res.json(captcha)`, if the `captcha` object contains sensitive data, it will be exposed in the API response.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n- Always sanitize and validate data before sending it in an API response.\n- Encrypt sensitive data before sending it in an API response.\n- Implement proper access controls to ensure that only authorized users can access sensitive data.\n- Use HTTPS for all API requests to ensure data is encrypted in transit.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf `captcha` object contains sensitive data, you should sanitize or encrypt it before sending it in the API response. Here is an example of how you can do this:\n\n```javascript\nconst crypto = require('crypto');\n\n// Encrypt sensitive data\nfunction encrypt(text) {\n  const cipher = crypto.createCipher('aes-256-cbc', 'encryptionKey');\n  let encrypted = cipher.update(text, 'utf8', 'hex');\n  encrypted += cipher.final('hex');\n  return encrypted;\n}\n\n// Sanitize and encrypt sensitive data before sending it in the API response\nconst sanitizedCaptcha = {\n  ...captcha,\n  sensitiveData: encrypt(captcha.sensitiveData),\n};\n\nres.json(sanitizedCaptcha);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `crypto`: This is a built-in Node.js module for cryptographic functionality.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP API Security: Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-api-security/)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "19ac5e02-c113-3e4c-8274-65ad2ad9a416",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected, but this rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the vulnerability lies in the `.catch()` block. The `error` object is not used, and no action is taken when a Promise rejection occurs.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always handle Promise rejections. This can be done by adding a `.catch()` block to the Promise chain, and taking appropriate action within this block. This might involve logging the error, retrying the operation, or sending a response to the client indicating that an error occurred.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nCaptchaModel.findOne({ where: { captchaId: req.body.captchaId } }).then((captcha: Captcha | null) => {\n    if ((captcha != null) && req.body.captcha === captcha.answer) {\n      next()\n    } else {\n      res.status(401).send(res.__('Wrong answer to CAPTCHA. Please try again.'))\n    }\n  }).catch((error: Error) => {\n    console.error(error);\n    res.status(500).send(res.__('An error occurred. Please try again.'))\n  });\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `error` object is logged to the console, and a response is sent to the client indicating that an error occurred.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to require the following library dependencies:\n\n- A database ORM library that provides the `findOne()` method, such as Sequelize.\n- An Express.js-like library that provides the `req`, `res`, and `next` objects.\n- A library that provides the `Captcha` model and `CaptchaModel` object.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "26d3ea80-a122-3c8e-b7f0-c090868cee60",
              "name": "NoSQL Injection Vulnerability in findOne() Function Due to Untrusted User Input",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to NoSQL Injection due to the use of untrusted user input in the `findOne()` function. This function is used to retrieve a single document from a MongoDB collection that matches a specified filter. If the filter is built using untrusted user input, an attacker can manipulate the query, potentially gaining unauthorized access to sensitive data or performing other malicious activities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to validate, sanitize, or escape user inputs before using them in a query. This can prevent an attacker from injecting malicious code into the query. Additionally, using parameterized queries or prepared statements can also help to prevent NoSQL Injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nimport express from 'express';\nimport { body, validationResult } from 'express-validator';\n\nconst app = express();\n\napp.post('/captcha', [\n  body('captchaId').isString().escape(),\n  body('captcha').isString().escape()\n], (req, res, next) => {\n  const errors = validationResult(req);\n  if (!errors.isEmpty()) {\n    return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() });\n  }\n\n  CaptchaModel.findOne({ where: { captchaId: req.body.captchaId } }).then((captcha: Captcha | null) => {\n    if ((captcha != null) && req.body.captcha === captcha.answer) {\n      next()\n    } else {\n      res.status(401).send(res.__('Wrong answer to CAPTCHA. Please try again.'))\n    }\n  }).catch((error: Error) => {\n    next(error)\n  })\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `express`: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- `express-validator`: An Express middleware for input validation.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-943: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Data Query Logic](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-943",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-943"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "00735695-7005-3a1d-ae05-41a39ecfbd7a",
              "name": "\"Vulnerability to Timing Attacks in String Comparisons with '===', '!==', '!=' and '==' \"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn JavaScript, the `===`, `!==`, `!=` and `==` operators are used for string comparisons. However, these operators can be vulnerable to timing attacks. A timing attack is a side channel attack in which the attacker attempts to compromise a cryptosystem by analyzing the time taken to execute cryptographic algorithms. \n\nIn the context of string comparisons, an attacker can potentially determine the correct value of a string (such as a password or token) by measuring the time it takes for the system to compare the attacker's input with the correct value. This is because these comparison operators compare the strings character by character and stop as soon as they find a difference.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use a constant-time string comparison function. This type of function will always take the same amount of time to compare two strings, regardless of their content. This makes it impossible for an attacker to gain information about the strings by measuring the time it takes to compare them.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple constant-time string comparison function in JavaScript:\n\n```javascript\nfunction constantTimeStringCompare(a, b) {\n    if (a.length !== b.length) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    let result = 0;\n    for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {\n        result |= a.charCodeAt(i) ^ b.charCodeAt(i);\n    }\n    return result === 0;\n}\n```\n\nYou can use this function to compare strings in a way that is not vulnerable to timing attacks:\n\n```javascript\nif (!constantTimeStringCompare(token, null)) {\n    next(new Error('Blocked illegal activity by ' + connection.remoteAddress));\n    return;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-208: Observable Timing Discrepancy](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/208.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-208",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/208.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-208"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "05e644ff-2390-3ff7-8f87-f4af99d9b3b9",
              "name": "Sensitive Data Exposure in API Response",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSensitive Data Exposure in API Response is a vulnerability that occurs when an application does not adequately protect sensitive information. In JavaScript, this can occur when an API endpoint returns a JSON object containing sensitive user data. This can include information such as passwords, credit card numbers, or personally identifiable information (PII) like social security numbers. If an attacker is able to intercept this data, they can use it for malicious purposes such as identity theft or fraud.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should ensure that sensitive data is not included in API responses unless absolutely necessary. If it is necessary, the data should be properly encrypted. You should also implement proper access controls to ensure that only authorized users can access sensitive data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code example, the `res.json({ user })` line is potentially exposing sensitive user data. A better approach would be to only include the necessary user data in the response. For example:\n\n```javascript\nres.json({\n  id: user.id,\n  name: user.name,\n  email: user.email\n})\n```\n\nIn this example, only the user's id, name, and email are included in the response. Any sensitive data, such as passwords or credit card numbers, are not included.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example requires the `express` library to create the API endpoint and handle the response.\n\n```bash\nnpm install express\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 - A3:2017-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "640435a1-908f-3666-b066-70c5001feb08",
              "name": "Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is vulnerable to XSS because it's directly injecting user-controlled data (`username`) into an HTML string without proper sanitization or encoding.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (HTML body, HTML attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) that the data lands. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming `username` is the user-controlled data, you should sanitize it before using it in your HTML string. Here's an example of how you can do this:\n\n```javascript\nlet sanitizedUsername = escapeHtml(username);\nbot.addUser(`${user.id}`, sanitizedUsername);\n```\n\nIn this example, `escapeHtml` is a function that you would need to implement or import from a library to sanitize the input. Here's a simple implementation:\n\n```javascript\nfunction escapeHtml(unsafe) {\n    return unsafe\n         .replace(/&/g, \"&amp;\")\n         .replace(/</g, \"&lt;\")\n         .replace(/>/g, \"&gt;\")\n         .replace(/\"/g, \"&quot;\")\n         .replace(/'/g, \"&#039;\");\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not indicate any specific library dependencies.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6cde1363-8262-39cc-89bb-262ca455a60e",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the user-controlled data (`user.id`) is directly used in the `bot.greet` function and then sent as a response. If the `bot.greet` function does not properly sanitize the input, it could lead to a potential XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (HTML body, HTML attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) that the data lands. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nconst escape = require('escape-html');\n\nres.status(200).json({\n    action: 'response',\n    body: bot.greet(escape(`${user.id}`))\n});\n```\n\nIn the above code, we are using the `escape-html` library to escape any potentially harmful characters in the `user.id` string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- express: Web application framework for Node.js\n- escape-html: Escape string for use in HTML\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
