            {
              "id": "e103da5c-437c-351e-9e79-73061bb86bd7",
              "name": "Unfiltered Database Query Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnfiltered Database Query Vulnerability, also known as SQL Injection, is a type of vulnerability where an attacker can inject malicious SQL code into a query. This can lead to unauthorized access to sensitive data, data corruption, or even data loss. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when user input is used directly in a database query without proper sanitization or parameterization.\n\nIn the provided code, the `ChallengeModel.findAll` function is vulnerable to SQL Injection because it directly uses user input in the `where` clause without any sanitization or parameterization.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should never trust user input and always sanitize or parameterize it before using it in a database query. This can be done using prepared statements, parameterized queries, or ORM libraries that automatically handle these issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nconst Op = Sequelize.Op;\nconst userStatus = getUserStatus(); // Get user status from a trusted source\n\nif (typeof userStatus !== 'number' || isNaN(userStatus)) {\n    throw new Error('Invalid user status');\n}\n\nconst challenges = await ChallengeModel.findAll({\n    where: {\n        codingChallengeStatus: {\n            [Op.gte]: userStatus\n        }\n    }\n});\n```\n\nIn the fixed code, we first get the user status from a trusted source and validate it. If it's not a valid number, we throw an error. Then, we use this validated user status in the `where` clause.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- Sequelize\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "20feae01-3b8e-3e79-95f2-5bd88e45e256",
              "name": "Sensitive Data Exposure in API Response",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSensitive Data Exposure in API Response is a vulnerability that occurs when an application does not adequately protect sensitive information from being disclosed in API responses. In JavaScript, this can occur when sensitive data is included in the response object without proper encryption or other protective measures. This can lead to unauthorized access to sensitive data, such as user credentials, personal information, or other confidential data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n- Never include sensitive data in API responses unless absolutely necessary.\n- Always encrypt sensitive data that must be included in API responses.\n- Implement proper access controls to ensure that only authorized users can access sensitive data.\n- Use secure communication protocols, such as HTTPS, to protect data in transit.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf the `continueCode` is sensitive data, it should not be sent in the response. If it is necessary to send it, it should be encrypted. Here is an example of how to encrypt data using the `crypto` library in Node.js:\n\n```javascript\nconst crypto = require('crypto');\nconst cipher = crypto.createCipher('aes-256-cbc', 'encryptionKey');\nlet encrypted = cipher.update(continueCode, 'utf8', 'hex');\nencrypted += cipher.final('hex');\n\nres.json({ continueCode: encrypted });\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- Crypto: A Node.js core module for cryptography functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP API Security Top 10 2019](https://owasp.org/www-project-api-security/)\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
