            {
              "id": "aedd74f3-19fb-3ed1-b664-db4452410bce",
              "name": "Direct Writing to Response Object May Lead to XSS Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) is a type of injection security vulnerability where an attacker can inject malicious scripts into webpages viewed by other users. In JavaScript, a common vulnerability is direct writing to the response object, which can lead to XSS attacks. This happens when user input is directly written to the response object without proper sanitization or encoding.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `res.send(countryMapping)`, the `countryMapping` variable is directly written to the response object. If `countryMapping` contains user input, this could potentially lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always sanitize and/or encode user input before writing it to the response object. This can be done using various methods, such as using a library that automatically encodes output, or manually encoding user input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix using the `escape-html` library to encode the user input:\n\n```javascript\nvar escape = require('escape-html');\nres.send(escape(countryMapping));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `escape-html` library is required for the code example to execute properly.\n\n```bash\nnpm install escape-html\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Node.js Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
