            {
              "id": "8443c841-392f-3fcd-b0c8-7f5cb329fee1",
              "name": "Sensitive Data Exposure in API Response",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSensitive Data Exposure in API Response is a vulnerability that occurs when an application does not adequately protect sensitive information from being disclosed in API responses. This can include information such as passwords, credit card numbers, health records, and personal data. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when sensitive data is included in an object that is then converted to JSON and sent in the response.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should ensure that sensitive data is not included in the API response. This can be done by carefully controlling what data is included in the object that is converted to JSON. You should also consider using encryption to protect sensitive data, and ensure that your application uses secure communication protocols such as HTTPS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code example, the `discount` object is being converted to JSON and sent in the response. If this object contains sensitive data, it could be exposed. To fix this, you should ensure that the `discount` object does not contain any sensitive data before it is sent in the response.\n\n```javascript\n// Ensure that the discount object does not contain sensitive data\nlet safeDiscount = {\n  amount: discount.amount,\n  code: discount.code\n  // Exclude any sensitive data\n};\n\nreturn res.json({ safeDiscount });\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example requires the `express` library to execute properly.\n\n```bash\nnpm install express\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP API Security: Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-api-security/)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
