            {
              "id": "97e078cb-c5a7-3018-8176-1f3a6cfa3cc2",
              "name": "Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. The \"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\" vulnerability in JavaScript refers to a situation where user-controlled data is directly used in an HTML string. This can allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts and execute them in the user's browser.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `html` variable is sliced and directly used to form a new string. If the `html` variable contains user-controlled data, this can lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always sanitize user-controlled data before using it in HTML strings. This can be done by encoding special characters that have meaning in HTML (like `<`, `>`, `&`, etc.) to their respective HTML entities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided code snippet using the `he` library to encode the HTML string:\n\n```javascript\nconst he = require('he');\nconst sendlfrResponse: string = he.encode(html).slice(0, 100) + '......';\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `he` library is required by the code example to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "278cb5d2-3b11-3b63-aa1a-540cb50d8c41",
              "name": "Unfiltered Database Query Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnfiltered Database Query Vulnerability, also known as SQL Injection, is a type of vulnerability that allows an attacker to inject malicious SQL code into a query. This can lead to unauthorized access to sensitive data, data corruption, or even data loss. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when user-supplied input is used directly in a database query without proper sanitization or parameterization.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `UserId` is taken directly from the request body and used in a database query. If an attacker can control the `UserId` value, they could potentially manipulate the query to return unauthorized data or perform other malicious actions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should never trust user input and always sanitize or parameterize it before using it in a database query. This can be done using prepared statements, parameterized queries, or ORM libraries that automatically handle these concerns.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's how you can fix the vulnerability in the provided code snippet using Sequelize, the ORM library that's likely being used here:\n\n```javascript\nconst userId = parseInt(req.body.UserId, 10);\nif (isNaN(userId)) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    const memories = await MemoryModel.findAll({ where: { UserId: userId } });\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we're parsing the `UserId` value to an integer before using it in the query. If the value can't be parsed to an integer, we handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet likely requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `express`: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- `pg` or `mysql`: PostgreSQL or MySQL client for Node.js, respectively.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
