            {
              "id": "9f5775d6-d8be-3253-9100-b5c98174308c",
              "name": "Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnselected Field Database Query Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a developer does not explicitly specify the fields to be returned in a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data if the query is used to return results to the client. An attacker can potentially gain access to data that they are not supposed to see.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always explicitly specify the fields to be returned in a database query. This can be done using the projection parameter in MongoDB's `find()` method. By doing this, only the specified fields will be returned in the query results, reducing the risk of exposing sensitive data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to specify the fields to be returned in a MongoDB query using the `find()` method:\n\n```javascript\ndb.ordersCollection.find({ email: updatedEmail }, { _id: 0, email: 1, order: 1 }).then((orders: Array<{}>) => {\n  // process orders\n});\n```\n\nIn this example, only the `email` and `order` fields will be returned in the query results. The `_id` field is excluded because it is returned by default, so you need to explicitly exclude it if you don't want it to be returned.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- MongoDB Node.js Driver\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
