            {
              "id": "4edb859c-50d1-3f86-bae3-14b40938decb",
              "name": "NoSQL Injection Vulnerability in findOne() Function Due to Untrusted User Input",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"NoSQL Injection Vulnerability in findOne() Function Due to Untrusted User Input\" is a security vulnerability that occurs when an application uses untrusted user input in a NoSQL query. In this case, the `findOne()` function is used to retrieve a user from the database. The function takes an object as a parameter, which specifies the conditions that the user must meet. If an attacker can control the `UserId` parameter, they can manipulate the query to retrieve any user they want, potentially gaining unauthorized access to sensitive data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize user input before using it in a query. This can prevent an attacker from injecting malicious code into the query. Additionally, you should use parameterized queries or prepared statements, which can ensure that user input is always treated as data, not as part of the query.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nconst userId = req.body.UserId;\nif (typeof userId !== 'string' || !userId.match(/^[0-9a-fA-F]{24}$/)) {\n  throw new Error('Invalid UserId');\n}\nconst user = await UserModel.findOne({ where: { id: userId, role: security.roles.customer } });\n```\n\nIn this version, the `UserId` is first validated to ensure it is a string and matches the format of a MongoDB ObjectId. If it does not, an error is thrown. This prevents an attacker from injecting malicious code into the query.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- Body-parser: Node.js body parsing middleware.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)\n\nPlease note that while CWE-89 specifically refers to SQL injection, the same principles apply to NoSQL injection."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "024601f6-4234-342a-9d7b-44f718da3326",
              "name": "NoSQL Injection Vulnerability in findOne() Function Due to Untrusted User Input",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"NoSQL Injection Vulnerability in findOne() Function Due to Untrusted User Input\" is a security vulnerability that occurs when an application uses untrusted user input in a NoSQL query. In this case, the `findOne()` function is used to query the database with a condition that includes user input (`req.body.UserId`). If an attacker can control the `UserId` parameter, they can potentially manipulate the query to retrieve unauthorized data, modify data, or perform other malicious actions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate, sanitize, and/or escape user input before using it in a database query. This can help prevent an attacker from injecting malicious code into the query.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix using the `express-validator` library to validate the `UserId` parameter:\n\n```javascript\nconst { check, validationResult } = require('express-validator');\n\napp.post('/api/wallet', [\n  check('UserId').isInt(),\n  (req, res) => {\n    const errors = validationResult(req);\n    if (!errors.isEmpty()) {\n      return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() });\n    }\n\n    const wallet = await WalletModel.findOne({ where: { UserId: req.body.UserId } });\n    // ...\n  }\n]);\n```\n\nIn this code, `check('UserId').isInt()` validates that the `UserId` parameter is an integer. If the validation fails, an error response is returned.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `express`: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- `express-validator`: An Express middleware for input validation.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-943: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Data Query Logic](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-943",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
