            {
              "id": "a33404b3-4035-3192-bb6c-62b3238b1278",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, crashes, and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the promise returned by `imageCaptchaInstance.save()` is not properly handled in case of rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that promises have a `.catch()` block to handle any errors that may occur. This allows you to control the application flow even when an error occurs, preventing unexpected behavior and potential security issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nimageCaptchaInstance.save().then(() => {\n  res.json(imageCaptcha)\n}).catch((error) => {\n  console.error(error);\n  // Handle the error appropriately here\n});\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `.catch()` block is used to catch any errors that may occur when the promise is rejected. The error is then logged to the console and can be handled appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not specify any library dependencies. However, it seems to be using some form of promise-based library or functionality, such as native JavaScript promises or a library like Bluebird.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
