            {
              "id": "8562e028-6d0a-319e-b913-fa2b1512b2fe",
              "name": "Sensitive Data Exposure in API Response",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSensitive Data Exposure in API Response is a security vulnerability that occurs when an application unintentionally exposes sensitive data in its API responses. This can include personal identifiable information (PII), financial data, or any other data that should be kept private. In JavaScript, this can occur when using the `res.json()` function to send data back to the client.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n- Always validate and sanitize input data.\n- Limit the data that you send back to the client. Only send what is necessary.\n- Use access controls to ensure that only authorized users can access sensitive data.\n- Encrypt sensitive data.\n\n## Code Fix\n\nIf `imageCaptcha` contains sensitive data, you should remove this data before sending it back to the client. Here's an example of how you can do this:\n\n```javascript\nlet safeImageCaptcha = {...imageCaptcha};\ndelete safeImageCaptcha.sensitiveData;\nres.json(safeImageCaptcha);\n```\n\nIn this example, `sensitiveData` is the property that contains sensitive data. Replace it with the actual property name in your code.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example uses the `express` library. To install it, use the following command:\n\n```\nnpm install express\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP API Security: Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-api-security/)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
