            {
              "id": "04565498-699b-3e43-8bd2-2c45f8d5f69b",
              "name": "Unfiltered Database Query Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnfiltered Database Query Vulnerability, also known as SQL Injection, is a common security vulnerability that occurs when an application does not properly validate user input before using it in a database query. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when using libraries such as Sequelize to interact with a database. If user input is directly used in a query without proper sanitization or parameterization, an attacker could manipulate the query to perform unauthorized actions on the database.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `UserId` variable is directly used in a Sequelize `findAll` query without any validation or sanitization. This could potentially allow an attacker to manipulate the `UserId` value to alter the query.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always validate and sanitize user input before using it in a database query. Sequelize provides a way to parameterize queries, which can help prevent SQL Injection attacks. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nImageCaptchaModel.findAll({\n    limit: 1,\n    where: {\n      UserId: Sequelize.literal(`UserId = ${Sequelize.escape(UserId)}`),\n      createdAt: {\n        [Op.gt]: new Date(Date.now() - 300000)\n      }\n    },\n    order: [['createdAt', 'DESC']]\n  }).then(captchas => {\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize\n- Sequelize-operators\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "574ad5e1-ad0a-316f-b4ca-52743959579b",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the `catch` block is empty, which means that any errors that occur during the execution of the promise will not be handled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always include error handling mechanisms when working with promises. This can be done by adding a `.catch()` block after your `.then()` block, and including code in the `catch` block to handle any errors that may occur.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's how you could modify the provided code to handle promise rejections:\n\n```javascript\nImageCaptchaModel.findAll({\n    limit: 1,\n    where: {\n      UserId,\n      createdAt: {\n        [Op.gt]: new Date(Date.now() - 300000)\n      }\n    },\n    order: [['createdAt', 'DESC']]\n  }).then(captchas => {\n    if (!captchas[0] || req.body.answer === captchas[0].answer) {\n      next()\n    } else {\n      res.status(401).send(res.__('Wrong answer to CAPTCHA. Please try again.'))\n    }\n  }).catch((error) => {\n    console.error(error);\n    res.status(500).send('An error occurred');\n  });\n```\n\nIn this updated code, the `catch` block logs the error and sends a 500 status code with a generic error message.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for the `findAll` method and `Op.gt` operator)\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "53e6cafb-6660-3692-b866-c94005fb1129",
              "name": "NoSQL Injection Vulnerability in findOne() Function Due to Untrusted User Input",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet above is vulnerable to NoSQL injection due to the use of untrusted user input in the `findOne()` function. This function is used to retrieve a single document from a MongoDB collection. The vulnerability arises when the `_id` field, which is directly taken from the user input (`req.body.id`), is used in the `findOne()` function without any sanitization or validation. An attacker can manipulate the input to execute arbitrary queries or modify the database.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to validate and sanitize user inputs before using them in database queries. This can be achieved by using input validation libraries such as `joi` or `express-validator`. Additionally, use parameterized queries or prepared statements to ensure that user input is always treated as data and not executable code.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using the `mongodb` library's `ObjectID` function to validate the `_id` field:\n\n```javascript\nconst { ObjectID } = require('mongodb')\n\n// ...\n\nconst id = req.body.id\nif (!ObjectID.isValid(id)) {\n  return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Invalid ID' })\n}\n\n// ...\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `mongodb`: A MongoDB driver for Node.js.\n- `express`: A web application framework for Node.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html) (Although this CWE is for SQL Injection, the principles are similar for NoSQL Injection)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6b35478d-0482-3d36-98e2-ce0d25be55a6",
              "name": "Potential NoSQL Injection from Unsanitized `req` Argument in `../data/mongodb` Statement",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Potential NoSQL Injection Vulnerability Detected in `../data/mongodb` Statement from `req` Argument\" vulnerability refers to a security flaw in a JavaScript application that uses MongoDB as its database. This vulnerability occurs when the application does not properly sanitize user input, allowing an attacker to manipulate database queries.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is fetching a review from the database using an ID that is directly passed into the query. If an attacker can control the `id` variable, they can potentially manipulate the query to return unintended data, delete data, or perform other malicious actions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always sanitize user input before using it in a database query. This can be done by using prepared statements, parameterized queries, or ORM libraries that automatically sanitize input. You should also use the principle of least privilege for database access, meaning each application should only have the database permissions it needs to function and no more.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn this case, you can use the `ObjectID` function from the `mongodb` library to ensure that the `id` is a valid MongoDB ObjectID:\n\n```javascript\nconst { ObjectID } = require('mongodb');\n\nconst id = new ObjectID(req.params.id);\nconst review = await db.reviewsCollection.findOne({ _id: id });\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `mongodb`: A MongoDB driver for Node.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)\n\nPlease note that while the CWE link refers to SQL Injection, the principles of the vulnerability and its mitigation are the same for NoSQL databases like MongoDB."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5d945c00-163f-3428-8b62-88009c0ab737",
              "name": "Potential NoSQL Injection from Unsanitized `req` Argument in `../data/mongodb` Statement",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nNoSQL Injection is a type of attack where an attacker can inject code into the query that would be executed by the NoSQL database. In the provided code snippet, the `id` variable is directly used in the `findOne` method without any sanitization or validation. This could potentially allow an attacker to manipulate the query, leading to unauthorized data access, data loss, or other security breaches.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to validate and sanitize all inputs that are used in database queries. This can be done using various methods such as using prepared statements, using database functions to escape special characters, or using libraries that provide these functionalities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix using the `mongodb` library's `ObjectID` function to ensure that the `id` is a valid MongoDB ObjectID:\n\n```javascript\nconst { ObjectID } = require('mongodb');\n\n// ...\n\nconst updatedReview: Review = await db.reviewsCollection.findOne({ _id: new ObjectID(id) });\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `mongodb`: A Node.js driver for MongoDB.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-943: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Data Query Logic](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-943",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-943"
                ]
              }
