            {
              "id": "b90b1b17-e52c-33a6-b13a-8fdf782e11e2",
              "name": "Sensitive Data Exposure in API Response",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSensitive Data Exposure in API Response is a vulnerability that occurs when an API unintentionally exposes sensitive information in its responses. This can happen when developers include sensitive data in the response objects without proper consideration of the security implications. In JavaScript, this can occur when using the `res.json()` function to send JSON responses in an Express.js application.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize input data.\n2. Implement proper access controls to ensure that only authorized users can access sensitive data.\n3. Avoid sending sensitive data in API responses unless absolutely necessary.\n4. Encrypt sensitive data when it is necessary to include it in an API response.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf `result` contains sensitive data, you should remove or anonymize this data before sending the response. Here's an example of how you can do this:\n\n```javascript\n// Assuming result is an object with a property 'password'\nlet safeResult = {...result};\ndelete safeResult.password;\nres.json(safeResult);\n```\n\nIn this example, we're creating a copy of `result` and deleting the `password` property before sending the response.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- express\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP API Security: Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-api-security/)\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c549c453-a3ca-322d-9539-89dcb936156f",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the promise returned by `BasketModel.findOrCreate()` is not properly handled. If the promise is rejected, the `.catch()` block will be executed, but it does not contain any code to handle the error, which can lead to unhandled promise rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle promise rejections. This can be done by adding a `.catch()` block to your promises, or by using `try/catch` with `async/await`. The error handling code should log the error and gracefully handle the failure, without exposing sensitive information.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nBasketModel.findOrCreate({ where: { UserId: user.data.id } })\n  .then(([basket]: [BasketModel, boolean]) => {\n    const token = security.authorize(user)\n    user.bid = basket.id // keep track of original basket\n    security.authenticatedUsers.put(token, user)\n    res.json({ authentication: { token, bid: basket.id, umail: user.data.email } })\n  }).catch((error: Error) => {\n    console.error(error);\n    res.status(500).json({ error: 'An error occurred' });\n  });\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `.catch()` block logs the error and sends a generic error response. This prevents the application from crashing and does not expose any sensitive information.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example appears to require the following libraries:\n\n- A database ORM library that provides the `findOrCreate()` method (e.g., Sequelize).\n- A security library that provides the `authorize()` and `authenticatedUsers.put()` methods.\n- An HTTP library that provides the `res.json()` and `res.status().json()` methods (e.g., Express.js).\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0606345e-aeab-3087-8b40-53d2b41ae535",
              "name": "Sensitive Data Exposure in API Response",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSensitive Data Exposure in API Response is a security vulnerability that occurs when an application unintentionally exposes sensitive information in its responses. This can happen when an application does not properly protect sensitive data, such as authentication tokens, user emails, and other personally identifiable information (PII). Attackers can exploit this vulnerability to gain unauthorized access to user accounts or sensitive data.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the application is returning a JSON object that includes an authentication token, a basket id, and a user email. This could potentially expose sensitive data if the API response is intercepted or logged somewhere.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n- Minimize the amount of sensitive data you send in your API responses. Only send what is absolutely necessary.\n- Encrypt sensitive data in transit using HTTPS.\n- Implement proper access controls to ensure that only authorized users can access sensitive data.\n- Use secure methods for storing and transmitting sensitive data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, you should remove the sensitive data from the API response. If the client needs this data, consider other secure methods of providing it. For example:\n\n```javascript\nres.json({ bid: basket.id })\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using the Express.js library for handling HTTP responses. Therefore, the Express.js library is required for this code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP API Security: Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-api-security/)\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "997f2de7-756c-349a-9cfb-09e81cb2de1e",
              "name": "Detected Variable SQL Query: Potential SQL Injection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to SQL Injection, a common security vulnerability that allows an attacker to manipulate SQL queries. This vulnerability occurs when user input is directly included in a SQL query without proper sanitization or parameterization. In this case, the user's email and password are directly included in the SQL query, which could allow an attacker to manipulate the query to gain unauthorized access or retrieve sensitive information.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate SQL Injection vulnerabilities, it is recommended to use parameterized queries or prepared statements. These methods ensure that user input is always treated as literal data and not part of the SQL command. \n\nIn addition, it is also recommended to use a web application firewall (WAF) to detect and block SQL Injection attacks. However, this should be used as an additional layer of security and not as a replacement for secure coding practices.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nmodels.sequelize.query(\n  `SELECT * FROM Users WHERE email = :email AND password = :password AND deletedAt IS NULL`,\n  { \n    replacements: { email: req.body.email || '', password: security.hash(req.body.password || '') },\n    model: UserModel, \n    plain: true \n  }\n) \n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js, used to build web applications and APIs.\n- A security library or function to hash passwords.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "112af544-3de1-392a-8349-90a82e01bf0f",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but this rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the promise returned by `models.sequelize.query` is not properly handled in case of rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always handle promise rejections. This can be done by adding a `.catch()` block to the promise chain, or by using `try/catch` with `async/await`. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, the promise rejection is already being caught. However, the catch block is empty, which means that the error is being swallowed and not handled. A simple fix would be to handle the error in the catch block, for example by logging the error and sending a response to the client:\n\n```javascript\nmodels.sequelize.query(`SELECT * FROM Users WHERE email = '${req.body.email || ''}' AND password = '${security.hash(req.body.password || '')}' AND deletedAt IS NULL`, { model: UserModel, plain: true })\n  .then((authenticatedUser) => {\n    // ...\n  }).catch((error: Error) => {\n    console.error(error);\n    res.status(500).send('An error occurred');\n  });\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- Express: A fast, unopinionated, and flexible Node.js web application framework.\n- A hashing library, such as bcrypt, for `security.hash()`.\n- A utility library, such as lodash, for `utils.queryResultToJson()`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 - 2017: A6 - Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a2ee4096-6e6b-3b8c-8ade-5eaf429e2630",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks. XSS is a type of injection, in which malicious scripts are injected into otherwise benign and trusted websites. XSS attacks occur when an attacker uses a web application to send malicious code, generally in the form of a browser side script, to a different end user.\n\nIn this case, the vulnerability arises from the fact that user-controlled data is directly used in an HTML string without proper sanitization or encoding. This allows an attacker to inject malicious scripts that can be executed in the context of the victim's browser.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always encode user-controlled data before using it in HTML strings. This ensures that any potentially harmful characters are rendered harmless.\n\nAdditionally, you should use parameterized queries or prepared statements to prevent SQL injection attacks. This ensures that user-controlled data is always treated as literal values, not part of the SQL command.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's how you can fix the vulnerability in the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nmodels.sequelize.query(\n  `SELECT * FROM Users WHERE email = :email AND password = :password AND deletedAt IS NULL`,\n  { \n    replacements: { \n      email: req.body.email || '', \n      password: security.hash(req.body.password || '') \n    }, \n    model: UserModel, \n    plain: true \n  }\n)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js, designed for building web applications and APIs.\n- A security library for hashing passwords.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
