            {
              "id": "59d63e3b-7390-3b6d-bb2c-6dffaf66a014",
              "name": "SQL Injection Vulnerability Prevention through Prepared Statements, Parameterized Queries, and ORM Frameworks",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe provided JavaScript code is vulnerable to SQL Injection attacks. This is a type of attack where an attacker can inject malicious SQL code into the query, which can then be executed by the database. This can lead to unauthorized access, data corruption, or even data loss.\n\nIn this specific code snippet, the problem arises from the fact that the user input is directly used in the SQL query without any sanitization or parameterization. This means that an attacker could potentially provide specially crafted input that could modify the SQL query to perform malicious actions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo prevent SQL Injection vulnerabilities, it is recommended to use Prepared Statements, Parameterized Queries, or ORM Frameworks. These techniques ensure that user input is always treated as literal values, not as part of the SQL command.\n\n- **Prepared Statements**: In this method, SQL command is predefined, and then the user input is then passed to it as parameters. This ensures that the user input cannot modify the structure of the SQL command.\n\n- **Parameterized Queries**: Similar to Prepared Statements, Parameterized Queries allow the developer to define all the SQL code, and then pass in each parameter to the query later.\n\n- **ORM Frameworks**: Object-Relational Mapping (ORM) is a technique that lets you interact with your database, like you would with SQL. In other words, it allows you to manipulate your database by using object-oriented programming.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using Sequelize's built-in parameterized query support:\n\n```javascript\nmodels.sequelize.query(\n  `SELECT * FROM Users WHERE email = :email AND password = :password AND deletedAt IS NULL`,\n  { \n    replacements: { email: req.body.email || '', password: security.hash(req.body.password || '') },\n    model: UserModel, \n    plain: true \n  }\n) // vuln-code-snippet vuln-line loginAdminChallenge loginBenderChallenge loginJimChallenge\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- `express`: Fast, unopinionated, minimalist web framework for Node.js\n- A security library that provides the `hash` function. This could be `crypto` or `bcrypt`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
