            {
              "id": "d30e7acb-d1d0-37fc-80a2-94030faa8318",
              "name": "\"Hardcoded Credentials Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nHardcoded Credentials Vulnerability in JavaScript refers to the insecure practice of embedding plaintext credentials directly into the source code. This vulnerability can lead to unauthorized access to the system if the source code is exposed or leaked. In the provided code snippet, the vulnerability does not seem to be present as there are no hardcoded credentials. However, the code is vulnerable to SQL Injection attacks due to the use of string concatenation in forming the SQL query.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using string concatenation to form SQL queries. Instead, use parameterized queries or prepared statements to prevent SQL Injection attacks. Never hardcode sensitive information such as credentials, API keys, or secrets in the source code. Instead, use environment variables or secure vault services to store such information.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nmodels.sequelize.query(\n  `SELECT * FROM Users WHERE email = :email AND password = :password AND deletedAt IS NULL`,\n  { \n    replacements: { email: req.body.email, password: security.hash(req.body.password) },\n    model: UserModel, \n    plain: true \n  }\n) \n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- An express-like request library to provide the `req.body` object.\n- A custom `security` library for hashing the password.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten 2017 A3:2017-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-259: Use of Hard-coded Password](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/259.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-259",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/259.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
