            {
              "id": "626ea329-d1ed-3aeb-9371-013bb01a4d31",
              "name": "Sensitive Data Exposure in API Response",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSensitive Data Exposure in API Response is a vulnerability that occurs when an application does not adequately protect sensitive information. In JavaScript, this can occur when data is sent to the client without proper sanitization or encryption. This can lead to unauthorized access to sensitive data, such as user credentials, credit card information, or personal user information.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the vulnerability lies in the fact that the `orderId` is being sent in the response without any form of protection. If the `orderId` is sensitive information, this could lead to unauthorized access.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, sensitive data should never be sent to the client without proper protection. This can be achieved through various methods, such as:\n\n- Data sanitization: Ensure that the data being sent does not contain any sensitive information.\n- Data encryption: Encrypt the data before sending it to the client. This ensures that even if the data is intercepted, it cannot be read without the decryption key.\n- Use HTTPS: HTTPS ensures that the data is encrypted during transmission, preventing it from being intercepted.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, if the `orderId` is sensitive information, it should not be sent in the response. If it is necessary to send the `orderId`, it should be encrypted before being sent. Here is an example of how this could be done using the `crypto` library:\n\n```javascript\nconst crypto = require('crypto');\n\n// Encrypt the orderId\nconst cipher = crypto.createCipher('aes-256-cbc', 'encryptionKey');\nlet encryptedOrderId = cipher.update(orderId, 'utf8', 'hex');\nencryptedOrderId += cipher.final('hex');\n\nres.json({ orderConfirmation: encryptedOrderId });\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the `express` library to create the server and handle the response. The `crypto` library is also required for the encryption of the `orderId`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 - A3:2017-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7b269ce1-5fda-32a8-b505-b4bc127de9fc",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability from user-controlled data in HTML string exists when an application includes untrusted data in a new HTML string used in a dynamic context. In this case, the application is using template literals to include user-controlled data (`email`) directly into an HTML string. If the `email` variable contains malicious script, it will be executed when the HTML string is rendered by the browser.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape untrusted data based on the HTML context (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) that the data will be placed into. In this case, since the data is being placed into an HTML string, HTML entity encoding should be applied.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nconst escape = require('escape-html');\n\ndoc.font('Times-Roman').fontSize(15).text(`${req.__('Customer')}: ${escape(email)}`, { align: 'left' })\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `escape-html`: A module for escaping HTML strings.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f74c8963-5a7f-372f-bb14-35b7359948b8",
              "name": "XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is using template literals to include user-controlled data (`orderId`) in an HTML string. If an attacker can control the `orderId` value, they could potentially inject malicious scripts, leading to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Use a safe API which avoids the use of the interpreter entirely or provides a parameterized interface.\n- Escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (HTML entity encoding).\n- Apply context-sensitive encoding when modifying the browser document on the client side.\n- Validate, filter or sanitize user-generated inputs.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn this case, you should sanitize the `orderId` before including it in the HTML string. Here's an example using the `escape-html` library:\n\n```javascript\nconst escapeHtml = require('escape-html');\n\nlet sanitizedOrderId = escapeHtml(orderId);\ndoc.font('Times-Roman').fontSize(15).text(`${req.__('Order')} #: ${sanitizedOrderId}`, { align: 'left' });\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `pdfkit` for creating the PDF document.\n- `escape-html` for sanitizing the user input.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://owasp.org/www-community/xss-filter-evasion-cheatsheet)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f9169fb3-7cc4-3792-81c3-053e4d11195c",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability from user-controlled data in HTML string exists in the JavaScript programming language. This vulnerability occurs when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n- Always validate user input: User input should always be validated before it is processed by the application.\n- Escape user input: User input should be properly escaped before it is displayed in the web page.\n- Use secure APIs: Use APIs, such as element.textContent, which automatically escape user input.\n- Use Content Security Policy (CSP): This can help to mitigate the impact of any potential XSS vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the user-controlled data is directly embedded into the HTML string. This can be fixed by properly escaping the user-controlled data before embedding it into the HTML string. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```javascript\nlet dateText = `${req.__('Date')}: ${date}`;\nlet escapedDateText = escape(dateText);\ndoc.font('Times-Roman').fontSize(15).text(escapedDateText, { align: 'left' });\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- PDFKit: A PDF generation library for Node.js.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f44698c6-3034-3ad5-bdab-0b310eb6e488",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the vulnerability arises if the `findOne` or `update` methods of `QuantityModel` reject their promises. While there are `.catch` blocks to handle errors from these methods, they simply pass the error to a `next` function. If this `next` function is not properly implemented to handle errors, or if it is not even a function, an unhandled promise rejection will occur.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that all promises have appropriate error handling mechanisms. This can be done by always including a `.catch` block or a second argument to `.then` for handling rejections. Additionally, make sure that any functions you pass errors to are capable of properly handling them.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nQuantityModel.findOne({ where: { ProductId: BasketItem.ProductId } })\n  .then((product: any) => {\n    const newQuantity = product.quantity - BasketItem.quantity\n    return QuantityModel.update({ quantity: newQuantity }, { where: { ProductId: BasketItem?.ProductId } })\n  })\n  .catch((error: unknown) => {\n    // Handle error appropriately here, e.g. log it and return a response indicating an internal server error\n    console.error(error)\n    res.status(500).send('An error occurred')\n  })\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `update` method's promise is returned from the `then` block, so its rejection will be caught by the `catch` block. The `catch` block has also been updated to log the error and send a response indicating an internal server error.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to use the Sequelize library for interacting with a database.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
