            {
              "id": "275ad4ac-de32-3eae-83f4-79f6bc9278cc",
              "name": "NoSQL Injection Vulnerability in findOne() Function Due to Untrusted User Input",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to NoSQL Injection due to the use of untrusted user input in the `findOne()` function. This function is used to retrieve a single document from a MongoDB collection that matches a specified filter. If the filter is built using untrusted user input, an attacker can manipulate the query, potentially gaining unauthorized access to sensitive data, or modifying data in unintended ways.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to validate, sanitize, or escape user input before using it in a NoSQL query. Avoid using user input directly in queries whenever possible. If user input must be used, ensure it is the correct type and format before including it in a query.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nconst productId = parseInt(BasketItem.ProductId, 10);\n\nif (isNaN(productId)) {\n  next(new Error('Invalid product id'));\n  return;\n}\n\nQuantityModel.findOne({ where: { ProductId: productId } }).then((product: any) => {\n  const newQuantity = product.quantity - BasketItem.quantity\n  QuantityModel.update({ quantity: newQuantity }, { where: { ProductId: productId } }).catch((error: unknown) => {\n    next(error)\n  })\n}).catch((error: unknown) => {\n  next(error)\n})\n```\n\nIn the above code, the `ProductId` is parsed into an integer before being used in the `findOne()` function. If the `ProductId` is not a valid number, an error is returned.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-943: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Data Query Logic](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-943",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-943"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "01f14987-c6a1-34cb-960d-11a2672808b9",
              "name": "NoSQL Injection Vulnerability in findOne() Function Due to Untrusted User Input",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"NoSQL Injection Vulnerability in findOne() Function Due to Untrusted User Input\" is a security vulnerability that occurs when an application uses untrusted user input in a NoSQL query. In this case, the `findOne()` function is used to retrieve a delivery method from the `DeliveryModel` based on the `id` provided in the request body. If an attacker can control the `deliveryMethodId` value, they can potentially manipulate the query to retrieve unauthorized data or perform other malicious actions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize user input before using it in a NoSQL query. This can help prevent NoSQL injection attacks by ensuring that the input does not contain any malicious code. Additionally, you should use parameterized queries or prepared statements whenever possible, as these can help prevent injection attacks by separating the data from the query logic.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the code:\n\n```javascript\nconst deliveryMethodId = req.body.orderDetails.deliveryMethodId;\n\nif (typeof deliveryMethodId !== 'string' || !deliveryMethodId.match(/^[0-9a-fA-F]{24}$/)) {\n  throw new Error('Invalid deliveryMethodId');\n}\n\nconst deliveryMethodFromModel = await DeliveryModel.findOne({ where: { id: deliveryMethodId } });\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first validate that the `deliveryMethodId` is a string and matches the expected format for a MongoDB ObjectId. If the validation fails, we throw an error and do not proceed with the query.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- Mongoose: An Object Data Modeling (ODM) library for MongoDB and Node.js.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)\n- [CWE-943: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Data Query Logic](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-943",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89",
                  "CWE-943"
                ]
              }
