            {
              "id": "1f168a74-6cd7-3fa2-a8ac-6aa5b964f140",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the potential XSS vulnerability arises from the use of template literals to include user-controlled data (`totalPoints`) directly into the HTML string that is being rendered. If `totalPoints` contains malicious script, it will be executed when the HTML string is rendered in the browser.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape user-controlled data before including it in your HTML. This can be done using various methods, such as using a secure server-side or JavaScript-based HTML encoder, or using a contextual output encoding/escaping library.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA simple fix for this specific vulnerability would be to ensure that `totalPoints` is a number before including it in the HTML string. This can be done using the `Number` function in JavaScript:\n\n```javascript\nlet points = Number(totalPoints);\nif (!isNaN(points)) {\n    doc.font('Helvetica-Bold').fontSize(15).text(`${req.__('Bonus Points Earned')}: ${points}`);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example seems to be using the `pdfkit` library for creating PDF documents, and the `i18n` library for internationalization. Therefore, these libraries would be required for the code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "15450362-963f-3112-ae82-b5c8cf05b675",
              "name": "NoSQL Injection Vulnerability in findOne() Function Due to Untrusted User Input",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"NoSQL Injection Vulnerability in findOne() Function Due to Untrusted User Input\" is a security vulnerability that occurs when an application uses untrusted user input in a NoSQL query. In this case, the `findOne()` function is used to query the database with a condition that includes user input (`req.body.UserId`). If an attacker can control the `UserId` parameter, they can potentially manipulate the query to retrieve unauthorized data, modify data, or perform other malicious actions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate, sanitize, and/or escape user input before using it in a database query. This can help prevent an attacker from injecting malicious code into the query.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix using the `express-validator` library to validate the `UserId` parameter:\n\n```javascript\nconst { check, validationResult } = require('express-validator');\n\napp.post('/api/wallet', [\n  check('UserId').isInt(),\n  (req, res) => {\n    const errors = validationResult(req);\n    if (!errors.isEmpty()) {\n      return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() });\n    }\n\n    const wallet = await WalletModel.findOne({ where: { UserId: req.body.UserId } });\n    // ...\n  }\n]);\n```\n\nIn this code, `check('UserId').isInt()` validates that the `UserId` parameter is an integer. If the validation fails, an error response is returned.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `express`: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- `express-validator`: An Express middleware for input validation.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-943: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Data Query Logic](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-943",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-943"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eb776073-e8df-3b7c-bcb6-aba320fbb168",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected, but this rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information or allow for other vulnerabilities to be exploited.\n\nIn the provided code, the promise returned by `db.ordersCollection.insert()` is not being handled for rejection. If the `insert()` operation fails for any reason (e.g., a network error, a database error, etc.), the promise will be rejected, but this rejection will not be caught or handled, leading to an Unhandled Promise Rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle promise rejections. This can be done by attaching a `.catch()` block to the promise, which will be executed if the promise is rejected. In the `.catch()` block, you can handle the error appropriately, such as by logging the error, retrying the operation, or returning a response to the user indicating that the operation failed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's how you can fix the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.ordersCollection.insert({\n  promotionalAmount: discountAmount,\n  paymentId: req.body.orderDetails ? req.body.orderDetails.paymentId : null,\n  addressId: req.body.orderDetails ? req.body.orderDetails.addressId : null,\n  orderId,\n  delivered: false,\n  email: (email ? email.replace(/[aeiou]/gi, '*') : undefined),\n  totalPrice,\n  products: basketProducts,\n  bonus: totalPoints,\n  deliveryPrice: deliveryAmount,\n  eta: deliveryMethod.eta.toString()\n}).then(() => {\n  doc.end()\n}).catch((error) => {\n  console.error('Failed to insert order:', error);\n  // Handle the error appropriately here\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to depend on the following libraries:\n\n- A MongoDB driver or ORM, such as Mongoose, for the `db.ordersCollection.insert()` operation.\n- An HTTP server library, such as Express, for the `req` object.\n- A PDF generation library, such as PDFKit, for the `doc.end()` operation.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-703: Improper Check or Handling of Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/703.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-703",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/703.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-703"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4925b9ea-a6e4-39ef-a952-646138759569",
              "name": "\"Object Injection Vulnerability through Bracket Notation using CouponCode as Key of Campaigns Type\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Object Injection Vulnerability through Bracket Notation using CouponCode as Key of Campaigns Type\" is a type of security vulnerability in JavaScript where an attacker can manipulate the application's logic by injecting malicious input into the object's key. In this case, the `couponCode` is used as a key of the `campaigns` object. If the `couponCode` is user-controlled input, it can lead to potential security risks such as unauthorized data access, data manipulation, or even remote code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always validate, sanitize, and escape user inputs. Never trust user inputs blindly. Also, avoid using user inputs directly as object keys if possible. If it's necessary to use user inputs as keys, make sure to implement proper input validation and sanitization mechanisms to ensure the input is safe to use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability. Before using `couponCode` as a key, we validate and sanitize it.\n\n```javascript\n// Assuming validCouponCodes is a list of all valid coupon codes\nconst validCouponCodes = ['COUPON1', 'COUPON2', 'COUPON3'];\n\nif (validCouponCodes.includes(couponCode)) {\n  const campaign = campaigns[couponCode as keyof typeof campaigns];\n} else {\n  throw new Error('Invalid coupon code');\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not require any specific library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: Injection](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A1_2017-Injection)\n- [CWE-74: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Output Used by a Downstream Component ('Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/74.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-74",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/74.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-74"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fa1b38e7-dce6-3ce7-8e8b-2ba84d129334",
              "name": "Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a developer does not specify the fields to be returned in a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data. In the provided code, the `find` method is used without specifying the fields to be returned, which means all fields associated with the matching `email` will be returned. This could potentially include sensitive data such as passwords, credit card numbers, etc.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always specify the fields to be returned in a database query. This can be done by passing an object to the `find` method where the keys are the field names and the values are `1` or `true` to include the field in the returned documents.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nconst order = await ordersCollection.find({ email: updatedEmail }, { _id: 1, email: 1, orderNumber: 1 });\n```\n\nIn this example, only the `_id`, `email`, and `orderNumber` fields will be returned in the matching documents.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Node.js\n- MongoDB Node.js Driver\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP - Query Parameterization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Query_Parameterization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-209",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-209"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b5913b33-e4e1-314a-80cd-868e144ec597",
              "name": "Potential NoSQL Injection Vulnerability Detected in `../data/mongodb` Statement from `req` Argument",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"Potential NoSQL Injection Vulnerability Detected in `../data/mongodb` Statement from `req` Argument\" refers to a security flaw in a JavaScript application that uses MongoDB as its database. This vulnerability occurs when the application does not properly sanitize user input, allowing an attacker to manipulate the query to the MongoDB database. In the provided code snippet, the `updatedEmail` variable is directly used in a MongoDB `find` operation, which could potentially allow an attacker to inject malicious code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to validate and sanitize all user inputs. This can be done by using input validation libraries such as `validator.js` or `joi`. Additionally, using parameterized queries or prepared statements can also help to prevent NoSQL injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided code snippet using parameterized queries:\n\n```javascript\nconst query = { email: { $eq: updatedEmail } };\nconst order = await ordersCollection.find(query);\n```\n\nIn this code, `$eq` is a MongoDB operator that matches values that are equal to a specified value. This ensures that the `updatedEmail` value is used as a string and not as part of the query syntax.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `mongodb`: The MongoDB driver for Node.js.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [CWE-943: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Data Query Logic](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-943",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-943"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1d465b29-23c0-3523-bc5f-23d268d8370a",
              "name": "Unrestricted Database Query Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnrestricted Database Query Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a developer does not properly control or limit what data can be queried from a database. This can lead to unauthorized access to sensitive data, data leakage, or even data loss. In the provided code snippet, the `find()` method is used without any parameters, which means it will return all documents in the `ordersCollection` collection. If this collection contains sensitive data, it could be exposed to unauthorized users.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always use query parameters to limit the data returned by the database. This can be done by passing an object to the `find()` method that specifies the conditions that the documents need to meet to be returned. \n\nAdditionally, developers should implement proper access controls to ensure that only authorized users can perform certain database operations. This can be done using middleware functions that verify the user's role before allowing them to perform a database operation.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\n// Only return orders that are not marked as sensitive\nconst query = { sensitive: false };\nconst order = await ordersCollection.find(query);\n```\n\nIn this example, the `find()` method will only return documents where the `sensitive` field is `false`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- MongoDB Node.js Driver: This is used to interact with MongoDB databases from Node.js applications.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten 2017: A6 - Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [OWASP Node.js Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-209",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-209"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "37293b0b-2278-37e9-9783-85046435922c",
              "name": "Potential NoSQL Injection from Unsanitized `req` Argument in `../data/mongodb` Statement",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"Potential NoSQL Injection from Unsanitized `req` Argument in `../data/mongodb` Statement\" refers to a security flaw in a JavaScript application that uses MongoDB as its database. This vulnerability occurs when the application does not properly sanitize the `req` (request) object before using it in a MongoDB query. An attacker can exploit this vulnerability by sending a maliciously crafted request that modifies the query, leading to unauthorized data access, data manipulation, or even data loss.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `req.params.id` is directly used in a MongoDB `update` operation without any sanitization or validation. This makes the application vulnerable to NoSQL injection attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always sanitize and validate user inputs before using them in a database query. This can be done using input validation libraries or by using parameterized queries or prepared statements.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nconst ObjectId = require('mongodb').ObjectId;\n\nlet id;\ntry {\n  id = new ObjectId(req.params.id);\n} catch (err) {\n  return res.status(400).send('Invalid ID format');\n}\n\nawait ordersCollection.update({ _id: id }, { $set: { delivered: deliveryStatus, eta } });\n```\n\nIn this fix, we are using MongoDB's `ObjectId` function to validate and sanitize the `id` parameter. If the `id` is not a valid MongoDB ObjectId, an error will be thrown, and the function will return a response with a status code of 400 (Bad Request).\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `mongodb`: A MongoDB driver for Node.js.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP Node.js Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#mongodb-injection-prevention)\n- [CWE-943: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Data Query Logic](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-943",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-943"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a5f220ea-3f7f-3423-a349-cbd25c4e1b81",
              "name": "Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a type of security vulnerability that occurs when a developer does not explicitly specify the fields to be returned from a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data. In the provided code, all fields of the `CardModel` associated with the `UserId` are returned, which might include sensitive information.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always explicitly specify the fields to be returned from a database query. This can be done using the `attributes` option in the `findAll` method. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nconst cards = await CardModel.findAll({ \n    where: { UserId: req.body.UserId },\n    attributes: ['field1', 'field2', 'field3'] // specify the fields to be returned\n})\n```\n\nReplace `'field1', 'field2', 'field3'` with the actual field names that you want to return.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- `express`: Fast, unopinionated, minimalist web framework for Node.js\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-209",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
