              "id": "936fffad-7cc0-34b0-99bd-333eeffe189d",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but this rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, if the `UserModel.findByPk()` function rejects the promise, the error is caught and passed to the `next()` function. However, if the `user.update()` function rejects the promise, this rejection is not handled and would result in an Unhandled Promise Rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle promise rejections. This can be done by adding a `.catch()` block after every promise, or by using `try/catch` blocks in `async/await` functions. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ntry {\n  const user = await UserModel.findByPk(loggedInUser.data.id);\n  if (user) {\n    await user.update({ profileImage: `/assets/public/images/uploads/${loggedInUser.data.id}.${ext}` });\n  }\n} catch (error) {\n  next(error);\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, both the `UserModel.findByPk()` and `user.update()` functions are inside a `try/catch` block, so if either of them rejects the promise, the rejection will be caught and handled.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to require the following libraries:\n\n- A promise-based ORM library for Node.js, such as Sequelize, for the `UserModel.findByPk()` and `user.update()` functions.\n- An Express.js or similar middleware-based web framework, for the `next()` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a9ddd206-8cb5-3c06-8c27-d6ea7f540223",
              "name": "Unrestricted Database Query Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnrestricted Database Query Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when user-supplied input is used directly in a database query without proper sanitization or validation. This can lead to serious security issues such as SQL Injection, where an attacker can manipulate the query to access, modify, or delete data in the database.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `id` parameter from the request is directly parsed and used in the `findAll` query. This can potentially allow an attacker to manipulate the `id` parameter to alter the query.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize user-supplied input before using it in a database query.\n2. Use parameterized queries or prepared statements to ensure that user input is always treated as literal data, not part of the SQL command.\n3. Limit the privileges of the database account used by the application. It should only have the minimum required permissions.\n4. Implement proper error handling so that detailed system or database error messages are not revealed to the user, which could provide useful information to an attacker.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nlet id = parseInt(req.params.id);\nif (isNaN(id)) {\n  // Handle error: id is not a number\n} else {\n  RecycleModel.findAll({\n    where: {\n      id: id\n    }\n  }).then((Recycle) => {\n    // ...\n  });\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `id` parameter is parsed into an integer before being used in the query. If the parsing fails (i.e., `id` is not a number), an error is handled.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet appears to use the following libraries:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js, used to handle HTTP requests and responses.\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3c9eaec8-4727-33a0-805f-3ec985df5371",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, crashes, and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the promise returned by `RecycleModel.findAll()` is not properly handled in case of rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always handle promise rejections by providing a `.catch()` block or by using `try/catch` with async/await. This allows you to control the application flow even when errors occur and prevent potential crashes or unexpected behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nRecycleModel.findAll({\n    where: {\n      id: JSON.parse(req.params.id)\n    }\n  }).then((Recycle) => {\n    return res.send(utils.queryResultToJson(Recycle))\n  }).catch((error: unknown) => {\n    console.error(error);\n    res.status(500).send({ error: 'An error occurred while processing your request.' });\n  });\n```\n\nIn the above code, the promise rejection is handled by logging the error and sending a response with a status code of 500 (Internal Server Error) and a descriptive error message.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using the following libraries:\n\n- Sequelize (for the `findAll` method)\n- Express (for handling HTTP requests and responses)\n- A utility library (for the `queryResultToJson` method)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_security_cheat_sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
