            {
              "id": "db242ea7-a131-3236-8869-3911f9e37671",
              "name": "Direct Writing to Response Object Detected, Potentially Exposing App to XSS Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Direct Writing to Response Object Detected, Potentially Exposing App to XSS Vulnerability\" is a security vulnerability that occurs when user input is directly written to the HTTP response object without proper sanitization or encoding. This can potentially expose the application to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks, where an attacker can inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the function `utils.queryResultToJson(Recycle)` is directly written to the HTTP response object using `res.send()`. If the `Recycle` object contains any user-supplied data, this could potentially lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always sanitize and/or encode user-supplied data before writing it to the HTTP response object. This can be done using various methods, such as HTML encoding, JavaScript encoding, or using a Content Security Policy (CSP).\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet using the `escape-html` library to encode the user-supplied data:\n\n```javascript\nconst escapeHtml = require('escape-html');\n\nreturn res.send(escapeHtml(utils.queryResultToJson(Recycle)));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `express`: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- `escape-html`: A library for escaping HTML entities.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Node.js Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9915088e-deca-3125-9e11-2d379b50e33b",
              "name": "Direct Writing to Response Object May Lead to XSS Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is directly writing user input to the response object. If the user input is not properly sanitized, this can lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Use a safe, context-specific output encoding library when inserting untrusted data into HTML output.\n2. Validate and sanitize user inputs to ensure they do not contain malicious scripts.\n3. Use Content Security Policy (CSP) to reduce the severity of any XSS vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nconst sanitizeHtml = require('sanitize-html');\n\nreturn res.send(sanitizeHtml(utils.queryResultToJson(errMsg)));\n```\n\nIn this fix, we use the `sanitize-html` library to sanitize the user input before sending it to the response object.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js (for the `res.send()` function)\n- A utility library that provides the `queryResultToJson()` function\n- sanitize-html (for sanitizing user input)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://owasp.org/www-community/xss-filter-evasion-cheatsheet)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b97a4123-4625-348c-b764-6da1b1d667d7",
              "name": "\"Open Redirect Vulnerability in URL Building Application\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nAn Open Redirect Vulnerability in URL Building Application refers to a security flaw in a web application that allows an attacker to redirect a user to an arbitrary domain of the attacker's choosing. This is often used in phishing attacks where the attacker tricks the user into clicking a link that appears to be from a trusted source, but actually redirects to a malicious site.\n\nIn JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when the application uses user input to construct a URL for redirection without properly validating or sanitizing the input. For example, the following code is vulnerable to open redirect attacks:\n\n```javascript\nres.redirect(toUrl);\n```\n\nIn this case, if `toUrl` is derived from user input and is not properly validated, an attacker could craft a URL that redirects the user to a malicious site.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize user input before using it to construct a URL for redirection. You should also use a allow-list of trusted URLs for redirection, and reject any redirection requests to URLs not in the allow-list.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how you can validate a URL before redirecting:\n\n```javascript\nvar url = require('url');\nvar allowedHosts = ['www.example.com', 'example.com'];\n\nres.redirect = function(toUrl) {\n    var host = url.parse(toUrl).host;\n    if (allowedHosts.indexOf(host) !== -1) {\n        this.location(toUrl);\n    } else {\n        throw new Error('Invalid redirect URL');\n    }\n};\n```\n\nIn this code, we first parse the `toUrl` using the `url` module to get the host of the URL. We then check if the host is in our allow-list of trusted hosts. If it is, we proceed with the redirection. If it's not, we throw an error.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code requires the `url` module, which is a built-in module in Node.js, so no additional dependencies are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Unvalidated_Redirects_and_Forwards_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-601",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-601"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "22e1b544-17e0-3a6e-bcd5-2ac469e5483c",
              "name": "NoSQL Injection Vulnerability in findOne() Function Due to Untrusted User Input",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet above is vulnerable to NoSQL Injection due to the use of untrusted user input in the `findOne()` function. This function is used to retrieve a single document from a MongoDB collection that matches a specified filter. If the filter is built using untrusted user input, an attacker can manipulate the query, potentially gaining unauthorized access to sensitive data or performing other malicious activities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to validate, sanitize, or escape user input before using it in a NoSQL query. Avoid using user input directly in queries whenever possible. If it is necessary to use user input in a query, use parameterized queries or prepared statements. These techniques can ensure that user input is treated as literal values, not part of the query syntax, thus preventing injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code fix involves using the `$eq` operator to ensure that the user input is treated as a literal value in the query:\n\n```javascript\nSecurityAnswerModel.findOne({\n  include: [{\n    model: UserModel,\n    where: { email: { $eq: email } }\n  }]\n})\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-943: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Data Query Logic](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-943",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-943"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d4020851-cd80-3561-9616-ac6b42492d76",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected but the rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the promise returned by `SecurityAnswerModel.findOne()` is properly handled with `.catch()`, but the promises returned by `user?.update()` and `UserModel.findByPk()` are not. If these promises are rejected, the rejection will not be caught and the application may crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that all promises have a `.catch()` block or are inside a `try/catch` block if using async/await. This will ensure that any errors are properly caught and handled, preventing unexpected application behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the fixed code:\n\n```javascript\nSecurityAnswerModel.findOne({\n  include: [{\n    model: UserModel,\n    where: { email }\n  }]\n}).then((data: SecurityAnswerModel | null) => {\n  if ((data != null) && security.hmac(answer) === data.answer) {\n    UserModel.findByPk(data.UserId).then((user: UserModel | null) => {\n      if (user) {\n        user.update({ password: newPassword }).then((user: UserModel) => {\n          verifySecurityAnswerChallenges(user, answer)\n          res.json({ user })\n        }).catch((error: unknown) => {\n          next(error)\n        })\n      }\n    }).catch((error: unknown) => {\n      next(error)\n    })\n  } else {\n    res.status(401).send(res.__('Wrong answer to security question.'))\n  }\n}).catch((error: unknown) => {\n  next(error)\n})\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- A promise-based ORM library, such as Sequelize.\n- An Express.js server or similar to provide the `res` and `next` functions.\n- A security library to provide the `security.hmac()` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f9514ac5-0e59-30b7-85d4-88f5de3e0687",
              "name": "Sensitive Data Exposure in API Response",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSensitive Data Exposure in API Response is a vulnerability that occurs when an application does not adequately protect sensitive information. In JavaScript, this can occur when an API endpoint returns a JSON object containing sensitive user data. This can include information such as passwords, credit card numbers, or personally identifiable information (PII) like social security numbers. If an attacker is able to intercept this data, they can use it for malicious purposes such as identity theft or fraud.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should ensure that sensitive data is not included in API responses unless absolutely necessary. If it is necessary, the data should be properly encrypted. You should also implement proper access controls to ensure that only authorized users can access sensitive data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code example, the `res.json({ user })` line is potentially exposing sensitive user data. A better approach would be to only include the necessary user data in the response. For example:\n\n```javascript\nres.json({\n  id: user.id,\n  name: user.name,\n  email: user.email\n})\n```\n\nIn this example, only the user's id, name, and email are included in the response. Any sensitive data, such as passwords or credit card numbers, are not included.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example requires the `express` library to create the API endpoint and handle the response.\n\n```bash\nnpm install express\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 - A3:2017-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
