            {
              "id": "19089234-aaed-325b-9e73-a0d8a049d2b3",
              "name": "Sensitive Data Exposure in API Response",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSensitive Data Exposure in API Response is a vulnerability that occurs when an API unintentionally exposes sensitive information in its responses. This can happen when developers include sensitive data in the response objects without proper consideration of the security implications. In JavaScript, this can occur when using the `res.json()` function to send responses to the client.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, there doesn't seem to be any sensitive data exposure as it only sends the number of solved challenges. However, if the `idsFindIt` array contained sensitive data and was sent in the response, it would be a case of sensitive data exposure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should ensure that they do not include sensitive data in the API responses. This can be achieved by carefully designing the API to only return the necessary data. Additionally, sensitive data should be properly encrypted when stored and only decrypted when necessary.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf `idsFindIt` array contained sensitive data, a fix would be to not include it in the response. Instead, only send the necessary data:\n\n```javascript\nres.json({ data: idsFindIt.length + ' solved challenges have been restored.' })\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using Express.js, a popular web application framework for Node.js. Therefore, the `express` package is required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP API Security: Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-api-security/)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
