            {
              "id": "2f7b0c2a-ee1e-388a-8f81-208d9b92034e",
              "name": "Sensitive Data Exposure in API Response",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSensitive Data Exposure in API Response is a vulnerability that occurs when an API unintentionally exposes sensitive information in its responses. This can happen when developers include sensitive data in the response objects without proper consideration of the security implications. In JavaScript, this can occur when using the `res.json()` function to send responses to the client.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, there doesn't seem to be any sensitive data exposure as it only sends the number of solved challenges that have been restored. However, if the `idsFixIt` array contained sensitive data and was sent in the response, it would be a case of sensitive data exposure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should:\n\n- Always validate and sanitize input data.\n- Limit the data that is sent in responses, only send what is necessary.\n- Use encryption for sensitive data.\n- Implement proper access controls to ensure that only authorized users can access certain data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, there doesn't seem to be any sensitive data exposure. However, if the `idsFixIt` array contained sensitive data, you should not include it in the response. Instead, you could return a success message or a boolean value indicating whether the operation was successful or not.\n\n```javascript\nres.json({ data: 'Challenges have been restored successfully.' })\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using the Express.js library, which provides the `res.json()` function. Therefore, the Express.js library is required for the code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP API Security: Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-api-security/)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
