            {
              "id": "64b7012f-67ac-3f02-9441-310e2565d8e7",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the promise returned by `user?.update({ lastLoginIp: lastLoginIp?.toString() })` is properly handled with a `.catch()` block. However, the promise returned by `UserModel.findByPk(loggedInUser.data.id)` is not properly handled. If this promise is rejected, the error will not be caught and handled, leading to an Unhandled Promise Rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that all promises have a `.catch()` block or are inside a `try/catch` block if using async/await. This will ensure that any errors that occur during the execution of the promise are caught and can be handled appropriately.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nUserModel.findByPk(loggedInUser.data.id)\n  .then((user: UserModel | null) => {\n    return user?.update({ lastLoginIp: lastLoginIp?.toString() });\n  })\n  .then((user: UserModel) => {\n    res.json(user);\n  })\n  .catch((error: Error) => {\n    next(error);\n  });\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example appears to be using the following libraries:\n\n- Sequelize (for the `UserModel.findByPk()` and `user.update()` methods)\n- Express.js (for the `res.json()` and `next()` methods)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3a51cfec-21d9-3f02-8c11-a1fc90cdb66f",
              "name": "\"Exposure of Error Details Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nExposure of Error Details Vulnerability in JavaScript refers to the situation where an application provides too much information in error messages. This can potentially expose sensitive information or system details that could be used by an attacker to exploit the system. In the given code snippet, `res.json(user)`, if the `user` object contains any error details, they would be sent back in the response, potentially exposing sensitive information.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should ensure that error messages returned to the client are generic and do not contain any sensitive information or system details. You should also consider implementing a centralized error handling mechanism that can sanitize error messages before they are sent to the client.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of directly sending the `user` object in the response, you should create a new object that only contains the necessary information. Here is an example:\n\n```javascript\nres.json({\n  id: user.id,\n  name: user.name,\n  email: user.email\n});\n```\n\nIn this example, only the `id`, `name`, and `email` properties of the `user` object are sent in the response. Any error details contained in the `user` object would not be exposed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example appears to be using Express.js, a web application framework for Node.js. Therefore, the following library would be required:\n\n- express\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 - A6:2017-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
