            {
              "id": "57cf33ba-378c-379e-a91c-cc495fb63177",
              "name": "SQL Injection Risk from Untrusted Input and Raw SQL Query Concatenation",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to SQL Injection attacks. This is due to the use of raw SQL query concatenation with untrusted input. The `criteria` variable is directly taken from the request query parameter `q` and is used in the SQL query without any sanitization or parameterization. This allows an attacker to manipulate the SQL query by providing specially crafted input, leading to unauthorized access to data, data manipulation or even data loss.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using raw SQL queries whenever possible. Instead, use a database abstraction layer, Object-Relational Mapping (ORM) library, or a database API that supports parameterized queries. These methods ensure that user input is always treated as literal values, not part of the SQL command.\n\nIf raw SQL queries are unavoidable, always sanitize and validate user input. This can be done using a whitelist of acceptable input, or by using a library that can correctly escape potentially dangerous characters.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe Sequelize ORM used in the code supports parameterized queries. Here is how you can modify the vulnerable line to use this feature:\n\n```javascript\nmodels.sequelize.query(`SELECT * FROM Products WHERE ((name LIKE :search OR description LIKE :search) AND deletedAt IS NULL) ORDER BY name`, \n{ replacements: { search: `%${criteria}%` }, type: models.sequelize.QueryTypes.SELECT })\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js, for building web applications and APIs.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7ab6e637-265d-334b-9cd3-c78c2695a07a",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected behavior, crashes, and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the vulnerability lies in the fact that the Promise returned by the `models.sequelize.query` function is not properly handled in case of a rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle Promise rejections. This can be done by attaching a `.catch()` block to the Promise chain, which will be invoked when a Promise is rejected. The `.catch()` block should contain appropriate error handling logic.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nmodels.sequelize.query(`SELECT * FROM Products WHERE ((name LIKE '%${criteria}%' OR description LIKE '%${criteria}%') AND deletedAt IS NULL) ORDER BY name`)\n  .then(([products]: any) => {\n    // ... existing code ...\n  })\n  .catch((error: ErrorWithParent) => {\n    // Add appropriate error handling logic here\n    console.error('An error occurred:', error);\n  });\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- Express: A fast, unopinionated, minimalist web framework for Node.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "844410fd-334d-3b48-a0a0-e1a42215b686",
              "name": "Preventing SQL Injection Vulnerability through Prepared Statements, Parameterized Queries, and ORM Frameworks",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe provided code snippet is vulnerable to SQL Injection, a common security vulnerability that occurs when an attacker can insert malicious SQL statements into an SQL query. This can lead to unauthorized access to sensitive data, data corruption, or even data loss. In JavaScript, SQL Injection can occur when using template literals or string concatenation to build SQL queries, as shown in the provided code snippet.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate SQL Injection vulnerabilities, it is recommended to use Prepared Statements, Parameterized Queries, or Object-Relational Mapping (ORM) Frameworks. These techniques ensure that user input is always treated as literal data, not part of the SQL command.\n\n- **Prepared Statements**: These are SQL statements that are sent to and parsed by the database server separately from any parameters. This way, it is impossible for an attacker to inject malicious SQL.\n\n- **Parameterized Queries**: These are similar to prepared statements, but they are more flexible. They allow you to define all the SQL code and then pass in each parameter to the query later.\n\n- **ORM Frameworks**: These are programming techniques for converting data between incompatible type systems using object-oriented programming languages. They create a \"virtual object database\" that can be used from within the programming language.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code snippet can be fixed by using Sequelize's built-in support for parameterized queries:\n\n```javascript\nmodels.sequelize.query(`SELECT * FROM Products WHERE ((name LIKE :search OR description LIKE :search) AND deletedAt IS NULL) ORDER BY name`, \n{ replacements: { search: `%${criteria}%` }, type: models.sequelize.QueryTypes.SELECT })\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "627ec23a-4d83-361f-a68f-c24785cbd287",
              "name": "Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that occurs when a developer does not explicitly specify the fields to be returned from a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data. For instance, if a user model has fields like password, credit card number, etc., and the developer uses a query like `UserModel.findAll()`, it will return all fields including the sensitive ones.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always specify the fields they want to return in the query. This is known as \"whitelisting\" fields. This way, even if there are sensitive fields in the model, they will not be returned unless explicitly specified.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to fix this vulnerability:\n\n```javascript\nUserModel.findAll({\n  attributes: ['id', 'name', 'email'] // specify the fields to return\n}).then(data => {\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\nIn this example, only the 'id', 'name', and 'email' fields will be returned.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-209",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-209"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5da62e3b-be78-35b7-9772-7737b03b1f4d",
              "name": "Sensitive Data Exposure in API Response",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSensitive Data Exposure in API Response is a vulnerability that occurs when an application unintentionally exposes sensitive data in its responses. This can happen when an application does not properly validate or sanitize the data it sends in its responses. In JavaScript, this can occur when using the `res.json()` function to send JSON responses without properly sanitizing the data.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `utils.queryResultToJson(products)` function is used to convert the `products` query result into a JSON object, which is then sent as a response. If the `products` query result contains sensitive data, this data will be exposed in the API response.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n- Always validate and sanitize data before sending it in a response.\n- Implement proper access controls to ensure that only authorized users can access sensitive data.\n- Use encryption to protect sensitive data in transit.\n- Regularly review and update your application's security settings and practices.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout knowing the exact structure and content of the `products` query result, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, a general recommendation would be to sanitize the `products` query result before converting it into a JSON object. Here's an example of how you might do this:\n\n```javascript\nconst sanitizedProducts = products.map(product => {\n  return {\n    id: product.id,\n    name: product.name,\n    // Include only the properties that should be exposed in the API response\n  };\n});\n\nres.json(utils.queryResultToJson(sanitizedProducts));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet appears to use the following libraries:\n\n- Express.js: This is a web application framework for Node.js, used here to send JSON responses (`res.json()`).\n- A custom `utils` library, which contains the `queryResultToJson()` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "813b6ed0-3438-316e-bc9b-5f7ab92f6ed4",
              "name": "NoSQL Injection Vulnerability in findOne() Function Due to Untrusted User Input",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet above is vulnerable to NoSQL injection due to the use of untrusted user input in the `findOne()` function. This function is used to retrieve a single document from a MongoDB collection that matches a specified filter. If the filter is constructed using untrusted user input, an attacker can manipulate the query, potentially gaining unauthorized access to sensitive data or performing other malicious actions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to validate, sanitize, or escape user inputs before using them in a query. This can prevent an attacker from injecting malicious code into the query. Additionally, using parameterized queries or prepared statements can also help to prevent NoSQL injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nconst email = query.email\nSecurityAnswerModel.findOne({\n  include: [{\n    model: UserModel,\n    where: { email: Sequelize.escape(email?.toString()) }\n  }]\n}).then((answer: SecurityAnswerModel | null) => {\n  if (answer != null) {\n    SecurityQuestionModel.findByPk(answer.SecurityQuestionId).then((question: SecurityQuestionModel | null) => {\n      res.json({ question })\n    }).catch((error: Error) => {\n      next(error)\n    })\n  } else {\n    res.json({})\n  }\n}).catch((error: unknown) => {\n  next(error)\n})\n```\n\nIn this fixed version, the `Sequelize.escape()` function is used to escape the user input before it is used in the query.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-943: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Data Query Logic](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-943",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-943"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "370b1447-e339-314b-becd-b1f1fd51e3a1",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected, but this rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the promise returned by `SecurityQuestionModel.findByPk(answer.SecurityQuestionId)` is not handled properly. If this promise is rejected, the error will not be caught and the application may crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle promise rejections. This can be done by adding a `.catch()` block to the promise chain, or by using `async/await` with a `try/catch` block.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nSecurityAnswerModel.findOne({\n  include: [{\n    model: UserModel,\n    where: { email: email?.toString() }\n  }]\n}).then((answer: SecurityAnswerModel | null) => {\n  if (answer != null) {\n    SecurityQuestionModel.findByPk(answer.SecurityQuestionId).then((question: SecurityQuestionModel | null) => {\n      res.json({ question })\n    }).catch((error: Error) => {\n      next(error)\n    })\n  } else {\n    res.json({})\n  }\n}).catch((error: Error) => {\n  next(error)\n})\n```\n\nIn this version, the promise returned by `SecurityQuestionModel.findByPk(answer.SecurityQuestionId)` is properly handled. If the promise is rejected, the error will be passed to the next middleware function, preventing the application from crashing.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
