            {
              "id": "3832539a-5530-3a6d-878b-e5f338fbd06a",
              "name": "Sensitive Data Exposure in API Response",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSensitive Data Exposure in API Response is a vulnerability that occurs when an application unintentionally exposes sensitive data in its responses. This can happen when an application does not properly validate or sanitize the data it sends in its responses. In JavaScript, this can occur when using the `res.json()` function to send JSON responses without properly checking the data being sent.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n- Always validate and sanitize data before sending it in a response.\n- Use access controls to ensure that only authorized users can access sensitive data.\n- Encrypt sensitive data to add an extra layer of protection.\n- Avoid sending sensitive data in responses whenever possible.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code example, the `res.json({ question })` function is sending a JSON response containing the `question` object. If the `question` object contains sensitive data, this could lead to a Sensitive Data Exposure vulnerability.\n\nA potential fix could be to create a new object that only contains the non-sensitive data from the `question` object, and send that in the response instead. Here's an example:\n\n```javascript\nlet safeQuestion = {\n  id: question.id,\n  text: question.text,\n  // other non-sensitive properties...\n};\n\nres.json({ safeQuestion });\n```\n\nIn this example, `safeQuestion` only includes the non-sensitive data from the `question` object. This ensures that no sensitive data is exposed in the response.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js, used to create the API.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP: Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not always be accessible."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3c7761d2-a7ec-3e3d-be2a-29c2a767eb83",
              "name": "Sensitive Data Exposure in API Response",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSensitive Data Exposure in API Response is a security vulnerability that occurs when an application unintentionally exposes sensitive information in its responses. This can happen when an API endpoint returns more data than it should, including sensitive data such as passwords, credit card numbers, or personal user information. This vulnerability can lead to serious data breaches if exploited by malicious actors.\n\nIn JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when using the `res.json()` function in Express.js to send JSON responses without properly filtering or sanitizing the data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n- Always validate, sanitize, and filter the data before sending it in the response.\n- Implement proper access controls to ensure that only authorized users can access sensitive data.\n- Use HTTPS to encrypt the data in transit and prevent it from being intercepted by attackers.\n- Regularly review and update your API endpoints to ensure that they are not exposing sensitive data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf you are using Express.js and the `res.json()` function, you should ensure that you are only including the necessary data in the response. For example, if you are returning a user object, you should exclude sensitive fields like the password:\n\n```javascript\napp.get('/api/users/:id', function(req, res) {\n  User.findById(req.params.id, function(err, user) {\n    if (err) return res.sendStatus(500);\n    if (!user) return res.sendStatus(404);\n\n    // Exclude sensitive fields\n    res.json({\n      id: user.id,\n      name: user.name,\n      email: user.email\n    });\n  });\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- Mongoose: An Object Data Modeling (ODM) library for MongoDB and Node.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP API Security Top 10 2019](https://owasp.org/www-project-api-security/)\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
