            {
              "id": "627efe94-ae5e-39ae-90d6-fe09e69444af",
              "name": "\"Exposure of Error Details Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nExposure of Error Details Vulnerability in JavaScript refers to the situation where an application provides too much information in error messages. This can potentially expose sensitive information or system details that could be used by an attacker to exploit the system. In the given code snippet, `res.json(result)` could potentially expose sensitive information if `result` contains error details.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to handle errors properly and avoid exposing sensitive information in error messages. This can be achieved by implementing a global error handler that catches all errors and returns a generic error message to the client. Additionally, logging the error details on the server side can be useful for debugging purposes without exposing sensitive information to the client.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how you can implement a global error handler in Express.js:\n\n```javascript\napp.use(function(err, req, res, next) {\n  console.error(err.stack); // log error stack trace on server side\n  res.status(500).json({ error: 'An error occurred' }); // send generic error message to client\n});\n```\n\nIn this example, `app.use` is used to add a middleware function that handles all errors. The `err` parameter is an object that contains error details. The `console.error(err.stack)` line logs the error stack trace on the server side, and the `res.status(500).json({ error: 'An error occurred' })` line sends a generic error message to the client.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 - A6:2017-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-209",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-209"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3325f785-142f-3a92-81ea-dccf6dc2b743",
              "name": "Sensitive Data Exposure in API Response",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSensitive Data Exposure in API Response is a vulnerability that occurs when an API unintentionally exposes sensitive information in its responses. This can happen when developers include sensitive data in the response objects without proper consideration of the security implications. In JavaScript, this can occur when using the `res.json()` function to send JSON responses in an Express.js application.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize input data.\n2. Implement proper access controls to ensure that only authorized users can access sensitive data.\n3. Avoid sending sensitive data in API responses unless absolutely necessary.\n4. Encrypt sensitive data when it is necessary to include it in an API response.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf `result` contains sensitive data, you should remove or anonymize this data before sending the response. Here's an example of how you can do this:\n\n```javascript\n// Assuming result is an object with a property 'password'\nlet safeResult = {...result};\ndelete safeResult.password;\nres.json(safeResult);\n```\n\nIn this example, we're creating a copy of `result` and deleting the `password` property before sending the response.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- express\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP API Security: Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-api-security/)\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
