            {
              "id": "7ade6257-a109-30ca-9a94-595cfdfa076f",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected, but this rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information or lead to other security vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the provided code, the `osaft.reload()` function returns a Promise, but there is no `.catch()` block to handle any errors that might occur during the execution of the Promise.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always include a `.catch()` block when working with Promises to handle any potential errors. This allows you to control the application flow even when an error occurs, and prevents the application from crashing unexpectedly.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nvoid osaft.reload().then(() => {\n    for (const product of config.get<ProductConfig[]>('products')) {\n      if (product.urlForProductTamperingChallenge !== undefined) {\n        urlForProductTamperingChallenge = product.urlForProductTamperingChallenge\n        break\n      }\n    }\n    if (urlForProductTamperingChallenge) {\n      if (!utils.contains(osaft.description, `${urlForProductTamperingChallenge}`)) {\n        if (utils.contains(osaft.description, `<a href=\"${config.get<string>('challenges.overwriteUrlForProductTamperingChallenge')}\" target=\"_blank\">`)) {\n          challengeUtils.solve(challenges.changeProductChallenge)\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }).catch((error) => {\n    console.error('An error occurred:', error);\n  });\n```\n\nIn this fix, a `.catch()` block is added to handle any errors that might occur during the execution of the Promise.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to require the following libraries:\n\n- A library that provides the `osaft` object and its `reload` method. The specific library is not clear from the provided code.\n- A configuration library, such as `config`, to get the 'products' and 'challenges.overwriteUrlForProductTamperingChallenge' configurations.\n- A utility library, such as `utils`, to provide the `contains` method.\n- A challenge utility library, such as `challengeUtils`, to provide the `solve` method.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
