            {
              "id": "4ef8ffb7-39b8-3e09-bc57-b0a095cfe85f",
              "name": "\"User-Controlled Data Vulnerability in Application Business Logic Decision Making\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"User-Controlled Data Vulnerability in Application Business Logic Decision Making\" is a type of vulnerability that occurs when an application's business logic decisions are influenced by user-controlled data. In the context of JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when user input is not properly validated or sanitized before being used in business logic operations.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the vulnerability arises from the fact that the application's decision to solve a password repeat challenge is based on whether the `passwordRepeat` field in the request body is not equal to the `password` field. An attacker could potentially manipulate these fields to bypass the challenge.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize user input before using it in business logic operations. This includes checking for expected data types, lengths, formats, and ranges.\n\n2. Implement server-side controls to enforce business rules and constraints, and do not rely solely on client-side controls.\n\n3. Use parameterized queries or prepared statements to prevent SQL injection attacks.\n\n4. Use secure coding practices and follow the principle of least privilege.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA potential fix for the provided code snippet could be to add server-side validation to ensure that the `passwordRepeat` field is indeed a repeat of the `password` field:\n\n```javascript\nif (req.body && typeof req.body.password === 'string' && typeof req.body.passwordRepeat === 'string') {\n    if (req.body.password === req.body.passwordRepeat) {\n        challengeUtils.solveIf(challenges.passwordRepeatChallenge, () => { return true; })\n    } else {\n        challengeUtils.solveIf(challenges.passwordRepeatChallenge, () => { return false; })\n    }\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid input\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not specify any library dependencies. However, it is likely that it requires the `express` library for handling HTTP requests and responses, and a custom `challengeUtils` library for solving challenges.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-807: Reliance on Untrusted Inputs in a Security Decision](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/807.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-807",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/807.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-807"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "074ab662-b168-3bf4-bd01-5c02ad1f5cad",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected, but this rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information or lead to other security vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the provided code, the Promise returned by `FeedbackModel.findAndCountAll()` is rejected, but there is no error handling logic in the `catch()` block. This means that if an error occurs during the execution of the Promise, it will not be handled and the application may crash or behave unexpectedly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle Promise rejections. This can be done by providing a function to the `catch()` method of the Promise that will handle any errors that occur. This function should take an error object as its argument and should contain logic to handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nFeedbackModel.findAndCountAll({ where: { rating: 5 } })\n  .then(({ count }: { count: number }) => {\n    if (count === 0) {\n      challengeUtils.solve(challenges.feedbackChallenge)\n    }\n  })\n  .catch((error) => {\n    console.error('An error occurred:', error);\n  });\n```\n\nIn this version of the code, the `catch()` method is provided with a function that takes an error object as its argument and logs the error to the console. This is a simple example of error handling; in a real application, you would likely want to do something more sophisticated, such as logging the error to an external service or showing an error message to the user.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to require the following libraries:\n\n- Sequelize (for the `findAndCountAll()` method)\n- A library that provides the `challengeUtils.solve()` function and the `challenges.feedbackChallenge` object. This could be a custom library specific to your application.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_security_cheat_sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "15dc4088-820a-3bac-ac70-4e5151da28d8",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" is a common vulnerability in JavaScript programming language, particularly in Node.js applications. It occurs when a promise is rejected but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes and potential security vulnerabilities, as the application may continue to execute with an inconsistent state.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always handle promise rejections by providing a `.catch()` block or by using `try/catch` with async/await. This ensures that any errors that occur during the execution of the promise are caught and can be handled appropriately, preventing the application from entering an inconsistent state.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, the promise rejection is not handled. This can be fixed by adding a `.catch()` block to handle any errors that may occur. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```javascript\nFeedbackModel.findAndCountAll({\n    where: {\n      comment: {\n        [Op.or]: knownVulnerableComponents()\n      }\n    }\n  }).then(({ count }: { count: number }) => {\n    if (count > 0) {\n      challengeUtils.solve(challenges.knownVulnerableComponentChallenge)\n    }\n  }).catch((error) => {\n    console.error('An error occurred:', error);\n  });\n```\n\nIn this fix, if an error occurs during the execution of the promise, it will be caught and logged to the console. Depending on the application, you may want to handle the error differently, such as by sending an error response to the user or by logging the error to an error tracking service.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for `findAndCountAll` and `Op.or`)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Node.js Top 10 - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "29219c47-e030-3532-ad0b-9862b829142a",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" is a common issue in JavaScript programming, particularly in Node.js applications. This vulnerability occurs when a Promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the provided code, the Promise returned by `FeedbackModel.findAndCountAll()` is not properly handled in case of rejection. If the Promise is rejected for any reason (for example, if the database is unavailable or if there is an error in the query), the application will crash or behave unpredictably.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle Promise rejections. This can be done by adding a `.catch()` block to the Promise chain, which will be called if the Promise is rejected. The `.catch()` block should handle the error appropriately, for example by logging the error and returning a default value.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nFeedbackModel.findAndCountAll({\n    where: {\n      comment: {\n        [Op.or]: weirdCryptos()\n      }\n    }\n  }).then(({ count }: { count: number }) => {\n    if (count > 0) {\n      challengeUtils.solve(challenges.weirdCryptoChallenge)\n    }\n  }).catch((error) => {\n    console.error('An error occurred:', error);\n  });\n```\n\nIn this version of the code, if the Promise is rejected, the error will be logged to the console and the application will not crash.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for the `findAndCountAll` function and `Op.or` operator)\n- A database driver compatible with Sequelize (such as `pg` for PostgreSQL or `mysql2` for MySQL)\n- The `challengeUtils` and `challenges` modules (these appear to be custom modules and are not part of any standard library)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Node.js Top 10: Unhandled Promise Rejections](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
