            {
              "id": "4e8443f8-40a8-3379-b2e2-453a0d308cff",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected, but this rejection is not caught or handled with a `.catch()` method. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the Promise returned by `ComplaintModel.findAndCountAll()` is not properly handled in case of rejection. If the Promise is rejected, the `.catch()` method is called with no arguments, meaning that the error is silently ignored.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always handle Promise rejections with a `.catch()` method. This method should take an error argument and handle it appropriately. This might involve logging the error, retrying the operation, or informing the user of the error.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nComplaintModel.findAndCountAll({\n    where: {\n      message: {\n        [Op.or]: knownVulnerableComponents()\n      }\n    }\n  }).then(({ count }: { count: number }) => {\n    if (count > 0) {\n      challengeUtils.solve(challenges.knownVulnerableComponentChallenge)\n    }\n  }).catch((error) => {\n    console.error('An error occurred:', error);\n  });\n```\n\nIn this version, the `.catch()` method takes an `error` argument and logs it to the console. This allows developers to see and diagnose the error.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- A database driver compatible with Sequelize, such as `pg` for Postgres or `mysql2` for MySQL.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_security_cheat_sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
