              "id": "ed4dbeec-2e37-3dd9-9918-11affbe07b8f",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected, but this rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information or lead to other security vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the provided code, the Promise returned by `FeedbackModel.findAndCountAll()` is rejected if an error occurs during the execution of the query. However, the `catch()` block is empty, meaning that the rejection is not handled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle Promise rejections. This can be done by providing a function to the `catch()` method of the Promise that can handle the error appropriately. This function should take an error object as its argument, and can then log the error, retry the operation, or handle the error in some other way.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nFeedbackModel.findAndCountAll({ where: { comment: { [Op.like]: '%epilogue-js%' } } }\n  ).then(({ count }: { count: number }) => {\n    if (count > 0) {\n      challengeUtils.solve(challenges.typosquattingNpmChallenge)\n    }\n  }).catch((error) => {\n    console.error('An error occurred:', error);\n  });\n```\n\nIn this version of the code, the `catch()` method is provided with a function that logs the error to the console. This is a simple way to handle the error, but in a real application, you would likely want to handle the error in a way that is more appropriate for your specific use case.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for the `findAndCountAll()` method and `Op.like` operator)\n- A library or module that provides the `challengeUtils.solve()` function and `challenges.typosquattingNpmChallenge` object.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
