              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" is a common issue in JavaScript programming, particularly in Node.js applications. This vulnerability occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the rejection. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including potential crashes or security vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the provided code, the promise returned by `FeedbackModel.findAndCountAll()` is not properly handled in case of rejection. If the promise is rejected for any reason (for example, if there is a problem with the database connection), the application may crash or behave unpredictably.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle promise rejections. This can be done by adding a `.catch()` block to your promises, which will be executed if the promise is rejected. In the `.catch()` block, you should handle the error appropriately, for example by logging the error and returning a response to the user.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nFeedbackModel.findAndCountAll({ where: { comment: { [Op.like]: '%ngy-cookie%' } } }\n).then(({ count }: { count: number }) => {\n  if (count > 0) {\n    challengeUtils.solve(challenges.typosquattingAngularChallenge)\n  }\n}).catch((error) => {\n  console.error('An error occurred:', error);\n  // Handle the error appropriately here\n});\n```\n\nIn this version of the code, if the promise is rejected, the error will be logged to the console and can be handled appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for the `findAndCountAll` function and `Op.like` operator)\n- A library or module that provides the `challengeUtils` and `challenges` objects\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
