                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the promise returned by `ComplaintModel.findAndCountAll()` is not properly handled in case of rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that promises have a `.catch()` method to handle any errors that may occur. This will prevent the application from crashing and provide a way to handle the error gracefully.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nComplaintModel.findAndCountAll({ where: { message: { [Op.like]: '%ngy-cookie%' } } })\n  .then(({ count }: { count: number }) => {\n    if (count > 0) {\n      challengeUtils.solve(challenges.typosquattingAngularChallenge)\n    }\n  }).catch((error) => {\n    console.error('An error occurred:', error);\n  });\n```\n\nIn this fix, a `.catch()` method is added to the promise to handle any errors that may occur. The error is logged to the console, but in a real application, you would likely want to handle the error in a way that is appropriate for your specific use case.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to require the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for the `findAndCountAll` method and `Op.like` operator)\n- A custom `challengeUtils` module (for the `solve` method)\n- A custom `challenges` module (for the `typosquattingAngularChallenge` constant)\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
