              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the promise returned by `ComplaintModel.findAndCountAll()` is not properly handled in case of rejection. If the promise is rejected, the `.catch()` block is empty and does not provide any error handling mechanism.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that promises have appropriate error handling mechanisms in place. This can be done by providing a `.catch()` block that handles the error appropriately. This could involve logging the error, retrying the operation, or providing a fallback operation.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nComplaintModel.findAndCountAll({ where: { message: { [Op.like]: '%pickle rick%' } } }\n  ).then(({ count }: { count: number }) => {\n    if (count > 0) {\n      challengeUtils.solve(challenges.hiddenImageChallenge)\n    }\n  }).catch((error) => {\n    console.error('An error occurred:', error);\n  });\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `.catch()` block logs the error to the console. Depending on the application, you may want to handle the error differently.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to require the following libraries:\n\n- Sequelize (for the `findAndCountAll` method and `Op.like` operator)\n- A library or module that provides `challengeUtils` and `challenges`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
