              "id": "a4813740-d3cd-3372-a8cf-b0e8b289e258",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" is a common issue in JavaScript programming, particularly in Node.js applications. This vulnerability occurs when a Promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the Promise returned by `FeedbackModel.findAndCountAll()` is not properly handled in case of rejection. If the Promise is rejected, the `.catch()` block is empty and does nothing to handle the error.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that Promises have appropriate error handling mechanisms in place. This can be done by providing a `.catch()` block that handles any errors that may occur during the execution of the Promise.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nFeedbackModel.findAndCountAll({ where: { comment: { [Op.or]: eslintScopeVulnIds() } } }\n  ).then(({ count }: { count: number }) => {\n    if (count > 0) {\n      challengeUtils.solve(challenges.supplyChainAttackChallenge)\n    }\n  }).catch((error) => {\n    console.error('An error occurred:', error);\n  });\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `.catch()` block logs the error to the console. Depending on the application, you may want to handle the error differently, such as by sending an error response to the client or logging the error to an external monitoring service.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for `findAndCountAll` and `Op.or`)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Node.js Top 10: Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
