              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected, but this rejection is not caught or handled with a `.catch()` method. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the vulnerability lies in the `.catch()` method, which is empty and does not handle the error.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle Promise rejections. This can be done by adding a `.catch()` method to your Promises that handles the error appropriately. This could involve logging the error, retrying the operation, or returning a default value.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nFeedbackModel.findAndCountAll({ where: { comment: { [Op.like]: '%' + config.get<string>('challenges.csafHashValue') + '%' } } }\n  ).then(({ count }: { count: number }) => {\n    if (count > 0) {\n      challengeUtils.solve(challenges.csafChallenge)\n    }\n  }).catch((error) => {\n    console.error('An error occurred:', error);\n  });\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `.catch()` method logs the error to the console. Depending on your application, you may want to handle the error differently.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for the `findAndCountAll` method and `Op.like` operator)\n- A configuration library (for the `config.get` method)\n- A custom `challengeUtils` library (for the `solve` method)\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP: Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active at the time of writing."
