              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, crashes, and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the Promise returned by `ComplaintModel.findAndCountAll()` is not properly handled in case of rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that Promises have a `.catch()` block to handle any errors that may occur during the execution of the Promise. This allows for proper error handling and prevents the application from crashing or behaving unexpectedly.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nComplaintModel.findAndCountAll({ where: { message: { [Op.like]: '%' + config.get<string>('challenges.csafHashValue') + '%' } } }\n  ).then(({ count }: { count: number }) => {\n    if (count > 0) {\n      challengeUtils.solve(challenges.csafChallenge)\n    }\n  }).catch((error) => {\n    console.error('An error occurred:', error);\n  });\n```\n\nIn the above code, a `.catch()` block has been added to handle any errors that may occur during the execution of the Promise.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for `findAndCountAll` and `Op.like`)\n- A configuration library (for `config.get`)\n- A custom utility library (for `challengeUtils.solve`)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
