              "id": "e775cb5a-6afe-3a2b-8016-a67d61139102",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejections Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejections Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the promise returned by `ComplaintModel.findAndCountAll()` is not properly handled in case of a rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always include a `.catch()` block when working with promises to handle any potential errors. This allows you to control the application flow even when an error occurs, preventing unexpected behavior and potential security issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nComplaintModel.findAndCountAll({ where: { message: { [Op.like]: '%6PPi37DBxP4lDwlriuaxP15HaDJpsUXY5TspVmie%' } } }\n  ).then(({ count }: { count: number }) => {\n    if (count > 0) {\n      challengeUtils.solve(challenges.leakedApiKeyChallenge)\n    }\n  }).catch((error) => {\n    console.error('An error occurred:', error);\n  });\n```\n\nIn this fix, a `.catch()` block is added to handle any errors that may occur when the promise is rejected. The error is logged to the console, but in a real-world application, you would likely want to handle the error in a way that is appropriate for your specific use case.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to require the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for the `findAndCountAll()` function and `Op.like` operator)\n- A custom `challengeUtils` module (for the `solve()` function)\n- A custom `challenges` module (for the `leakedApiKeyChallenge` constant)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejections](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active at the time of writing."
