            {
              "id": "2572b827-62ae-36f2-b582-9e47c0d229ec",
              "name": "Direct Writing to Response Object May Lead to XSS Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) is a type of injection security vulnerability where an attacker can inject malicious scripts into webpages viewed by other users. In JavaScript, a common source of XSS vulnerabilities is the direct writing to the response object. This can occur when user input is directly included in the response without proper sanitization or encoding.\n\nIn the provided sink `res.send(compiledTemplate)`, if `compiledTemplate` includes user input that is not properly sanitized or encoded, it may lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always sanitize and/or encode user input before including it in the response. Sanitization involves removing or replacing any potentially harmful characters in the user input. Encoding involves converting potentially harmful characters into a safe format that the browser will not interpret as code.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix using the `escape-html` library to encode user input:\n\n```javascript\nvar escapeHtml = require('escape-html');\n\n// ...\n\nvar userInput = /* get user input */;\nvar sanitizedInput = escapeHtml(userInput);\nvar compiledTemplate = /* use sanitizedInput to create template */;\n\nres.send(compiledTemplate);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- `escape-html`: This library is used to encode user input to prevent XSS attacks.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0f169198-adcb-36ec-a576-1cb3b13439a0",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability due to Undefined 'subs' Used with '<script>' Tag\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks. This is because the variable `subs` is directly embedded into the script tag without any sanitization. If `subs` is controlled by an external source or user input, it can be manipulated to inject malicious scripts, leading to an XSS attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should ensure that the `subs` variable is not controlled by an external source or user input. If it is necessary to use external or user-controlled data, you should sanitize the data before using it. Sanitization can be done by encoding or escaping special characters that can be used to form a script.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA simple fix would be to sanitize the `subs` variable before using it. Here is an example of how you can do this:\n\n```javascript\nfunction sanitizeHTML(str) {\n    var temp = document.createElement('div');\n    temp.textContent = str;\n    return temp.innerHTML;\n}\n\nvar sanitizedSubs = sanitizeHTML(subs);\ncompiledTemplate = compiledTemplate.replace('<script id=\"subtitle\"></script>', '<script id=\"subtitle\" type=\"text/vtt\" data-label=\"English\" data-lang=\"en\">' + sanitizedSubs + '</script>')\n```\n\nIn this example, the `sanitizeHTML` function is used to sanitize the `subs` variable. This function creates a temporary DOM element and sets its `textContent` to the input string. This automatically encodes special characters in the string. The `innerHTML` of the temporary element is then returned, which is the sanitized version of the input string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not require any external library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
