            {
              "id": "6bf3c30e-f808-304b-84d4-93532ab7c4ed",
              "name": "XSS Vulnerability in User-Controlled HTML Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is vulnerable to XSS because it directly includes user input (`req.params.challenge`) in the response without proper sanitization or encoding. An attacker could potentially inject malicious scripts by manipulating the `challenge` parameter in the request.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, it is recommended to:\n\n- Use frameworks that automatically escape XSS by design, such as Ruby on Rails, React JS, etc.\n- Escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL).\n- Apply context-sensitive encoding when modifying the browser document on the client side.\n- Use Content Security Policy (CSP) as a defense-in-depth mitigating control against XSS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code can be fixed by sanitizing the user input before including it in the response. Here is an example using the `escape-html` library:\n\n```javascript\nconst escape = require('escape-html');\n\nres.status(404).json({ status: 'error', error: `No code challenge for challenge key: ${escape(req.params.challenge)}` })\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- escape-html: A library to escape string for use in HTML.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://owasp.org/www-community/xss-filter-evasion-cheatsheet)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c3f1255d-761d-3282-9c75-9c2ea5e315da",
              "name": "\"Bracket Notation Object Injection through accuracy.getFindItAttempts(key) - 1\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Bracket Notation Object Injection through accuracy.getFindItAttempts(key) - 1\" vulnerability in JavaScript refers to a situation where an attacker can manipulate the input to the `getFindItAttempts(key)` function, which is then used as a key to access properties of an object using bracket notation. This can lead to unauthorized access to sensitive data or even remote code execution if the object properties contain functions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to validate and sanitize all inputs to the `getFindItAttempts(key)` function. This can be done by checking if the input is of the expected type and within the expected range. If the input is not valid, the function should throw an error or return a default value.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a possible fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```javascript\nconst nextHint = codingChallengeInfos.hints[\n  typeof key === 'number' && key >= 0 && key < accuracy.getFindItAttempts.length \n    ? accuracy.getFindItAttempts(key) - 1 \n    : 0\n]\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first check if the key is a number and within the range of valid indices for the `getFindItAttempts` array. If it is, we use it to access the array. If it is not, we default to the first element of the array.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not appear to require any specific library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: Injection](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A1_2017-Injection)\n- [CWE-74: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Output Used by a Downstream Component ('Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/74.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-74",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/74.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-74"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6b476c3d-f23c-3c02-9bca-daf146a4ca8d",
              "name": "NoSQL Injection Vulnerability in findOne() Function Due to Untrusted User Input",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"NoSQL Injection Vulnerability in findOne() Function Due to Untrusted User Input\" is a security vulnerability that occurs when an application uses untrusted user input in a NoSQL query. In this case, the `findOne()` function is used to query the database with a condition that includes user input (`req.body.UserId`). If an attacker can control the `UserId` parameter, they can potentially manipulate the query to retrieve unauthorized data, modify data, or perform other malicious actions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate, sanitize, and/or escape user input before using it in a database query. This can help prevent an attacker from injecting malicious code into the query.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix using the `express-validator` library to validate the `UserId` parameter:\n\n```javascript\nconst { check, validationResult } = require('express-validator');\n\napp.post('/api/wallet', [\n  check('UserId').isInt(),\n  (req, res) => {\n    const errors = validationResult(req);\n    if (!errors.isEmpty()) {\n      return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() });\n    }\n\n    const wallet = await WalletModel.findOne({ where: { UserId: req.body.UserId } });\n    // ...\n  }\n]);\n```\n\nIn this code, `check('UserId').isInt()` validates that the `UserId` parameter is an integer. If the validation fails, an error response is returned.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `express`: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- `express-validator`: An Express middleware for input validation.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-943: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Data Query Logic](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-943",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-943"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9d9a3c74-a4ae-3405-9394-395df54eaaa9",
              "name": "NoSQL Injection Vulnerability in findOne() Function Due to Untrusted User Input",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to NoSQL injection due to the use of untrusted user input in the `findOne()` function. This function is used to retrieve a single document from a MongoDB collection that matches a specified filter. If the filter is built using untrusted user input, an attacker can manipulate the query, potentially gaining unauthorized access to sensitive data or performing other malicious actions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to validate, sanitize, or escape user inputs before using them in a database query. This can prevent an attacker from injecting malicious code into the query. Additionally, using parameterized queries or prepared statements can also help to prevent NoSQL injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nconst cardId = req.body.paymentId\nconst userId = req.body.UserId\n\nif (typeof cardId !== 'string' || typeof userId !== 'string') {\n    throw new Error('Invalid input')\n}\n\nconst sanitizedCardId = sanitize(cardId)\nconst sanitizedUserId = sanitize(userId)\n\nconst card = sanitizedCardId ? await CardModel.findOne({ where: { id: sanitizedCardId, UserId: sanitizedUserId } }) : null\n```\n\nIn this fix, the user inputs are first checked to ensure they are strings. Then, they are sanitized using a hypothetical `sanitize()` function before being used in the `findOne()` function. This function should be implemented to remove or escape any potentially harmful characters.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet appears to require the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js (for handling HTTP requests and responses)\n- Sequelize (for interacting with the database)\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-943: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Data Query Logic](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-943",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-943"
                ]
              }
