            {
              "id": "2c5cfa5e-3f01-3574-a477-82b9244217f2",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected, but this rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, if the `increment` operation fails for any reason, the error is caught but not handled, which can lead to this vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle Promise rejections. This can be done by adding error handling logic in the `catch` block. This logic can include logging the error, returning a response with an error status and message, or retrying the operation, depending on the specific requirements of your application.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nWalletModel.increment({ balance: req.body.balance }, { where: { UserId: req.body.UserId } })\n  .then(() => {\n    res.status(200).json({ status: 'success', data: req.body.balance })\n  })\n  .catch((error) => {\n    console.error(error);\n    res.status(500).json({ status: 'error', message: 'An error occurred while incrementing the balance.' });\n  });\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `catch` block has been updated to log the error and return a response with a status of 500 and an error message.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to use the following libraries:\n\n- Sequelize (for the `increment` operation)\n- Express (for handling requests and responses)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d28cd339-a360-3ae8-9117-d88aef28cd70",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected, but this rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always handle Promise rejections. This can be done by using `.catch()` at the end of your Promise chain to catch any errors that may occur. Additionally, you can listen for the `unhandledrejection` event at the process level and handle any unhandled rejections there.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's how you can fix the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ncheckDiffs(keys)\n  .then(data => {\n    console.log(('---------------------------------------'))\n    writeToFile(data)\n    console.log(`${colors.bold('All file diffs have been locked!')} Commit changed cache.json to git.`)\n  })\n  .catch(error => {\n    console.error('An error occurred:', error);\n  });\n```\n\nIn this updated code, a `.catch()` block has been added to handle any errors that may occur during the execution of the Promise.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not specify any library dependencies. However, it seems to use a `colors` library for console output formatting. Therefore, the `colors` library would be a required dependency.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c5a8c63d-9f84-36fc-a01d-551cf7b5fd69",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the promise returned by `seePatch(file)` is not handled, which could lead to an Unhandled Promise Rejection if `seePatch(file)` rejects.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always handle promise rejections. This can be done by adding a `.catch()` block to the promise chain, or by using `try/catch` with `async/await`. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, the `forEach` loop is used with an async function, which does not wait for promises to resolve or reject. Instead, use `Promise.all()` with `map()` to handle all promises and catch any rejections.\n\n```javascript\ncheckDiffs(keys)\n  .then(async data => {\n    console.log('---------------------------------------')\n    const fileData = getDataFromFile()\n    const filesWithDiff = checkData(data, fileData)\n    if (filesWithDiff.length === 0) {\n      console.log(`${colors.green(colors.bold('No new file diffs recognized since last lock!'))} No action required.`)\n    } else {\n      console.log(`${colors.red(colors.bold('New file diffs recognized since last lock!'))} Double-check and amend listed files and lock new state with ${colors.bold('npm run rsn:update')}`)\n      console.log(`Be aware that diffs for the ${filesWithDiff.length} affected files below contain ${colors.bold('all changes')} including locked & cached ones! Compare carefully!`)\n      console.log('---------------------------------------')\n      try {\n        await Promise.all(filesWithDiff.map(file => seePatch(file)))\n      } catch (error) {\n        console.error(error)\n      }\n      process.exitCode = 1\n    }\n  })\n  .catch(error => console.error(error))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- A promise-based function `checkDiffs()`\n- A function `getDataFromFile()`\n- A function `checkData()`\n- A function `seePatch()`\n- A library `colors` for console color formatting\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_security_cheat_sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
