            {
              "id": "61d47e5f-10bb-36e7-849c-033c428b6499",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" is a type of vulnerability in JavaScript that occurs when a promise rejection is not properly handled or caught. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, potential crashes, and even security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the promise returned by `retrieveCodeSnippet(val.split('_')[0])` is not caught, which means if it rejects, the rejection will not be handled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle promise rejections. This can be done by adding a `.catch()` block to the promise chain, or by using an `async/await` function with a `try/catch` block. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ntry {\n  await retrieveCodeSnippet(val.split('_')[0])\n    .then(snippet => {\n      // ... existing code ...\n    })\n    .catch(error => {\n      console.error('An error occurred:', error);\n    });\n} catch (error) {\n  console.error('An error occurred:', error);\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, a `.catch()` block is added to the promise chain to handle any rejections from `retrieveCodeSnippet(val.split('_')[0])`. Additionally, the entire `await` expression is wrapped in a `try/catch` block to handle any other errors that might occur.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `fs` (built-in Node.js module)\n- `colors` (third-party module)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_security_cheat_sheet.html#handle-errors-and-exceptions-properly)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a00a524e-2163-397b-b200-0f8b1750ab9e",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application reads a file from the file system based on a filename provided by the user. If the user can control the filename, they could potentially read any file on the system that the application has access to. This is a security vulnerability known as Path Traversal.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Use frameworks that automatically escape XSS by design, such as Ruby on Rails, React JS, etc.\n- Escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) will resolve Reflected and Stored XSS vulnerabilities.\n- Apply context-sensitive encoding when modifying the browser document on the client side acts against DOM XSS.\n\nFor the specific code snippet, you should validate the filename provided by the user to ensure it is safe. This could involve checking it against a list of allowed filenames, or sanitizing it to remove any potentially harmful characters.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix using a whitelist approach:\n\n```javascript\nconst allowedFiles = ['file1.txt', 'file2.txt', 'file3.txt'];\nif (allowedFiles.includes(file)) {\n    const fileData = fs.readFileSync(fixesPath + '/' + file).toString();\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `fs` module, which is a built-in module in Node.js and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "dc855120-ddd7-3bfd-bb34-f65b7d9f325b",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is logging a string to the console that includes user-controlled data (`file`). If this data is not properly sanitized, it could lead to a potential XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (HTML body, HTML attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) that the data lands. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn this case, you should sanitize the `file` variable before logging it to the console. Here's an example of how you can do this using the `escape-html` library:\n\n```javascript\nconst escapeHtml = require('escape-html');\n\nconsole.log(colors.bold(escapeHtml(file) + '\\n'));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `colors`: This library is used to add color and style to Node.js console.log statements.\n- `escape-html`: This library is used to escape a string for use in HTML.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://owasp.org/www-community/xss-filter-evasion-cheatsheet)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "language": "en-US",
